window.IRIS_STD = {"version":"v0.69.0","modules":[{"name":"core","title":"Collections","group":"Core","summary":"List, Map, Set, Deque, Heap, SortedMap and Arena, and sorting and searching slices, which every program has without an import.","intro":"<h2 id=\"lists-maps-and-sets\"><a href=\"#lists-maps-and-sets\">Lists, maps and sets</a></h2>\n<p><code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> and <code>Set</code> need no setup. A new one is empty, and <code>free</code> gives its memory back. A <code>str</code> or <code>String</code> key is copied into a map or a set:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"t\">List</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>] sizes\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> sizes.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> n <span class=\"k\">in</span> [<span class=\"n\">512</span>, <span class=\"n\">64</span>, <span class=\"n\">4096</span>] {\n        sizes.<span class=\"f\">push</span>(n)\n    }\n    <span class=\"f\">sort</span>(sizes.<span class=\"f\">items</span>())\n\n    <span class=\"t\">Map</span>[<span class=\"t\">str</span>, <span class=\"t\">i64</span>] ports\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> ports.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    ports.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;https&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">443</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"t\">Set</span>[<span class=\"t\">str</span>] seen\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> seen.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    _ = seen.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;10.0.0.5&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>sizes.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ports.<span class=\"f\">getOr</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;https&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">0</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>seen.<span class=\"f\">has</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;10.0.0.5&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>64 443 true</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"queues-and-ordered-maps\"><a href=\"#queues-and-ordered-maps\">Queues and ordered maps</a></h2>\n<p><code>Deque</code> adds and takes at both ends. <code>Heap</code> and <code>SortedMap</code> are made with a <code>less</code> function that sets their order:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"t\">bool</span> <span class=\"f\">lower</span>(<span class=\"t\">i64</span> a, <span class=\"t\">i64</span> b) =&gt; a &lt; b\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">Deque</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>] d\n    d.<span class=\"f\">push</span>(<span class=\"n\">2</span>)\n    d.<span class=\"f\">pushFront</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">Heap</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>] h = <span class=\"t\">Heap</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>]{less: lower}\n    h.<span class=\"f\">push</span>(<span class=\"n\">30</span>)\n    h.<span class=\"f\">push</span>(<span class=\"n\">10</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">SortedMap</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>, <span class=\"t\">str</span>] m = <span class=\"t\">SortedMap</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>, <span class=\"t\">str</span>]{less: lower}\n    m.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"n\">404</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;Not Found&quot;</span>)\n    m.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"n\">200</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</span>)\n    k, v, _ := m.<span class=\"f\">first</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>d.<span class=\"f\">popFront</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>h.<span class=\"f\">pop</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>k<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>v<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>1 10 200 OK</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"slices\"><a href=\"#slices\">Slices</a></h2>\n<p><code>sort</code>, <code>reverse</code>, <code>has</code>, <code>indexOf</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>binarySearch</code> take any slice, and a <code>List</code> through <code>items()</code>. <code>sortBy</code> and <code>binarySearchBy</code> take a <code>less</code> function for values that <code>&lt;</code> can't compare.</p>","doc":"The names every program has without an import: List, Map, Set, Deque,\nHeap, SortedMap and Arena, the functions on slices (sort, sortBy,\nbinarySearch, reverse, has, indexOf, min, max), and the built-in\nfunctions print, format, alloc, free, sizeof, sleep, after, panic and the\nothers.","decls":[{"name":"Arena","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Arena {\n    pub i64 blockSize = 64KiB\n}","doc":"Memory for many values that are freed together, like the parts of one\nrequest. a.alloc(T, n) gives n items of T, all at zero, as alloc does.\nThey are not freed one by one: reset gives back everything at once and\nkeeps a block to give out again, and free gives back all of it. The\narena gets its memory from the heap in blocks of blockSize bytes; a\nvalue bigger than a quarter of that gets a block of its own.","file":"core/arena.iris","line":7},{"name":"Arena.reset","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Arena.reset()","doc":"Gives back everything the arena gave out, and keeps one block for more.\nWhat it gave out before must not be used after this.","file":"core/arena.iris","line":23},{"name":"Arena.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Arena.free()","doc":"Gives back all the arena's memory.","file":"core/arena.iris","line":26},{"name":"Arena.used","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Arena.used()","doc":"The bytes given out since the arena was made or reset.","file":"core/arena.iris","line":29},{"name":"Arena.copy","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Arena.copy(str text)","doc":"A copy of the text in the arena's memory.","file":"core/arena.iris","line":32},{"name":"Deque","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Deque[T] {\n    pub i64 len\n}","doc":"A queue with both ends open: values go in and come out at the front and\nat the back, each in the same time however many there are. A new Deque is\nempty; free gives its memory back.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":4},{"name":"Deque.reserve","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Deque[T].reserve(i64 n)","doc":"Makes room for at least n values without growing again.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":17},{"name":"Deque.push","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Deque[T].push(T value)","doc":"Adds a value at the back.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":65},{"name":"Deque.pushFront","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Deque[T].pushFront(T value)","doc":"Adds a value at the front.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":72},{"name":"Deque.pop","kind":"method","sig":"pub T Deque[T].pop()","doc":"Takes the value at the back off.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":80},{"name":"Deque.popFront","kind":"method","sig":"pub T Deque[T].popFront()","doc":"Takes the value at the front off.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":87},{"name":"Deque.first","kind":"method","sig":"pub T Deque[T].first()","doc":"The value at the front.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":96},{"name":"Deque.last","kind":"method","sig":"pub T Deque[T].last()","doc":"The value at the back.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":102},{"name":"Deque.at","kind":"method","sig":"pub T Deque[T].at(i64 i)","doc":"The value at i, counting from the front at 0.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":114},{"name":"Deque.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Deque[T].set(i64 i, T value)","doc":"Replaces the value at i.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":120},{"name":"Deque.clear","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Deque[T].clear()","doc":"Empties the deque and keeps its memory for new values.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":126},{"name":"Deque.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Deque[T].free()","doc":"Gives the deque's memory back; the deque is empty after.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":132},{"name":"Deque.freeEach","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Deque[T].freeEach()","doc":"Frees each value, then the deque: for a deque of Strings or of structs\nwith a free method.","file":"core/deque.iris","line":141},{"name":"Deque.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub (T, bool) Deque[T].next(i64 *at)","doc":"for v in deque, from the front to the back","file":"core/deque.iris","line":149},{"name":"Heap","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Heap[T] {\n    pub bool(T, T) less\n    pub i64 len\n}","doc":"A priority queue: pop always takes out the value that goes first. Which\none that is comes from less, given when the heap is made:\nHeap[Job]{less: sooner}. less(a, b) says whether a goes before b. A new\nHeap is empty; free gives its memory back.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":5},{"name":"Heap.push","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Heap[T].push(T value)","doc":"Adds a value.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":18},{"name":"Heap.peek","kind":"method","sig":"pub T Heap[T].peek()","doc":"The value that goes first, without taking it out.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":34},{"name":"Heap.pop","kind":"method","sig":"pub T Heap[T].pop()","doc":"Takes out the value that goes first.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":42},{"name":"Heap.items","kind":"method","sig":"pub T[] Heap[T].items()","doc":"The values, in no particular order. It is valid until the heap changes.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":70},{"name":"Heap.clear","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Heap[T].clear()","doc":"Takes every value out and keeps the memory.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":73},{"name":"Heap.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Heap[T].free()","doc":"Gives the heap's memory back; the heap is empty after.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":79},{"name":"Heap.freeEach","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Heap[T].freeEach()","doc":"Frees each value, then the heap: for a heap of Strings or of structs with\na free method.","file":"core/heap.iris","line":86},{"name":"List","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct List[T] {\n    pub i64 len\n}","doc":"A list of values that grows as values are added. A new List is empty;\nfree gives its memory back.","file":"core/list.iris","line":3},{"name":"List.reserve","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].reserve(i64 n)","doc":"Makes room for at least n values without growing again.","file":"core/list.iris","line":9},{"name":"List.push","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].push(T value)","doc":"Adds a value at the end.","file":"core/list.iris","line":22},{"name":"List.at","kind":"method","sig":"pub T List[T].at(i64 i)","doc":"The value at i, from 0.","file":"core/list.iris","line":39},{"name":"List.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].set(i64 i, T value)","doc":"Replaces the value at i.","file":"core/list.iris","line":45},{"name":"List.last","kind":"method","sig":"pub T List[T].last()","doc":"The last value.","file":"core/list.iris","line":51},{"name":"List.pop","kind":"method","sig":"pub T List[T].pop()","doc":"Takes the last value off.","file":"core/list.iris","line":57},{"name":"List.insert","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].insert(i64 i, T value)","doc":"Puts a value at i, moving the ones after it up.","file":"core/list.iris","line":64},{"name":"List.remove","kind":"method","sig":"pub T List[T].remove(i64 i)","doc":"Takes the value at i out, moving the ones after it down.","file":"core/list.iris","line":78},{"name":"List.items","kind":"method","sig":"pub T[] List[T].items()","doc":"The values as a slice. It is valid until the list changes size.","file":"core/list.iris","line":89},{"name":"List.sort","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].sort(bool(T, T) less)","doc":"Sorts the values: less says whether a goes before b. Values that are\nequal keep their order.","file":"core/list.iris","line":93},{"name":"List.reverse","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].reverse()","doc":"Turns the order around.","file":"core/list.iris","line":135},{"name":"List.clear","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].clear()","doc":"Empties the list and keeps its memory for new values.","file":"core/list.iris","line":148},{"name":"List.resize","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].resize(i64 n)","doc":"Makes the list n long: new items are zero, and items past n are dropped\n(their memory isn't freed; free them first when they own any).","file":"core/list.iris","line":154},{"name":"List.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].free()","doc":"Gives the list's memory back; the list is empty after.","file":"core/list.iris","line":167},{"name":"List.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub (T, bool) List[T].next(i64 *at)","doc":"for v in list","file":"core/list.iris","line":174},{"name":"List.has","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool List[T].has(T v)","doc":"Whether a value equal to v is in the list. T needs ==.","file":"core/list.iris","line":185},{"name":"List.indexOf","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 List[T].indexOf(T v)","doc":"Where the first value equal to v is, or -1. T needs ==.","file":"core/list.iris","line":188},{"name":"List.freeEach","kind":"method","sig":"pub void List[T].freeEach()","doc":"Frees each value, then the list: for a list of Strings or of structs with\na free method.","file":"core/list.iris","line":199},{"name":"Map","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Map[K, V] {\n    pub i64 len\n}","doc":"A map from keys to values. Keys can be integers, bool, enums, pointers,\nstr or String; a str or String key is copied, so the map keeps its own.\nA new Map is empty; free gives its memory back.","file":"core/map.iris","line":4},{"name":"Map.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Map[K, V].set(K k, V v)","doc":"Sets the value of k, adding k if it isn't there.","file":"core/map.iris","line":74},{"name":"Map.get","kind":"method","sig":"pub (V, bool) Map[K, V].get(K k)","doc":"The value of k, and whether k is there.","file":"core/map.iris","line":91},{"name":"Map.getOr","kind":"method","sig":"pub V Map[K, V].getOr(K k, V fallback)","doc":"The value of k, or fallback when k isn't there.","file":"core/map.iris","line":105},{"name":"Map.has","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Map[K, V].has(K k)","doc":"Whether k is there.","file":"core/map.iris","line":111},{"name":"Map.remove","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Map[K, V].remove(K k)","doc":"Takes k out; false when it wasn't there.","file":"core/map.iris","line":117},{"name":"Map.clear","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Map[K, V].clear()","doc":"Takes every key out and keeps the memory.","file":"core/map.iris","line":133},{"name":"Map.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Map[K, V].free()","doc":"Gives the map's memory back; the map is empty after.","file":"core/map.iris","line":145},{"name":"Map.keys","kind":"method","sig":"pub List[K] Map[K, V].keys()","doc":"The keys, in no particular order. A str or String key is the map's own\ncopy, valid until the key is removed; free only the list.","file":"core/map.iris","line":155},{"name":"Map.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub (K, V, bool) Map[K, V].next(i64 *at)","doc":"for k, v in map, in no particular order","file":"core/map.iris","line":167},{"name":"Set","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Set[T] {\n    pub i64 len\n}","doc":"A set of values, each kept once. Values can be integers, bool, enums,\npointers, str or String; a str or String is copied, so the set keeps its\nown. A new Set is empty; free gives its memory back.","file":"core/set.iris","line":4},{"name":"Set.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Set[T].add(T v)","doc":"Adds v; false when it was there already.","file":"core/set.iris","line":10},{"name":"Set.has","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Set[T].has(T v)","doc":"Whether v is there.","file":"core/set.iris","line":20},{"name":"Set.remove","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Set[T].remove(T v)","doc":"Takes v out; false when it wasn't there.","file":"core/set.iris","line":23},{"name":"Set.clear","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Set[T].clear()","doc":"Takes every value out and keeps the memory.","file":"core/set.iris","line":30},{"name":"Set.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Set[T].free()","doc":"Gives the set's memory back; the set is empty after.","file":"core/set.iris","line":36},{"name":"Set.values","kind":"method","sig":"pub List[T] Set[T].values()","doc":"The values, in no particular order, as a new List. A str or String is\nthe set's own copy, valid until it is removed; free only the list.","file":"core/set.iris","line":43},{"name":"Set.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub (T, bool) Set[T].next(i64 *at)","doc":"for v in set, in no particular order","file":"core/set.iris","line":46},{"name":"sort","kind":"func","sig":"pub void sort[T](T[] xs)","doc":"Sorts the values from small to large, in place: it needs no memory of\nits own. Values that are equal may change places; List.sort keeps them in\ntheir order.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":9},{"name":"sortBy","kind":"func","sig":"pub void sortBy[T](T[] xs, bool(T, T) less)","doc":"Sorts the values in place: less says whether a goes before b. Values\nthat are equal may change places; List.sort keeps them in their order.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":28},{"name":"binarySearch","kind":"func","sig":"pub (i64, bool) binarySearch[T](T[] xs, T v)","doc":"Where v is in a sorted slice, and whether it is there. When it isn't,\nthe place is where it would go to keep the slice sorted.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":253},{"name":"binarySearchBy","kind":"func","sig":"pub (i64, bool) binarySearchBy[T](T[] xs, T v, bool(T, T) less)","doc":"binarySearch for a slice sorted with less.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":268},{"name":"reverse","kind":"func","sig":"pub void reverse[T](T[] xs)","doc":"Turns the order around.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":283},{"name":"has","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool has[T](T[] xs, T v)","doc":"Whether a value equal to v is in the slice. T needs ==.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":296},{"name":"indexOf","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 indexOf[T](T[] xs, T v)","doc":"Where the first value equal to v is, or -1. T needs ==.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":299},{"name":"min","kind":"func","sig":"pub T min[T](T[] xs)","doc":"The smallest value. An empty slice stops the program.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":309},{"name":"max","kind":"func","sig":"pub T max[T](T[] xs)","doc":"The largest value. An empty slice stops the program.","file":"core/slices.iris","line":323},{"name":"SortedMap","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct SortedMap[K, V] {\n    pub bool(K, K) less\n    pub i64 len\n}","doc":"A map that keeps its keys in order, from less, given when the map is\nmade: SortedMap[str, i64]{less: before}. less(a, b) says whether a goes\nbefore b; two keys where neither goes first are the same key. Loops go\nover the keys in order, and range gives the keys between two keys. Keys\ncan be what a Map takes; a str or String key is copied, so the map keeps\nits own. A new SortedMap is empty; free gives its memory back.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":7},{"name":"SortedMap.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void SortedMap[K, V].set(K k, V v)","doc":"Sets the value of k, adding k if it isn't there.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":170},{"name":"SortedMap.get","kind":"method","sig":"pub (V, bool) SortedMap[K, V].get(K k)","doc":"The value of k, and whether k is there.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":288},{"name":"SortedMap.getOr","kind":"method","sig":"pub V SortedMap[K, V].getOr(K k, V fallback)","doc":"The value of k, or fallback when k isn't there.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":298},{"name":"SortedMap.has","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool SortedMap[K, V].has(K k)","doc":"Whether k is there.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":304},{"name":"SortedMap.remove","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool SortedMap[K, V].remove(K k)","doc":"Takes k out; false when it wasn't there.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":310},{"name":"SortedMap.first","kind":"method","sig":"pub (K, V, bool) SortedMap[K, V].first()","doc":"The first key and its value, and false when the map is empty.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":393},{"name":"SortedMap.last","kind":"method","sig":"pub (K, V, bool) SortedMap[K, V].last()","doc":"The last key and its value, and false when the map is empty.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":404},{"name":"SortedMap.keys","kind":"method","sig":"pub List[K] SortedMap[K, V].keys()","doc":"The keys in order, as a new List. A str or String key is the map's own\ncopy, valid until the key is removed; free only the list.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":416},{"name":"SortedMap.clear","kind":"method","sig":"pub void SortedMap[K, V].clear()","doc":"Takes every key out and keeps the memory.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":438},{"name":"SortedMap.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void SortedMap[K, V].free()","doc":"Gives the map's memory back; the map is empty after.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":456},{"name":"SortedMap.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub (K, V, bool) SortedMap[K, V].next(i64 *at)","doc":"for k, v in map, in the order of the keys. The map must not change\nduring the loop.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":481},{"name":"SortedMap.range","kind":"method","sig":"pub SortedRange[K, V] SortedMap[K, V].range(K from, K to)","doc":"The keys from from up to to, to not included, with their values: for\nk, v in map.range(a, b). The map must not change during the loop.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":507},{"name":"SortedRange","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct SortedRange[K, V] { }","doc":"The keys from one key up to another, from range.","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":499},{"name":"SortedRange.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub (K, V, bool) SortedRange[K, V].next(i64 *at)","doc":"for k, v in map.range(a, b)","file":"core/sorted.iris","line":518}]},{"name":"str","title":"Text","group":"Core","summary":"Searching, cutting, trimming and changing text, and reading numbers from it.","intro":"","doc":"Working with text: searching, trimming, splitting, changing case and\nreading numbers. A function whose first parameter is the text is also a\nmethod of str and String, with no import: s.trim() is str.trim(s).","decls":[{"name":"ParseError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum ParseError { empty, notNumber, tooBig }","doc":"What parseInt and parseFloat fail with: empty for empty text, notNumber for\ntext that isn't a number, tooBig for a number that doesn't fit.","file":"str/str.iris","line":7},{"name":"find","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 find(str s, str sub)","doc":"Where sub first appears in s, or -1.","file":"str/str.iris","line":16},{"name":"contains","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool contains(str s, str sub)","doc":"Whether sub appears in s.","file":"str/str.iris","line":27},{"name":"startsWith","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool startsWith(str s, str prefix)","doc":"Whether s starts with prefix.","file":"str/str.iris","line":30},{"name":"endsWith","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool endsWith(str s, str suffix)","doc":"Whether s ends with suffix.","file":"str/str.iris","line":33},{"name":"compare","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 compare(str a, str b)","doc":"Compares a and b byte by byte: below 0 when a comes first, 0 when they\nare equal, above 0 when b comes first.","file":"str/str.iris","line":37},{"name":"count","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 count(str s, str sub)","doc":"How many times sub appears in s, not overlapping.","file":"str/str.iris","line":48},{"name":"trim","kind":"func","sig":"pub str trim(str s)","doc":"s without spaces, tabs and line breaks at either end.","file":"str/str.iris","line":67},{"name":"trimLeft","kind":"func","sig":"pub str trimLeft(str s)","doc":"s without spaces, tabs and line breaks at the start.","file":"str/str.iris","line":70},{"name":"trimRight","kind":"func","sig":"pub str trimRight(str s)","doc":"s without spaces, tabs and line breaks at the end.","file":"str/str.iris","line":79},{"name":"cut","kind":"func","sig":"pub (str, str, bool) cut(str s, str sep)","doc":"The text before and after the first sep, and whether sep was found.","file":"str/str.iris","line":88},{"name":"findLast","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 findLast(str s, str sub)","doc":"Where sub last appears in s, or -1.","file":"str/str.iris","line":97},{"name":"split","kind":"func","sig":"pub List[str] split(str s, str sep)","doc":"The parts of s between each sep: \"a,b,,c\" split at \",\" is \"a\", \"b\", \"\"\nand \"c\". The parts point into s; the List is the caller's to free.","file":"str/str.iris","line":113},{"name":"fields","kind":"func","sig":"pub List[str] fields(str s)","doc":"The words of s: the parts between runs of spaces, tabs and line breaks,\nwith none empty. They point into s.","file":"str/str.iris","line":132},{"name":"format","kind":"builtin","sig":"format","doc":"The text with its {} values put in, as a new String, like format. In a\nconst, with constants in the braces, it is worked out when compiling and\ngives a str: const QUERY = str.format(\"SELECT 1 FROM {TABLE}\").","file":"str/str.iris","line":154},{"name":"join","kind":"func","sig":"pub String join(str[] parts, str sep)","doc":"The parts one after the other, with sep between them.","file":"str/str.iris","line":157},{"name":"lower","kind":"func","sig":"pub String lower(str s)","doc":"A copy of s with ASCII letters in lower case.","file":"str/str.iris","line":169},{"name":"upper","kind":"func","sig":"pub String upper(str s)","doc":"A copy of s with ASCII letters in upper case.","file":"str/str.iris","line":181},{"name":"replace","kind":"func","sig":"pub String replace(str s, str old, str new)","doc":"s with every old replaced by new.","file":"str/str.iris","line":193},{"name":"repeat","kind":"func","sig":"pub String repeat(str s, i64 n)","doc":"s n times in a row.","file":"str/str.iris","line":213},{"name":"parseInt","kind":"func","sig":"pub !i64 parseInt(str s)","doc":"The integer written in s, with an optional - or + in front.","file":"str/str.iris","line":222},{"name":"parseFloat","kind":"func","sig":"pub !f64 parseFloat(str s)","doc":"The floating point number written in s: 1.5, -2e10, inf, nan.","file":"str/str.iris","line":250},{"name":"parseIntBase","kind":"func","sig":"pub !i64 parseIntBase(str s, i64 base)","doc":"The integer written in s in a base from 2 to 36, with an optional - or\n+ in front: \"ff\" in base 16 is 255.","file":"str/str.iris","line":264},{"name":"REPLACEMENT","kind":"const","sig":"pub const i32 REPLACEMENT = 0xfffd","doc":"The character that replaces bytes that aren't UTF-8.","file":"str/str.iris","line":299},{"name":"decodeRune","kind":"func","sig":"pub (i32, i64) decodeRune(str s, i64 at)","doc":"The character (code point) that starts at byte at of s, and how many\nbytes it takes. Bytes that aren't UTF-8 give REPLACEMENT and 1.","file":"str/str.iris","line":303},{"name":"Runes","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Runes {\n    pub str text\n}","doc":"The characters of a text, for a loop: for r in s.runes() { }.","file":"str/str.iris","line":316},{"name":"Runes.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub (i32, bool) Runes.next(i64 *at)","doc":"for r in s.runes(): the next character.","file":"str/str.iris","line":325},{"name":"runes","kind":"func","sig":"pub Runes runes(str s)","doc":"The characters of s, one code point at a time.","file":"str/str.iris","line":322},{"name":"runeCount","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 runeCount(str s)","doc":"How many characters (code points) s has; bytes that aren't UTF-8 count\none each.","file":"str/str.iris","line":355},{"name":"validUtf8","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool validUtf8(str s)","doc":"Whether s is all UTF-8.","file":"str/str.iris","line":381},{"name":"appendRune","kind":"func","sig":"pub void appendRune(String *out, i32 r)","doc":"Adds the UTF-8 bytes of character r to out; a value that isn't a\ncharacter adds REPLACEMENT.","file":"str/str.iris","line":385},{"name":"fromRune","kind":"func","sig":"pub String fromRune(i32 r)","doc":"Character r as a new String.","file":"str/str.iris","line":412},{"name":"isDigit","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isDigit(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is a decimal digit, 0 to 9.","file":"str/str.iris","line":419},{"name":"isHexDigit","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isHexDigit(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is a hex digit: 0 to 9, a to f or A to F.","file":"str/str.iris","line":422},{"name":"isLetter","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isLetter(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is an ASCII letter, a to z or A to Z.","file":"str/str.iris","line":425},{"name":"isAlnum","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isAlnum(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is an ASCII letter or digit.","file":"str/str.iris","line":428},{"name":"isUpper","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isUpper(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is an upper-case ASCII letter.","file":"str/str.iris","line":431},{"name":"isLower","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isLower(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is a lower-case ASCII letter.","file":"str/str.iris","line":434},{"name":"isSpace","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isSpace(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is white space: space, tab, a line break, or one of Unicode's\nspaces such as the no-break space.","file":"str/str.iris","line":438},{"name":"isPunct","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isPunct(i32 c)","doc":"Whether c is ASCII punctuation: !\"#$%&'()*+,-./:;<=>?@[\\]^_`{|}~.","file":"str/str.iris","line":446},{"name":"toUpper","kind":"func","sig":"pub i32 toUpper(i32 c)","doc":"c in upper case, for ASCII letters; anything else as it is.","file":"str/str.iris","line":449},{"name":"toLower","kind":"func","sig":"pub i32 toLower(i32 c)","doc":"c in lower case, for ASCII letters; anything else as it is.","file":"str/str.iris","line":452},{"name":"padLeft","kind":"func","sig":"pub String padLeft(str s, i64 width, str fill = \" \")","doc":"s with fill in front until it is width characters wide; s itself when it\nis that wide already.","file":"str/str.iris","line":458},{"name":"padRight","kind":"func","sig":"pub String padRight(str s, i64 width, str fill = \" \")","doc":"s with fill after it until it is width characters wide.","file":"str/str.iris","line":467},{"name":"splitN","kind":"func","sig":"pub List[str] splitN(str s, str sep, i64 n)","doc":"The parts of s between each sep, at most n of them: the last part holds\nthe rest. n of 0 or less gives all parts, as split does.","file":"str/str.iris","line":497},{"name":"trimPrefix","kind":"func","sig":"pub str trimPrefix(str s, str prefix)","doc":"s without prefix at its start, when it starts with it; s otherwise.","file":"str/str.iris","line":520},{"name":"trimSuffix","kind":"func","sig":"pub str trimSuffix(str s, str suffix)","doc":"s without suffix at its end, when it ends with it; s otherwise.","file":"str/str.iris","line":523},{"name":"cutLast","kind":"func","sig":"pub (str, str, bool) cutLast(str s, str sep)","doc":"The text before and after the last sep, and whether sep was found.","file":"str/str.iris","line":526},{"name":"lines","kind":"func","sig":"pub List[str] lines(str s)","doc":"The lines of s, without their line breaks (\\n or \\r\\n). A line break at\nthe very end doesn't make an empty last line. They point into s.","file":"str/str.iris","line":536},{"name":"equalFold","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool equalFold(str a, str b)","doc":"Whether a and b are the same text when ASCII letters are compared\nwithout their case: \"Content-Type\" and \"content-type\".","file":"str/str.iris","line":552},{"name":"title","kind":"func","sig":"pub String title(str s)","doc":"A copy of s with the first ASCII letter of each word in upper case.","file":"str/str.iris","line":565},{"name":"reverse","kind":"func","sig":"pub String reverse(str s)","doc":"s with its characters in the opposite order.","file":"str/str.iris","line":580}]},{"name":"math","title":"Maths","group":"Core","summary":"Constants and functions on floating point numbers, and integer maths: gcd, square roots, powers, floor division, and checked, saturating and wrapping arithmetic.","intro":"","doc":"Floating point math: roots, powers, logarithms, rounding and angles.","decls":[{"name":"pi","kind":"const","sig":"pub const pi = 3.141592653589793","doc":"The ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter.","file":"math/math.iris","line":4},{"name":"e","kind":"const","sig":"pub const e = 2.718281828459045","doc":"The base of the natural logarithm.","file":"math/math.iris","line":6},{"name":"sqrt","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 sqrt(f64 x)","doc":"The square root of x; NaN for a negative x.","file":"math/math.iris","line":10},{"name":"cbrt","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 cbrt(f64 x)","doc":"The cube root of x.","file":"math/math.iris","line":14},{"name":"pow","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 pow(f64 x, f64 y)","doc":"x to the power y.","file":"math/math.iris","line":18},{"name":"exp","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 exp(f64 x)","doc":"e to the power x.","file":"math/math.iris","line":22},{"name":"log","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 log(f64 x)","doc":"The natural logarithm of x; -inf for 0, NaN below it.","file":"math/math.iris","line":26},{"name":"log2","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 log2(f64 x)","doc":"The base-2 logarithm of x.","file":"math/math.iris","line":30},{"name":"log10","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 log10(f64 x)","doc":"The base-10 logarithm of x.","file":"math/math.iris","line":34},{"name":"floor","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 floor(f64 x)","doc":"The largest whole number not above x: floor(2.7) is 2, floor(-2.1) is -3.","file":"math/math.iris","line":38},{"name":"ceil","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 ceil(f64 x)","doc":"The smallest whole number not below x: ceil(2.1) is 3.","file":"math/math.iris","line":42},{"name":"round","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 round(f64 x)","doc":"x rounded to the nearest whole number, halves away from zero: round(2.5) is\n3.","file":"math/math.iris","line":47},{"name":"trunc","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 trunc(f64 x)","doc":"x with its fraction dropped, toward zero: trunc(-2.7) is -2.","file":"math/math.iris","line":51},{"name":"abs","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 abs(f64 x)","doc":"x without its sign.","file":"math/math.iris","line":55},{"name":"sin","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 sin(f64 x)","doc":"The sine of x, in radians.","file":"math/math.iris","line":59},{"name":"cos","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 cos(f64 x)","doc":"The cosine of x, in radians.","file":"math/math.iris","line":63},{"name":"tan","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 tan(f64 x)","doc":"The tangent of x, in radians.","file":"math/math.iris","line":67},{"name":"atan2","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 atan2(f64 y, f64 x)","doc":"The angle of the point (x, y) from the x axis, in radians, from -pi to pi.","file":"math/math.iris","line":71},{"name":"hypot","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 hypot(f64 x, f64 y)","doc":"The length of the hypotenuse: the square root of x*x + y*y, without overflow\nalong the way.","file":"math/math.iris","line":76},{"name":"min","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 min(f64 a, b)","doc":"The smaller of a and b.","file":"math/math.iris","line":79},{"name":"max","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 max(f64 a, b)","doc":"The larger of a and b.","file":"math/math.iris","line":82},{"name":"isNaN","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isNaN(f64 x)","doc":"Whether x is not a number (NaN).","file":"math/math.iris","line":85},{"name":"inf","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 inf()","doc":"Infinity.","file":"math/math.iris","line":88},{"name":"clamp","kind":"func","sig":"pub T clamp[T](T x, T lo, T hi)","doc":"x, kept from lo to hi: lo when below it, hi when above.","file":"math/math.iris","line":93},{"name":"absInt","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 absInt(i64 x)","doc":"x without its sign. The smallest i64 has no positive twin and overflows.","file":"math/math.iris","line":96},{"name":"gcd","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 gcd(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"The greatest common divisor of a and b, never negative; 0 for 0 and 0.","file":"math/math.iris","line":99},{"name":"lcm","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 lcm(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"The least common multiple of a and b, never negative; 0 when either is 0.","file":"math/math.iris","line":111},{"name":"isqrt","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 isqrt(i64 x)","doc":"The square root of x, rounded down; x must not be negative.","file":"math/math.iris","line":119},{"name":"ipow","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 ipow(i64 base, i64 exp)","doc":"base to the power exp, for an exp of 0 or more.","file":"math/math.iris","line":136},{"name":"divFloor","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 divFloor(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a divided by b, rounded down rather than toward zero: -7 / 2 is -4.","file":"math/math.iris","line":156},{"name":"mod","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 mod(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"The remainder of a divided by b, with the sign of b: -7 mod 3 is 2.","file":"math/math.iris","line":162},{"name":"addChecked","kind":"func","sig":"pub (i64, bool) addChecked(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a + b, and false when it doesn't fit in an i64.","file":"math/math.iris","line":177},{"name":"subChecked","kind":"func","sig":"pub (i64, bool) subChecked(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a - b, and false when it doesn't fit in an i64.","file":"math/math.iris","line":184},{"name":"mulChecked","kind":"func","sig":"pub (i64, bool) mulChecked(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a * b, and false when it doesn't fit in an i64.","file":"math/math.iris","line":191},{"name":"addSat","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 addSat(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a + b, or the largest or smallest i64 when it doesn't fit.","file":"math/math.iris","line":198},{"name":"subSat","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 subSat(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a - b, or the largest or smallest i64 when it doesn't fit.","file":"math/math.iris","line":201},{"name":"mulSat","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 mulSat(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a * b, or the largest or smallest i64 when it doesn't fit.","file":"math/math.iris","line":204},{"name":"addWrap","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 addWrap(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a + b, wrapping around past the ends of i64, in every build.","file":"math/math.iris","line":207},{"name":"subWrap","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 subWrap(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a - b, wrapping around past the ends of i64, in every build.","file":"math/math.iris","line":210},{"name":"mulWrap","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 mulWrap(i64 a, i64 b)","doc":"a * b, wrapping around past the ends of i64, in every build.","file":"math/math.iris","line":213}]},{"name":"big","title":"Big numbers","group":"Core","summary":"Whole numbers of any size, with division, powers, modular powers and any base from 2 to 36.","intro":"<h2 id=\"numbers-of-any-size\"><a href=\"#numbers-of-any-size\">Numbers of any size</a></h2>\n<p><code>big.parse</code> reads a whole number from text and <code>big.from</code> makes one from an <code>i64</code>. Each operation gives a new <code>Int</code>, and <code>{n}</code> writes it in decimal:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> big\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> a := <span class=\"k\">try</span> big.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;123456789012345678901234567890&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> b := big.<span class=\"f\">from</span>(-<span class=\"n\">987654321</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> sum := a.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(b)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> product := a.<span class=\"f\">mul</span>(b)\n    q, r := a.<span class=\"f\">divMod</span>(b)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> {\n        q.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n        r.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>sum<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>product<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>q<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> remainder </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>123456789012345678900246913569\n-121932631124828532112482853211126352690\n-124999998873437499901 remainder 574845669</code></pre>\n<p>Division rounds toward zero. The remainder has the sign of the number divided, as with integers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"powers-and-other-bases\"><a href=\"#powers-and-other-bases\">Powers and other bases</a></h2>\n<p><code>pow</code> raises to a power. <code>modPow</code> raises to a power modulo a number without making the whole power. <code>text</code> writes a number in any base from 2 to 36:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> big\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> two := big.<span class=\"f\">from</span>(<span class=\"n\">2</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> p := two.<span class=\"f\">pow</span>(<span class=\"n\">100</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> hex := p.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"n\">16</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>p<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> has </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>p.<span class=\"f\">bitLen</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bits: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>hex<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> m := <span class=\"k\">try</span> big.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;1000000007&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> exp := <span class=\"k\">try</span> big.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;1000000005&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> inv := big.<span class=\"f\">from</span>(<span class=\"n\">3</span>).<span class=\"f\">modPow</span>(exp, m)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;3 * </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>inv<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> mod </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> is 1&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> mask := <span class=\"k\">try</span> big.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff_ffff&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">16</span>)\n    n := mask.<span class=\"f\">toI64</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"n\">0</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>1267650600228229401496703205376 has 101 bits: 10000000000000000000000000\n3 * 333333336 mod 1000000007 is 1\nBigError.tooBig</code></pre>","doc":"Whole numbers of any size: adding, multiplying, dividing, powers, and\nreading and writing them in any base from 2 to 36. Each operation gives\na new Int, which its caller frees.","decls":[{"name":"BigError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum BigError { invalid, tooBig }","doc":"What reading or converting a number fails with: invalid for text that\nisn't a number, tooBig for a value that doesn't fit in an i64.","file":"big/big.iris","line":7},{"name":"Int","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Int { }","doc":"A whole number of any size. Its zero value is 0.","file":"big/big.iris","line":10},{"name":"Int.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Int.free()","doc":"Gives the number's memory back.","file":"big/big.iris","line":51},{"name":"Int.copy","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.copy()","doc":"A copy of the number.","file":"big/big.iris","line":86},{"name":"Int.isZero","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Int.isZero()","doc":"Whether the number is 0.","file":"big/big.iris","line":97},{"name":"Int.sign","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Int.sign()","doc":"-1, 0 or 1, as the number is below, at or above 0.","file":"big/big.iris","line":100},{"name":"Int.bitLen","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Int.bitLen()","doc":"How many bits the magnitude takes: 0 for 0, 8 for 255.","file":"big/big.iris","line":103},{"name":"Int.toI64","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Int.toI64()","doc":"The number as an i64; BigError.tooBig when it doesn't fit.","file":"big/big.iris","line":118},{"name":"Int.negate","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.negate()","doc":"The number with its sign turned around.","file":"big/big.iris","line":207},{"name":"Int.abs","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.abs()","doc":"The number without its sign.","file":"big/big.iris","line":214},{"name":"Int.compare","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Int.compare(Int b)","doc":"Compares with b: below 0 when the number is less, 0 when equal, above 0\nwhen greater.","file":"big/big.iris","line":222},{"name":"Int.equal","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Int.equal(Int b)","doc":"Whether the number equals b.","file":"big/big.iris","line":231},{"name":"Int.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.add(Int b)","doc":"The number plus b.","file":"big/big.iris","line":234},{"name":"Int.sub","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.sub(Int b)","doc":"The number minus b.","file":"big/big.iris","line":250},{"name":"Int.mul","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.mul(Int b)","doc":"The number times b.","file":"big/big.iris","line":257},{"name":"Int.divMod","kind":"method","sig":"pub (Int, Int) Int.divMod(Int b)","doc":"The number divided by b, rounded toward zero, and the remainder, which\nhas the number's sign, as with integers. b must not be 0.","file":"big/big.iris","line":261},{"name":"Int.div","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.div(Int b)","doc":"The number divided by b, rounded toward zero. b must not be 0.","file":"big/big.iris","line":270},{"name":"Int.rem","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.rem(Int b)","doc":"The remainder of the number divided by b, with the number's sign.","file":"big/big.iris","line":277},{"name":"Int.pow","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.pow(i64 e)","doc":"The number to the power e, for an e of 0 or more.","file":"big/big.iris","line":284},{"name":"Int.modPow","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.modPow(Int e, Int m)","doc":"The number to the power e, modulo m: for e of 0 or more and m above 0,\nwithout the full power ever being made.","file":"big/big.iris","line":310},{"name":"Int.gcd","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.gcd(Int b)","doc":"The greatest common divisor of the number and b, never negative.","file":"big/big.iris","line":346},{"name":"Int.shl","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.shl(i64 n)","doc":"The number times 2 to the power n, for an n of 0 or more.","file":"big/big.iris","line":360},{"name":"Int.shr","kind":"method","sig":"pub Int Int.shr(i64 n)","doc":"The number divided by 2 to the power n, rounded toward zero, for an n\nof 0 or more.","file":"big/big.iris","line":376},{"name":"Int.text","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Int.text(i64 base = 10)","doc":"The number written in a base from 2 to 36, with lower-case letters past\n9 and a - in front when it is negative.","file":"big/big.iris","line":447},{"name":"Int.format","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Int.format()","doc":"The number in decimal, so that \"{n}\" writes it.","file":"big/big.iris","line":512},{"name":"from","kind":"func","sig":"pub Int from(i64 v)","doc":"v as an Int.","file":"big/big.iris","line":75},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Int parse(str s, i64 base = 10)","doc":"The number written in s in a base from 2 to 36, with an optional - or +\nin front; BigError.invalid when it isn't one. _ between digits is\nallowed: \"1_000_000\".","file":"big/big.iris","line":395}]},{"name":"random","title":"Random numbers","group":"Core","summary":"Numbers and bytes from the system's random source, and seeded generators.","intro":"","doc":"Random numbers. The functions here use a fast generator of the thread's\nown, seeded from the system; they are not for secrets: use bytes for\nkeys and tokens. An Rng gives the same numbers again from the same seed.","decls":[{"name":"int","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 int(i64 lo, i64 hi)","doc":"A number from lo up to hi - 1: int(1, 7) is a die.","file":"random/random.iris","line":29},{"name":"float","kind":"func","sig":"pub f64 float()","doc":"A number from 0 up to 1, never 1.","file":"random/random.iris","line":32},{"name":"coin","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool coin()","doc":"true or false.","file":"random/random.iris","line":35},{"name":"bytes","kind":"func","sig":"pub void bytes(u8[] buf)","doc":"Fills buf with bytes from the system's secure source, for keys, tokens\nand passwords.","file":"random/random.iris","line":39},{"name":"shuffle","kind":"func","sig":"pub void shuffle[T](T[] items)","doc":"Puts the items in a random order.","file":"random/random.iris","line":42},{"name":"Rng","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Rng { }","doc":"A generator that gives the same numbers from the same seed.","file":"random/random.iris","line":54},{"name":"Rng.int","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Rng.int(i64 lo, i64 hi)","doc":"A number from lo up to hi - 1, from this generator.","file":"random/random.iris","line":66},{"name":"Rng.float","kind":"method","sig":"pub f64 Rng.float()","doc":"A number from 0 up to 1, from this generator.","file":"random/random.iris","line":69},{"name":"Rng.coin","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Rng.coin()","doc":"true or false, each half the time, from this generator.","file":"random/random.iris","line":72},{"name":"seeded","kind":"func","sig":"pub Rng seeded(u64 seed)","doc":"A generator started from seed.","file":"random/random.iris","line":59}]},{"name":"time","title":"Time","group":"Core","summary":"The time of day, dates, a clock for measuring, and Durations.","intro":"<h2 id=\"dates\"><a href=\"#dates\">Dates</a></h2>\n<p><code>time.date</code> makes a moment from its parts, in UTC. Its methods give the parts back, and <code>format</code> writes it with a layout:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> time\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    t := time.<span class=\"f\">date</span>(<span class=\"n\">2026</span>, <span class=\"n\">10</span>, <span class=\"n\">17</span>, <span class=\"n\">9</span>, <span class=\"n\">30</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>t.<span class=\"f\">year</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">-</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>t.<span class=\"f\">month</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">-</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>t.<span class=\"f\">day</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> is a </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>t.<span class=\"f\">weekday</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> iso = t.<span class=\"f\">iso</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> eu = t.<span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;DD/MM/YYYY hh:mm&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>iso<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>eu<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    later := t.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"n\">36h</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> d = later.<span class=\"f\">dateText</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;36 hours later: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>d<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>later.<span class=\"f\">sub</span>(t)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> after&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>2026-10-17 is a saturday\n2026-10-17T09:30:00Z, 17/10/2026 09:30\n36 hours later: 2026-10-18, 36h0m0s after</code></pre>\n<p>The layout's letters are <code>YYYY</code>, <code>MM</code>, <code>DD</code>, <code>hh</code>, <code>mm</code>, <code>ss</code>, <code>SSS</code> (milliseconds) and <code>Z</code> (the offset). Other characters are copied.</p>\n<h2 id=\"time-zones\"><a href=\"#time-zones\">Time zones</a></h2>\n<p>A moment is the same in every time zone. The offset only changes how it is shown. <code>local()</code> shows it in the computer's time zone, <code>utc()</code> in UTC, and <code>withOffset</code> with an offset in seconds east of UTC:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> time\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    t := time.<span class=\"f\">date</span>(<span class=\"n\">2026</span>, <span class=\"n\">10</span>, <span class=\"n\">17</span>, <span class=\"n\">9</span>, <span class=\"n\">30</span>)\n    rome := t.<span class=\"f\">withOffset</span>(<span class=\"n\">2</span> * <span class=\"n\">3600</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> a = rome.<span class=\"f\">iso</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>a<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, hour </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>rome.<span class=\"f\">hour</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, same moment: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>rome.<span class=\"f\">unix</span>() == t.<span class=\"f\">unix</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>2026-10-17T11:30:00+02:00, hour 11, same moment: true</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"parsing\"><a href=\"#parsing\">Parsing</a></h2>\n<p><code>time.parse</code> reads ISO 8601 text, with or without an offset. Any other text fails with <code>TimeError.invalid</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> time\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    t := <span class=\"k\">try</span> time.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;2026-10-17T11:30:00+02:00&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> u = t.<span class=\"f\">utc</span>().<span class=\"f\">iso</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(u)\n\n    _ = time.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;yesterday&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        time.<span class=\"t\">Time</span>{}\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>2026-10-17T09:30:00Z\nTimeError.invalid</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"measuring\"><a href=\"#measuring\">Measuring</a></h2>\n<p><code>time.now()</code> is the wall clock, which jumps when the computer's clock is set. To measure how long something takes, use <code>time.clock()</code>, which gives a <code>Duration</code> from a clock that only goes forward:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> time\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    start := time.<span class=\"f\">clock</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">work</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;took </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>time.<span class=\"f\">clock</span>() - start<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>","doc":"Time: the wall clock, a clock for measuring, and dates. A Time is a\nmoment; it is shown in UTC unless local() or an offset says otherwise.","decls":[{"name":"TimeError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum TimeError { invalid }","doc":"What parse and parseDuration fail with: invalid for text they can't read.","file":"time/time.iris","line":7},{"name":"Weekday","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Weekday { monday, tuesday, wednesday, thursday, friday, saturday, sunday }","doc":"The days of the week, Monday first.","file":"time/time.iris","line":10},{"name":"Time","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Time {\n    pub i64 ns\n    pub i32 offset\n}","doc":"A moment: nanoseconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC, and the offset of the\ntime zone it is shown in, in seconds east of UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":14},{"name":"Time.local","kind":"method","sig":"pub Time Time.local()","doc":"The same moment in the computer's time zone.","file":"time/time.iris","line":91},{"name":"Time.utc","kind":"method","sig":"pub Time Time.utc()","doc":"The same moment in UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":94},{"name":"Time.withOffset","kind":"method","sig":"pub Time Time.withOffset(i32 seconds)","doc":"The same moment shown with an offset in seconds east of UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":97},{"name":"Time.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub Time Time.add(Duration d)","doc":"The moment d after this one; a negative d goes back.","file":"time/time.iris","line":100},{"name":"Time.addDays","kind":"method","sig":"pub Time Time.addDays(i64 n)","doc":"The same time of day, days later on the calendar, in the time zone the\nmoment is shown in; a negative n goes back.","file":"time/time.iris","line":104},{"name":"Time.addMonths","kind":"method","sig":"pub Time Time.addMonths(i64 n)","doc":"The same day and time, months later on the calendar. A day the month\ndoesn't have becomes its last: January 31 and a month is February 28.","file":"time/time.iris","line":108},{"name":"Time.addYears","kind":"method","sig":"pub Time Time.addYears(i64 n)","doc":"The same day and time, years later. February 29 becomes February 28 in a\nyear that isn't a leap year.","file":"time/time.iris","line":112},{"name":"Time.sub","kind":"method","sig":"pub Duration Time.sub(Time other)","doc":"The time from other to this moment.","file":"time/time.iris","line":131},{"name":"Time.before","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Time.before(Time other)","doc":"Whether this moment is before other.","file":"time/time.iris","line":134},{"name":"Time.after","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Time.after(Time other)","doc":"Whether this moment is after other.","file":"time/time.iris","line":137},{"name":"Time.unix","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.unix()","doc":"Seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":140},{"name":"Time.unixMs","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.unixMs()","doc":"Milliseconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":143},{"name":"Time.year","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.year()","doc":"The year, in the time zone the moment is shown in.","file":"time/time.iris","line":146},{"name":"Time.month","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.month()","doc":"1 to 12.","file":"time/time.iris","line":153},{"name":"Time.day","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.day()","doc":"1 to 31.","file":"time/time.iris","line":160},{"name":"Time.hour","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.hour()","doc":"The hour, 0 to 23.","file":"time/time.iris","line":167},{"name":"Time.minute","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.minute()","doc":"The minute, 0 to 59.","file":"time/time.iris","line":173},{"name":"Time.second","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.second()","doc":"The second, 0 to 59.","file":"time/time.iris","line":179},{"name":"Time.nanosecond","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Time.nanosecond()","doc":"The nanoseconds after the second, 0 to 999999999.","file":"time/time.iris","line":185},{"name":"Time.weekday","kind":"method","sig":"pub Weekday Time.weekday()","doc":"The day of the week.","file":"time/time.iris","line":191},{"name":"Time.format","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Time.format(str layout = \"\")","doc":"The moment written with a layout: YYYY year, MM month, DD day, hh hour,\nmm minute, ss second, SSS milliseconds, Z the offset (Z for UTC, else\n+02:00). Other characters are copied. Without a layout it is iso(), which\nis also how a Time is put into text: \"{t}\".","file":"time/time.iris","line":211},{"name":"Time.iso","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Time.iso()","doc":"ISO 8601, as in 2026-10-17T09:30:00Z, with the fraction of a second when\nthere is one.","file":"time/time.iris","line":267},{"name":"Time.dateText","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Time.dateText()","doc":"The date, as in 2026-10-17.","file":"time/time.iris","line":286},{"name":"now","kind":"func","sig":"pub Time now()","doc":"Now, in UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":34},{"name":"clock","kind":"func","sig":"pub Duration clock()","doc":"A clock that only goes forward, for measuring: the difference of two\nreadings is the time between them.","file":"time/time.iris","line":38},{"name":"since","kind":"func","sig":"pub Duration since(Duration start)","doc":"How long it has been since start, a reading of clock().","file":"time/time.iris","line":41},{"name":"unix","kind":"func","sig":"pub Time unix(i64 seconds)","doc":"The moment a number of seconds after 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":44},{"name":"date","kind":"func","sig":"pub Time date(i64 year, i64 month, i64 day, i64 hour = 0, i64 minute = 0, i64 second = 0)","doc":"A date and time in UTC. month is 1 to 12; a month or day past the end\ngoes on into the next year or month: date(2026, 14, 1) is 2027-02-01,\ndate(2026, 1, 32) is 2026-02-01.","file":"time/time.iris","line":49},{"name":"parseDuration","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Duration parseDuration(str s)","doc":"Reads a duration as it is written: 1m30s, 250ms, 1.5h, 2h3m0s. The\nunits are ns, us, ms, s, m and h; a number alone is seconds.","file":"time/time.iris","line":314},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Time parse(str s)","doc":"Reads ISO 8601: 2026-10-17, 2026-10-17T09:30, 2026-10-17T09:30:00Z,\n2026-10-17 09:30:00.250+02:00. Without an offset it is UTC.","file":"time/time.iris","line":324}]},{"name":"pool","title":"Pools","group":"Core","summary":"Connections, or anything costly to open, shared by tasks and threads.","intro":"<h2 id=\"a-pool-of-connections\"><a href=\"#a-pool-of-connections\">A pool of connections</a></h2>\n<p><code>pool.make</code> takes what opening an item needs, such as an address, and the function that opens one. <code>take</code> gives an idle item or opens a new one. <code>give</code> puts it back for the next caller.</p>\n<p>Any struct with <code>pub</code> methods <code>close</code> and <code>reusable</code> can be pooled. <code>reusable</code> says whether the next caller can use the item. An item that isn't reusable is closed when it is given back:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> pool\n\n<span class=\"c\">// A connection to a backend, as a real one would hold a net.Conn.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Backend</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> id\n    <span class=\"t\">bool</span> broken\n}\n\n<span class=\"k\">pub</span> <span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"t\">Backend</span>.<span class=\"f\">close</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;close </span><span class=\"e\">{</span><span class=\"k\">self</span>.id<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">pub</span> <span class=\"t\">bool</span> <span class=\"t\">Backend</span>.<span class=\"f\">reusable</span>() =&gt; !<span class=\"k\">self</span>.broken\n\n<span class=\"t\">i64</span> opened = <span class=\"n\">0</span>\n\n!<span class=\"t\">Backend</span> <span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"t\">str</span> *address) {\n    opened += <span class=\"n\">1</span>\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;open </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>opened<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> to </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>*address<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"t\">Backend</span>{id: opened}\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    backends := pool.<span class=\"f\">make</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;10.0.0.5:9000&quot;</span>, dial, max: <span class=\"n\">2</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> backends.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    a := <span class=\"k\">try</span> backends.<span class=\"f\">take</span>()\n    backends.<span class=\"f\">give</span>(a)\n\n    b := <span class=\"k\">try</span> backends.<span class=\"f\">take</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;got </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b.id<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> again&quot;</span>)\n    b.broken = <span class=\"k\">true</span>\n    backends.<span class=\"f\">give</span>(b)\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;open now: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>backends.<span class=\"f\">size</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>open 1 to 10.0.0.5:9000\ngot 1 again\nclose 1\nopen now: 0</code></pre>\n<p>When <code>max</code> items are in use, <code>take</code> waits until one is given back. After <code>wait</code> (30 seconds unless given) it fails with <code>PoolError.timeout</code>.</p>\n<p>A <code>Pool</code> can be copied and shared by tasks and threads, and the copies share the items. The <code>redis</code> client keeps its connections in a pool.</p>","doc":"A pool of connections, or of anything that is costly to open and can be\nused again: tasks and threads take one, use it, and give it back.\n\n    p := pool.make(address, connect, max: 8)\n    defer p.close()\n    c := try p.take()\n    defer p.give(c)\n\nT is what is pooled. It has close, and reusable, which says whether it\ncan be given to the next caller or should be closed instead. C is what\nopening one needs, such as an address: the pool keeps it and calls\nopen with it.","decls":[{"name":"PoolError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum PoolError { timeout }","doc":"What take fails with when no item comes free in time. A failure to open\none is the open function's own error.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":18},{"name":"Item","kind":"interface","sig":"pub interface Item {\n    void close()\n    bool reusable()\n}","doc":"What a pooled item has: close, and reusable, false when it is broken or\nin a state the next caller can't use, so the pool closes it.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":22},{"name":"Pool","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Pool[T: Item, C] { }","doc":"Items shared by tasks and threads. Copies of a Pool share the same\nitems.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":40},{"name":"Pool.take","kind":"method","sig":"pub !T Pool[T, C].take()","doc":"An item: an idle one, a new one, or, when max are in use, the next one\ngiven back. Give it back with give when done.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":59},{"name":"Pool.give","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Pool[T, C].give(T it)","doc":"Gives an item back. One that isn't reusable, or one given back after\nclose, is closed.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":89},{"name":"Pool.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Pool[T, C].add(T it)","doc":"Adds an item opened elsewhere, such as a first one opened to check the\nconfig, to the idle ones. It counts toward max.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":103},{"name":"Pool.size","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Pool[T, C].size()","doc":"How many items are open: idle ones and ones in use.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":111},{"name":"Pool.idle","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Pool[T, C].idle()","doc":"How many items are idle in the pool.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":119},{"name":"Pool.config","kind":"method","sig":"pub C *Pool[T, C].config()","doc":"The config the pool opens items with.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":127},{"name":"Pool.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Pool[T, C].close()","doc":"Closes the idle items. Items in use are closed when they are given back.\nThe config is the caller's to free.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":131},{"name":"make","kind":"func","sig":"pub Pool[T, C] make[T: Item, C](C config, !T(C *) open, i64 max = 10, Duration wait = 30s)","doc":"A pool that opens items with open(&config), at most max at a time. take\nwaits up to wait for one to come free when max are in use. Nothing is\nopened until the first take.","file":"pool/pool.iris","line":47}]},{"name":"sync","title":"Waiting and limits","group":"Core","summary":"Wait groups, semaphores, run-once, read-write locks and a pool of worker tasks, for tasks and threads.","intro":"<h2 id=\"waiting-for-others\"><a href=\"#waiting-for-others\">Waiting for others</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>WaitGroup</code> counts what is still running. Call <code>add</code> before starting each task or thread, <code>done</code> when each one ends, and <code>wait</code> to wait until all have ended. A <code>Once</code> runs a function the first time only, however many callers there are:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> sync\n\nsync.<span class=\"t\">WaitGroup</span> group\nsync.<span class=\"t\">Once</span> setup\n<span class=\"t\">Atomic</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>] checked\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">loadConfig</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;config loaded once&quot;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">probe</span>(<span class=\"t\">i64</span> port) {\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> group.<span class=\"f\">done</span>()\n    setup.<span class=\"f\">run</span>(loadConfig)\n    checked.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>)\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> port <span class=\"k\">in</span> [<span class=\"n\">22</span>, <span class=\"n\">80</span>, <span class=\"n\">443</span>] {\n        group.<span class=\"f\">add</span>()\n        <span class=\"k\">spawn</span> <span class=\"f\">probe</span>(port)\n    }\n    t := <span class=\"k\">thread</span> <span class=\"f\">probe</span>(<span class=\"n\">8080</span>)\n    group.<span class=\"f\">add</span>()\n\n    group.<span class=\"f\">wait</span>()\n    t.<span class=\"f\">join</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>checked.<span class=\"f\">load</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> ports checked&quot;</span>)\n    group.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>config loaded once\n4 ports checked</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"limiting-how-many-at-once\"><a href=\"#limiting-how-many-at-once\">Limiting how many at once</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>Semaphore</code> lets a set number of callers in at once. <code>Workers</code> are tasks that take jobs from a queue and hand each one to a function. They suit work that waits, such as requests to other servers, a few at a time:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> sync\n\n<span class=\"t\">Atomic</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>] done\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">fetch</span>(<span class=\"t\">i64</span> id) {\n    <span class=\"f\">sleep</span>(<span class=\"n\">2ms</span>) <span class=\"c\">// a request to a backend</span>\n    done.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>)\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    w := sync.<span class=\"f\">workers</span>(<span class=\"n\">4</span>, fetch)\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> id <span class=\"k\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">0</span>..<span class=\"n\">20</span> {\n        w.<span class=\"f\">submit</span>(id)\n    }\n\n    w.<span class=\"f\">finish</span>() <span class=\"c\">// waits for every job, then stops the tasks</span>\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>done.<span class=\"f\">load</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> fetched&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>20 fetched</code></pre>\n<p>An <code>RwLock</code> lets in many readers at once, or one writer. A waiting writer keeps new readers out, and readers that waited go in before the next writer, so neither side waits forever.</p>\n<p>A <code>WaitGroup</code>, a <code>Once</code> and an <code>RwLock</code> each hold a <code>Mutex</code>, so they can't be copied. Keep them in a global or a struct and pass a pointer.</p>","doc":"Waiting for and limiting other tasks and threads: wait groups,\nsemaphores, run-once, read-write locks and a pool of worker tasks. Each\nworks between tasks of one thread and between threads.","decls":[{"name":"WaitGroup","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct WaitGroup { }","doc":"Waits for a number of tasks or threads to finish: add before starting\neach, done when each ends, and wait until all are. A WaitGroup can't be\ncopied; pass a pointer to it.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":8},{"name":"WaitGroup.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub void WaitGroup.add(i64 n = 1)","doc":"n more to wait for.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":15},{"name":"WaitGroup.done","kind":"method","sig":"pub void WaitGroup.done()","doc":"One of them is finished.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":29},{"name":"WaitGroup.wait","kind":"method","sig":"pub void WaitGroup.wait()","doc":"Waits until the count is 0.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":32},{"name":"WaitGroup.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void WaitGroup.free()","doc":"Gives the WaitGroup's memory back; nothing may be waiting on it.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":54},{"name":"Semaphore","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Semaphore { }","doc":"Lets at most a number of callers in at once; the others wait for a\nrelease.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":60},{"name":"Semaphore.acquire","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Semaphore.acquire()","doc":"Waits for a place, and takes it.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":77},{"name":"Semaphore.release","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Semaphore.release()","doc":"Gives a place back.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":82},{"name":"Semaphore.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Semaphore.free()","doc":"Gives the Semaphore's memory back; nothing may be waiting on it.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":87},{"name":"semaphore","kind":"func","sig":"pub Semaphore semaphore(i64 n)","doc":"A Semaphore that lets n in at once.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":65},{"name":"Once","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Once { }","doc":"Runs a function once, the first time run is called, and makes every\nother caller wait until it has finished. A Once can't be copied.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":94},{"name":"Once.run","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Once.run(void() f)","doc":"Calls f, unless it has been called already.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":100},{"name":"Once.done","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Once.done()","doc":"Whether the function has run.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":113},{"name":"RwLock","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct RwLock { }","doc":"Many readers at once, or one writer. A writer that waits keeps new\nreaders out, and a writer that finishes lets the waiting readers in\nbefore the next writer, so neither waits for ever. An RwLock can't be\ncopied.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":119},{"name":"RwLock.readLock","kind":"method","sig":"pub void RwLock.readLock()","doc":"Waits until no writer has the lock or waits for it, then reads.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":137},{"name":"RwLock.readUnlock","kind":"method","sig":"pub void RwLock.readUnlock()","doc":"One reader is finished.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":140},{"name":"RwLock.writeLock","kind":"method","sig":"pub void RwLock.writeLock()","doc":"Waits until no one reads or writes, then writes.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":147},{"name":"RwLock.writeUnlock","kind":"method","sig":"pub void RwLock.writeUnlock()","doc":"The writer is finished: the waiting readers go first, then the next\nwriter.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":151},{"name":"RwLock.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void RwLock.free()","doc":"Gives the RwLock's memory back: it holds none, so this only marks the end\nof its use.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":159},{"name":"Workers","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Workers[T] { }","doc":"A number of tasks that take jobs from a queue and hand each to one\nfunction: work that waits, such as requests to other servers, done a few\nat a time. The tasks run on the thread that made the Workers.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":170},{"name":"Workers.submit","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Workers[T].submit(T job)","doc":"Hands a job to the next task that is free, waiting while the queue is\nfull.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":202},{"name":"Workers.finish","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Workers[T].finish()","doc":"Waits for every job given so far to be done, then stops the tasks and\ngives the Workers' memory back.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":208},{"name":"workers","kind":"func","sig":"pub Workers[T] workers[T](i64 n, void(T) handle, i64 queue = 64)","doc":"n tasks, each calling handle with the jobs given to submit. queue jobs\ncan wait to be taken before submit waits too.","file":"sync/sync.iris","line":178}]},{"name":"json","title":"JSON","group":"Text and data","summary":"Turning @json structs into JSON text and back.","intro":"<h2 id=\"encoding-and-decoding\"><a href=\"#encoding-and-decoding\">Encoding and decoding</a></h2>\n<p><code>json.encode</code> turns a struct marked <code>@json</code> into JSON, and <code>json.decode</code> reads it back. Field names are used as written. <code>String</code> and <code>str</code> fields become JSON strings, numbers and <code>bool</code> stay as they are, and enums go by their value's name. The <a href=\"../library-tour/01-json.html\">JSON chapter</a> of the Library Tour has every rule. This is the short version:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> json\n\n<span class=\"k\">enum</span> <span class=\"t\">Role</span> { user, admin }\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">User</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> id\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> name\n    <span class=\"t\">Role</span> role\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span>[] tags\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">User</span> u = <span class=\"k\">try</span> json.<span class=\"f\">decode</span>(<span class=\"t\">User</span>, <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;{&quot;id&quot;: 7, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;role&quot;: &quot;admin&quot;, &quot;tags&quot;: [&quot;math&quot;]}&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.id<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.role<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.tags.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = json.<span class=\"f\">encode</span>(u)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(text)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>7 Ada admin 1\n{&quot;id&quot;:7,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Ada&quot;,&quot;role&quot;:&quot;admin&quot;,&quot;tags&quot;:[&quot;math&quot;]}</code></pre>\n<p>The decoded value owns its text and slices. <code>scoped</code> frees them with the <code>free</code> method that <code>@json</code> adds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"arrays\"><a href=\"#arrays\">Arrays</a></h2>\n<p><code>json.decode(Point[], text)</code> reads a JSON array into a slice of structs:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> json\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Point</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> x\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> y\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    ps := <span class=\"k\">try</span> json.<span class=\"f\">decode</span>(<span class=\"t\">Point</span>[], <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;[{&quot;x&quot;: 1, &quot;y&quot;: 2}, {&quot;x&quot;: 3, &quot;y&quot;: 4}]&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> p <span class=\"k\">in</span> ps {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>p.x<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">,</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>p.y<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n    <span class=\"f\">free</span>(ps)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>1,2\n3,4</code></pre>\n<p><code>json.decode(List[Point], text)</code> reads it into a <code>List</code> instead. A <code>List</code> field of a <code>@json</code> struct is read and written as an array the same way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"errors\"><a href=\"#errors\">Errors</a></h2>\n<p>Decoding fails with a <code>JsonError</code>, and the message says where:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> json\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Point</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> x\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> y\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    _ = json.<span class=\"f\">decode</span>(<span class=\"t\">Point</span>, <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;{&quot;x&quot;: &quot;one&quot;}&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.message<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"t\">Point</span>{}\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>JsonError.wrongType: x: expected a number, found a string</code></pre>\n<p><code>json.quote</code> writes one string as JSON, for building JSON text by hand.</p>","doc":"JSON for @json structs. json.encode and json.decode are built into the\ncompiler, which writes a table for each type they see.","decls":[{"name":"JsonError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum JsonError { syntax, wrongType, missing, range }","doc":"What went wrong reading JSON: the text isn't JSON (syntax), a value has\nthe wrong type, a required field is missing, or a number doesn't fit.","file":"json/json.iris","line":6},{"name":"encode","kind":"builtin","sig":"encode","doc":"Turns a value into JSON text: json.encode(user).","file":"json/json.iris","line":10},{"name":"decode","kind":"builtin","sig":"decode","doc":"Reads a value of a type from JSON text: json.decode(User, text).","file":"json/json.iris","line":14},{"name":"quote","kind":"func","sig":"pub String quote(str text)","doc":"text as a JSON string, in quotes, with \", \\ and control characters\nescaped: quote(\"say \\\"hi\\\"\") is \"say \\\"hi\\\"\" with the quotes.","file":"json/json.iris","line":21},{"name":"decodeInto","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void decodeInto(u8 *table, str text, u8 *out)","doc":"What json.decode calls.","file":"json/json.iris","line":37}]},{"name":"csv","title":"CSV","group":"Text and data","summary":"Reading and writing comma-separated values.","intro":"","doc":"Comma-separated values (RFC 4180): fields in double quotes may hold the\nseparator, quotes (written twice) and line breaks.","decls":[{"name":"CsvError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum CsvError { end, quote }","doc":"What reading fails with: end when there are no more records, quote for a\nquoted field with no closing quote.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":6},{"name":"Record","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Record {\n    pub List[String] fields\n}","doc":"One row: its fields, owned by the record.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":9},{"name":"Record.len","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Record.len()","doc":"How many fields the record has.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":14},{"name":"Record.at","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Record.at(i64 i)","doc":"Field i, or \"\" past the end.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":17},{"name":"Record.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Record.free()","doc":"Frees the fields and the list that holds them.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":20},{"name":"Reader","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Reader {\n    pub str sep\n    pub i64 line\n}","doc":"Reads records from text, one at a time.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":25},{"name":"Reader.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Record Reader.next()","doc":"The next record. At the end it fails with CsvError.end.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":38},{"name":"reader","kind":"func","sig":"pub Reader reader(str text)","doc":"A Reader at the start of text, with \",\" as the separator.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":35},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[Record] parse(str text)","doc":"Every record in text.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":101},{"name":"quote","kind":"func","sig":"pub String quote(str field, str sep = \",\")","doc":"field, quoted when it holds the separator, a quote or a line break.","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":117},{"name":"row","kind":"func","sig":"pub String row(str[] fields, str sep = \",\")","doc":"One line of CSV for fields, ending in \"\\n\".","file":"csv/csv.iris","line":142}]},{"name":"toml","title":"TOML","group":"Text and data","summary":"TOML files into @json structs or a Value, and written back.","intro":"<h2 id=\"into-a-struct\"><a href=\"#into-a-struct\">Into a struct</a></h2>\n<p><code>toml.decode[T](text)</code> reads TOML into a <code>@json</code> struct, the way <code>json.decode</code> reads JSON (see <a href=\"../library-tour/01-json.html\">JSON</a>). Tables are structs, arrays are slices or <code>List</code>s, and keys the struct doesn't have are skipped. A date or a time goes into a <code>String</code> as written:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> toml\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Backend</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> addr\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> weight = <span class=\"n\">1</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Config</span> {\n    <span class=\"a\">@json</span>(required) <span class=\"t\">String</span> name\n    <span class=\"t\">u16</span> port = <span class=\"n\">80</span>\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> started\n    <span class=\"t\">List</span>[<span class=\"t\">Backend</span>] backends\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    text := <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;\n        name = &quot;edge&quot;\n        port = 8443\n        started = 2026-10-23T09:00:00Z\n\n        [[backends]]\n        addr = &quot;10.0.0.11:8080&quot;\n        weight = 3\n\n        [[backends]]\n        addr = &quot;10.0.0.12:8080&quot;\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">Config</span> c = <span class=\"k\">try</span> toml.decode[<span class=\"t\">Config</span>](text)\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>c.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> on </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>c.port<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, since </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>c.started<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> b <span class=\"k\">in</span> c.backends {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b.addr<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> weight </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b.weight<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n\n    _ = toml.decode[<span class=\"t\">Config</span>](<span class=\"s\">&quot;port = </span><span class=\"e\">\\&quot;</span><span class=\"s\">high</span><span class=\"e\">\\&quot;</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.message<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"t\">Config</span>{}\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>edge on 8443, since 2026-10-23T09:00:00Z\n10.0.0.11:8080 weight 3\n10.0.0.12:8080 weight 1\nTomlError.wrongType: port: expected a number, found a string</code></pre>\n<p><code>toml.encode(v)</code> writes a struct back as TOML. Plain values come first, then each table under a <code>[header]</code>, and a <code>List</code> of structs as <code>[[tables]]</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"any-document\"><a href=\"#any-document\">Any document</a></h2>\n<p><code>toml.parse(text)</code> reads any TOML document into a <code>toml.Value</code> that is a table. <code>get</code> gives the value of a key, or <code>null</code>. <code>at</code> gives the value at a place in an array, and <code>len</code> the number of values. A value's <code>kind</code> says what it holds, and its data is in <code>text</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>float</code> or <code>boolean</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> toml\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    text := <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;\n        title = &quot;Shop&quot;\n        ports = [8000, 8001]\n\n        [database]\n        host = &quot;db.lan&quot;\n        pool = { min = 2, max = 16 }\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> toml.<span class=\"t\">Value</span> doc = <span class=\"k\">try</span> toml.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(text)\n\n    db := doc.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;database&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>doc.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;title&quot;</span>).text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>db.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;host&quot;</span>).text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, pool </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>db.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;pool&quot;</span>).<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;max&quot;</span>).int<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    ports := doc.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;ports&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ports.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> ports, the first </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ports.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>).int<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, a </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ports.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>).kind<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;no user: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>db.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;user&quot;</span>) == <span class=\"k\">null</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> i <span class=\"k\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">0</span>..db.len {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>db.keys.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(i)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> is a </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>db.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(i).kind<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>Shop: db.lan, pool 16\n2 ports, the first 8000, a integer\nno user: true\nhost is a string\npool is a table</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"writing\"><a href=\"#writing\">Writing</a></h2>\n<p><code>toml.table()</code>, <code>toml.array()</code>, <code>toml.string(s)</code>, <code>toml.integer(n)</code>, <code>toml.float(f)</code>, <code>toml.boolean(b)</code> and <code>toml.datetime(s)</code> make values. <code>set</code> puts a value in a table and <code>push</code> in an array, which then own it. <code>toml.write(doc)</code> gives the text:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> toml\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> toml.<span class=\"t\">Value</span> doc = toml.<span class=\"f\">table</span>()\n    doc.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</span>, toml.<span class=\"f\">string</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;edge&quot;</span>))\n\n    tls := toml.<span class=\"f\">table</span>()\n    tls.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;cert&quot;</span>, toml.<span class=\"f\">string</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/etc/edge/cert.pem&quot;</span>))\n    tls.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;ratio&quot;</span>, toml.<span class=\"f\">float</span>(<span class=\"n\">1.0</span>))\n    doc.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;tls&quot;</span>, tls)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = toml.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(doc)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(text)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>name = &quot;edge&quot;\n\n[tls]\ncert = &quot;/etc/edge/cert.pem&quot;\nratio = 1.0\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"errors\"><a href=\"#errors\">Errors</a></h2>\n<p>Text that isn't TOML fails with <code>TomlError.syntax</code>, and the message gives the line. As TOML 1.0 requires, a key given twice, a table defined twice and a key added to an inline table are errors too. Filling a struct can also fail with <code>wrongType</code>, <code>missing</code> and <code>range</code>, as <code>json.decode</code> does.</p>","doc":"TOML 1.0: configuration files read into @json structs, as json.decode\nreads JSON, or into a Value that holds any document; and written back.\nDates and times are kept as the text they were written in.\n\n    scoped Config c = try toml.decode[Config](text)\n    scoped toml.Value doc = try toml.parse(text)","decls":[{"name":"TomlError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum TomlError { syntax, wrongType, missing, range }","doc":"What reading TOML fails with: syntax when the text isn't TOML, and when\nfilling a struct, wrongType, missing and range as json.decode has them.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":12},{"name":"Kind","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Kind { string, integer, float, boolean, datetime, array, table }","doc":"What a Value holds.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":15},{"name":"Value","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Value {\n    pub Kind kind\n    pub bool boolean\n    pub String text\n    pub i64 int\n    pub f64 float\n    pub List[Value] items\n    pub List[String] keys\n}","doc":"A TOML value. A table keeps its keys in the order they were written,\neach with its value at the same place in items.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":19},{"name":"Value.get","kind":"method","sig":"pub Value *Value.get(str key)","doc":"The value of key in a table, or null when it has none.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":71},{"name":"Value.at","kind":"method","sig":"pub Value *Value.at(i64 i)","doc":"Value i of an array or a table, or null past the end.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":102},{"name":"Value.len","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Value.len()","doc":"How many values an array or a table has.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":110},{"name":"Value.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Value.set(str key, Value v)","doc":"Sets key in a table to v, which the table owns after.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":113},{"name":"Value.push","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Value.push(Value v)","doc":"Adds v at the end of an array, which owns it after.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":125},{"name":"Value.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Value.free()","doc":"Frees the value and everything in it.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":128},{"name":"string","kind":"func","sig":"pub Value string(str s)","doc":"A string.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":50},{"name":"integer","kind":"func","sig":"pub Value integer(i64 n)","doc":"An integer.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":53},{"name":"float","kind":"func","sig":"pub Value float(f64 f)","doc":"A floating point number.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":56},{"name":"boolean","kind":"func","sig":"pub Value boolean(bool b)","doc":"true or false.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":59},{"name":"datetime","kind":"func","sig":"pub Value datetime(str s)","doc":"A date, a time or both, as TOML writes them: 1979-05-27T07:32:00Z.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":62},{"name":"array","kind":"func","sig":"pub Value array()","doc":"An empty array.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":65},{"name":"table","kind":"func","sig":"pub Value table()","doc":"An empty table.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":68},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Value parse(str text)","doc":"Reads a TOML document into a table.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":877},{"name":"write","kind":"func","sig":"pub String write(Value doc)","doc":"TOML text for a table.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":1051},{"name":"decode","kind":"func","sig":"pub !T decode[T](str text)","doc":"Reads a @json struct from TOML text: toml.decode[Config](text). Keys the\nstruct doesn't have are skipped, and fields whose keys are missing keep\ntheir defaults. Dates and times go into String fields as text.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":1105},{"name":"encode","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String encode[T](T v)","doc":"TOML text for a @json struct.","file":"toml/toml.iris","line":1255}]},{"name":"ini","title":"INI","group":"Text and data","summary":"INI files: sections, keys and comments, read and written.","intro":"<h2 id=\"reading\"><a href=\"#reading\">Reading</a></h2>\n<p><code>ini.parse(text)</code> reads INI text into an <code>ini.File</code>, which holds its sections in order, each with its keys and values. <code>get(section, key)</code> gives a value, or <code>&quot;&quot;</code> when it isn't there. <code>lookup</code> also says whether it is there.</p>\n<ul><li>Keys before the first <code>[section]</code> are in the section <code>&quot;&quot;</code>.</li><li>Lines that start with <code>;</code> or <code>#</code> are comments, and so is the rest of a line after <code> ;</code> or <code> #</code>.</li><li>A value may be in quotes, which keep its spaces and comment characters.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> ini\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    text := <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;\n        ; the shop&#x27;s settings\n        name = shop\n\n        [database]\n        host = db.lan\n        port: 5432\n        user = &quot;ada lovelace&quot;   ; who logs in\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> ini.<span class=\"t\">File</span> f = <span class=\"k\">try</span> ini.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(text)\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>f.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>f.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;database&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;host&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">:</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>f.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;database&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;port&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> as [</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>f.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;database&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;user&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">]&quot;</span>)\n    _, found := f.<span class=\"f\">lookup</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;database&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;password&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;password set: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>found<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> s <span class=\"k\">in</span> f.sections {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;[</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>s.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">] </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>s.keys.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> keys&quot;</span>)\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>shop: db.lan:5432 as [ada lovelace]\npassword set: false\n[] 1 keys\n[database] 3 keys</code></pre>\n<p>A key given twice keeps the last value. A line that is not a section, a <code>key = value</code>, a <code>key: value</code> or a comment fails with <code>IniError.syntax</code> and its line number.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing\"><a href=\"#writing\">Writing</a></h2>\n<p><code>set(section, key, value)</code> sets a value and adds the section or the key when missing. <code>remove</code> takes a key out. <code>write</code> gives the file as text, with a blank line before each section and without the comments:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> ini\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> ini.<span class=\"t\">File</span> f = <span class=\"k\">try</span> ini.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;[database]</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">host = db.lan</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">port = 5432</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    f.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;database&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;port&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;6543&quot;</span>)\n    f.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;cache&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;size&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;64MiB&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = f.<span class=\"f\">write</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(text)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>[database]\nhost = db.lan\nport = 6543\n\n[cache]\nsize = 64MiB\n</code></pre>","doc":"INI files: [sections] of key = value lines, with ; and # comments.\n\n    scoped ini.File f = try ini.parse(text)\n    host := f.get(\"database\", \"host\")","decls":[{"name":"IniError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum IniError { syntax }","doc":"What reading fails with: syntax for a line that is neither a section, a\nkey = value nor a comment.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":10},{"name":"Section","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Section {\n    pub String name\n    pub List[String] keys\n    pub List[String] values\n}","doc":"One [section] and its keys, in the order they were written. The keys\nbefore the first [section] are in one named \"\".","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":14},{"name":"Section.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Section.free()","doc":"Frees the section's text.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":21},{"name":"Section.lookup","kind":"method","sig":"pub (str, bool) Section.lookup(str key)","doc":"The value of key, and whether the section has it.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":28},{"name":"Section.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Section.set(str key, str value)","doc":"Sets key to value, adding it when the section doesn't have it.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":38},{"name":"Section.remove","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Section.remove(str key)","doc":"Takes key out; false when it wasn't there.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":51},{"name":"File","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct File {\n    pub List[Section] sections\n}","doc":"An INI file: its sections, in order. The first is always the one named \"\"\nfor the keys before any [section].","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":66},{"name":"File.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void File.free()","doc":"Frees the sections.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":71},{"name":"File.section","kind":"method","sig":"pub Section *File.section(str name)","doc":"The section called name, or null.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":74},{"name":"File.lookup","kind":"method","sig":"pub (str, bool) File.lookup(str section, str key)","doc":"The value of key in section, and whether it is there.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":94},{"name":"File.get","kind":"method","sig":"pub str File.get(str section, str key)","doc":"The value of key in section, or \"\" when it isn't there.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":103},{"name":"File.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void File.set(str section, str key, str value)","doc":"Sets key in section to value, adding the section or the key as needed.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":109},{"name":"File.remove","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool File.remove(str section, str key)","doc":"Takes key out of section; false when it wasn't there.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":112},{"name":"File.write","kind":"method","sig":"pub String File.write()","doc":"The file as INI text, a blank line before each section.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":190},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !File parse(str text)","doc":"Reads INI text. A key given twice keeps the last value.","file":"ini/ini.iris","line":142}]},{"name":"xml","title":"XML","group":"Text and data","summary":"XML read as a stream of events or as a tree of elements, and written.","intro":"<h2 id=\"a-tree\"><a href=\"#a-tree\">A tree</a></h2>\n<p><code>xml.parse(text)</code> reads a document into its root <code>xml.Element</code>. An element has a <code>name</code>, its <code>attrs</code>, its <code>children</code> and its <code>text</code>, which is the text directly inside it. <code>child(name)</code> gives the first child element with that name, or <code>null</code>. <code>attr(name)</code> gives an attribute's value, or <code>&quot;&quot;</code> when it is missing. Text made only of spaces and line breaks is left out, and entities such as <code>&amp;amp;</code> are decoded:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> xml\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    text := <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;\n        &lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot;?&gt;\n        &lt;catalog&gt;\n          &lt;book id=&quot;bk101&quot; lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n            &lt;title&gt;XML &amp;amp; you&lt;/title&gt;\n            &lt;price&gt;44.95&lt;/price&gt;\n          &lt;/book&gt;\n          &lt;book id=&quot;bk102&quot;&gt;\n            &lt;title&gt;Midnight Rain&lt;/title&gt;\n            &lt;price&gt;5.95&lt;/price&gt;\n          &lt;/book&gt;\n        &lt;/catalog&gt;\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> xml.<span class=\"t\">Element</span> root = <span class=\"k\">try</span> xml.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(text)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> b <span class=\"k\">in</span> root.children {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b.<span class=\"f\">attr</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;id&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b.<span class=\"f\">child</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;title&quot;</span>).text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b.<span class=\"f\">child</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;price&quot;</span>).text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> [</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b.<span class=\"f\">attr</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;lang&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">]&quot;</span>)\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>bk101: XML &amp; you, 44.95 [en]\nbk102: Midnight Rain, 5.95 []</code></pre>\n<p>A text child has no name, and <code>isText()</code> tells it apart from an element. <code>allText()</code> joins all the text inside an element and its descendants. Comments, processing instructions and the <code>DOCTYPE</code> line are skipped. DTDs are not read. A name with a prefix, like <code>atom:id</code>, is kept as it is.</p>\n<h2 id=\"events\"><a href=\"#events\">Events</a></h2>\n<p><code>xml.reader(text)</code> reads a document a step at a time, which suits large ones. Each <code>next</code> gives an <code>xml.Event</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>.start</code>, with its <code>name</code> and <code>attrs</code>.</li><li><code>.text</code>, with its <code>text</code>.</li><li><code>.end</code>, with its <code>name</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>An empty tag such as <code>&lt;br/&gt;</code> gives a start and an end. At the end of the document <code>next</code> fails with <code>XmlError.end</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> xml\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    rd := xml.<span class=\"f\">reader</span>(<span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;&lt;log&gt;&lt;line level=&quot;warn&quot;&gt;disk at 91%&lt;/line&gt;&lt;line level=&quot;info&quot;&gt;ok&lt;/line&gt;&lt;sync/&gt;&lt;/log&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> {\n        ev := rd.<span class=\"f\">next</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n            <span class=\"k\">if</span> e.code != xml.<span class=\"t\">XmlError</span>.end {\n                <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.message<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n            }\n            <span class=\"k\">break</span>\n        }\n\n        <span class=\"k\">match</span> ev.kind {\n            .start =&gt; <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;start </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ev.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> [</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ev.<span class=\"f\">attr</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;level&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">]&quot;</span>)\n            .text =&gt; <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;  </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ev.text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n            .end =&gt; <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;end </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ev.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        }\n        ev.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>start log []\nstart line [warn]\n  disk at 91%\nend line\nstart line [info]\n  ok\nend line\nstart sync []\nend sync\nend log</code></pre>\n<p>Text that isn't well-formed fails with <code>XmlError.syntax</code> and its line: a tag closed by the wrong end tag, a tag never closed, an attribute without quotes, an entity XML doesn't define, or a second root element.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing\"><a href=\"#writing\">Writing</a></h2>\n<p><code>xml.element(name)</code> makes an element. <code>set</code> gives it an attribute, <code>add</code> a child (which the element then owns) and <code>addText</code> text. <code>write</code> gives the element as XML on one line, with <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&quot;</code> and <code>&#x27;</code> written as entities. <code>xml.escape(text)</code> does the same to any text:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> xml\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> xml.<span class=\"t\">Element</span> order = xml.<span class=\"f\">element</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;order&quot;</span>)\n    order.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;7&quot;</span>)\n\n    item := xml.<span class=\"f\">element</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</span>)\n    item.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;qty&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;2&quot;</span>)\n    item.<span class=\"f\">addText</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;tea &amp; &lt;cake&gt;&quot;</span>)\n    order.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(item)\n    order.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(xml.<span class=\"f\">element</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;gift&quot;</span>))\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = order.<span class=\"f\">write</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(text)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>&lt;order id=&quot;7&quot;&gt;&lt;item qty=&quot;2&quot;&gt;tea &amp;amp; &amp;lt;cake&amp;gt;&lt;/item&gt;&lt;gift/&gt;&lt;/order&gt;</code></pre>","doc":"XML: reading it as a stream of events (a start tag with its attributes,\ntext, an end tag) or as a tree of Elements, and writing it. Comments,\nprocessing instructions and the DOCTYPE line are skipped; DTDs are not\nread, and a name with a prefix, like atom:id, is kept as it is.\n\n    scoped xml.Element root = try xml.parse(text)\n    title := root.child(\"title\").text","decls":[{"name":"XmlError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum XmlError { syntax, end }","doc":"What reading fails with: syntax for text that isn't well-formed XML, end\nwhen a Reader has no more events.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":13},{"name":"Kind","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Kind { start, end, text }","doc":"What an Event is.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":16},{"name":"Attr","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Attr {\n    pub String name\n    pub String value\n}","doc":"An attribute: name=\"value\", with the value's entities decoded.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":19},{"name":"Attr.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Attr.free()","doc":"Frees the attribute's text.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":25},{"name":"Event","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Event {\n    pub Kind kind\n    pub str name\n    pub List[Attr] attrs\n    pub String text\n}","doc":"One step through the document: a start tag with its attributes, an end\ntag, or text (with its entities decoded, and CDATA as it is). An empty\ntag, <br/>, gives a start and an end.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":33},{"name":"Event.attr","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Event.attr(str name)","doc":"The value of an attribute of a start tag, or \"\" when it has none.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":42},{"name":"Event.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Event.free()","doc":"Frees the event's text and attributes.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":45},{"name":"Reader","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Reader {\n    pub bool keepSpace\n}","doc":"Reads events from text, one at a time.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":60},{"name":"Reader.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Reader.free()","doc":"Frees what the reader keeps; it does this itself when it fails or ends.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":76},{"name":"Reader.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Event Reader.next()","doc":"The next event. At the end of the document it fails with XmlError.end.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":216},{"name":"reader","kind":"func","sig":"pub Reader reader(str text)","doc":"A Reader at the start of text.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":73},{"name":"Element","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Element {\n    pub String name\n    pub String text\n    pub List[Attr] attrs\n    pub List[Element] children\n}","doc":"An element: its name, attributes and children. A text child has no name\nand its text in text; an element's own text is the text directly inside\nit, joined.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":413},{"name":"Element.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Element.free()","doc":"Frees the element and everything in it.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":424},{"name":"Element.isText","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Element.isText()","doc":"Whether this is text rather than an element.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":432},{"name":"Element.attr","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Element.attr(str name)","doc":"The value of an attribute, or \"\" when it has none.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":435},{"name":"Element.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Element.set(str name, str value)","doc":"Sets an attribute, adding it when the element doesn't have it.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":438},{"name":"Element.child","kind":"method","sig":"pub Element *Element.child(str name)","doc":"The first child element called name, or null.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":451},{"name":"Element.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Element.add(Element child)","doc":"Adds a child element, which the element owns after.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":462},{"name":"Element.addText","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Element.addText(str text)","doc":"Adds text at the end of the element.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":465},{"name":"Element.allText","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Element.allText()","doc":"All the text inside the element and the elements in it, in order.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":471},{"name":"Element.write","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Element.write()","doc":"The element as XML text, on one line; one with no children is written\n<name/>.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":553},{"name":"element","kind":"func","sig":"pub Element element(str name)","doc":"A new element with no attributes or children.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":421},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Element parse(str text)","doc":"Reads a document into its root element. Text that is only spaces and\nline breaks is left out.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":489},{"name":"escape","kind":"func","sig":"pub String escape(str text)","doc":"text with &, <, >, \" and ' written as entities, for text or an\nattribute's value.","file":"xml/xml.iris","line":525}]},{"name":"regex","title":"Regular expressions","group":"Text and data","summary":"Patterns in RE2's syntax, matched in time linear in the text.","intro":"<h2 id=\"matching-and-finding\"><a href=\"#matching-and-finding\">Matching and finding</a></h2>\n<p><code>regex.compile</code> turns a pattern into a <code>Regex</code>, or fails with <code>RegexError.syntax</code>. A raw string (<code>r&quot;...&quot;</code>) keeps backslashes as they are, which makes patterns easier to read. <code>matches</code> says whether the text has a match anywhere. <code>find</code> gives the first match:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> regex\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    re := <span class=\"k\">try</span> regex.<span class=\"f\">compile</span>(<span class=\"s\">r&quot;(\\w+)@(\\w+)\\.com&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> re.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>re.<span class=\"f\">matches</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;write to ada@example.com&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>re.<span class=\"f\">matches</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;no mail&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    m, found := re.<span class=\"f\">find</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;write to ada@example.com today&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> found {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.<span class=\"t\">str</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> at </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.start<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">..</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.end<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: user </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.<span class=\"f\">group</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, host </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.<span class=\"f\">group</span>(<span class=\"n\">2</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n    m.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>true false\nada@example.com at 9..24: user ada, host example</code></pre>\n<p>A <code>Match</code> gives slices of the searched text, so the text must stay alive while the match is used.</p>\n<h2 id=\"every-match\"><a href=\"#every-match\">Every match</a></h2>\n<p><code>findAll</code> gives every match, left to right, and <code>split</code> gives the text between them. A limit stops after that many:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> regex\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    re := regex.<span class=\"f\">must</span>(<span class=\"s\">r&quot;\\d+&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> re.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    text := <span class=\"s\">&quot;3 apples, 12 pears and 7 figs&quot;</span>\n    ms := re.<span class=\"f\">findAll</span>(text)\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> m <span class=\"k\">in</span> ms {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(m.<span class=\"t\">str</span>())\n        m.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n    ms.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    parts := regex.<span class=\"f\">must</span>(<span class=\"s\">r&quot;,\\s*&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> parts.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">List</span>[<span class=\"t\">str</span>] words = parts.<span class=\"f\">split</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;red, green,blue&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>words.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>words.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>words.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>words.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">2</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>3\n12\n7\n3: red green blue</code></pre>\n<p><code>regex.must</code> is <code>compile</code> for a pattern written in the program. An invalid pattern there is a mistake in the program, so <code>must</code> panics instead of giving an error.</p>\n<h2 id=\"replacing\"><a href=\"#replacing\">Replacing</a></h2>\n<p><code>replace</code> gives new text with each match replaced. In the replacement, <code>$1</code> is group 1, <code>${name}</code> is a named group and <code>$$</code> is a <code>$</code>. Write a replacement with <code>${name}</code> as a raw string, so its braces aren't read as values:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> regex\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    re := regex.<span class=\"f\">must</span>(<span class=\"s\">r&quot;(?P&lt;year&gt;\\d{4})-(?P&lt;month&gt;\\d\\d)-(?P&lt;day&gt;\\d\\d)&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> re.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> eu = re.<span class=\"f\">replace</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;from 2026-10-17 to 2026-12-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">r&quot;${day}/${month}/${year}&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(eu)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>re.<span class=\"f\">groups</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> groups, month is group </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>re.<span class=\"f\">index</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;month&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>from 17/10/2026 to 01/12/2026\n3 groups, month is group 2</code></pre>","doc":"Regular expressions, in the syntax of RE2 and Go. Matching takes time in\nproportion to the length of the text, whatever the pattern: there is no\nbacktracking, so a pattern from a user can't make a search take forever.\n\n    re := try regex.compile(r\"(\\w+)@(\\w+)\\.com\")\n    defer re.free()\n    m, found := re.find(\"mail ada@example.com\")\n    if found {\n        print(m.group(1))       // ada\n    }\n    m.free()\n\nThe syntax:\n\n    x        a character; \\. \\* \\\\ and so on for the special ones\n    .        any character but a newline ((?s) lets it match one too)\n    [abc]    one of the characters; [a-z] a range; [^abc] any other\n    \\d \\w \\s a digit, a word character [0-9A-Za-z_], a space; \\D \\W \\S\n             anything else\n    ^ $      the start and the end of the text ((?m): of a line too)\n    \\A \\z    the start and the end of the text only\n    \\b \\B    a word boundary, and not one\n    xy x|y   x then y; x or y\n    x* x+ x? none or more, one or more, none or one\n    x{n} x{n,} x{n,m}   n times, n or more, from n to m\n    x*? x+? x?? x{n,m}? the same, taking as few as it can\n    (x)      a group, which is kept: group(1)\n    (?P<name>x) (?<name>x)  a group with a name: group(re.index(\"name\"))\n    (?:x)    a group that isn't kept\n    (?i) (?m) (?s)  for the rest of the group: letters match in either\n             case, ^ and $ at lines, . matches a newline; (?i:x) for x only\n\nCharacters are UTF-8; positions are byte offsets. Ignoring case works for\nASCII letters.","decls":[{"name":"RegexError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum RegexError { syntax, tooLarge }","doc":"What compile fails with: syntax for a pattern that isn't valid, tooLarge for\none that makes too big a program.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":40},{"name":"Regex","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Regex { }","doc":"A compiled pattern.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":78},{"name":"Regex.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Regex.free()","doc":"Frees the compiled pattern.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":89},{"name":"Regex.groups","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Regex.groups()","doc":"How many groups the pattern has, not counting the whole match.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":98},{"name":"Regex.index","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Regex.index(str name)","doc":"The number of the group called name, or -1.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":101},{"name":"Regex.matches","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Regex.matches(str text)","doc":"Whether the pattern matches somewhere in text.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1137},{"name":"Regex.find","kind":"method","sig":"pub (Match, bool) Regex.find(str text)","doc":"The first match in text, with its groups.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1143},{"name":"Regex.findAll","kind":"method","sig":"pub List[Match] Regex.findAll(str text, i64 limit = -1)","doc":"Every match in text, one after the other without overlapping; at most\nlimit of them when limit isn't below 0. As in Go, an empty match right\nafter another match doesn't count.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1159},{"name":"Regex.replace","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Regex.replace(str text, str with, i64 limit = -1)","doc":"text with every match replaced by with, where $1 or ${1} is group 1,\n${name} a named group, and $$ a $.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1198},{"name":"Regex.split","kind":"method","sig":"pub List[str] Regex.split(str text, i64 limit = -1)","doc":"The parts of text between the matches; at most limit parts when limit\nisn't below 0.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1260},{"name":"compile","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Regex compile(str pattern)","doc":"Compiles a pattern. A pattern that isn't valid fails with\nRegexError.syntax, and the message says what and where.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":841},{"name":"must","kind":"func","sig":"pub Regex must(str pattern)","doc":"compile, for a pattern known to be valid: a bad one stops the program.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":872},{"name":"Match","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Match {\n    pub i64 start\n    pub i64 end\n}","doc":"A match: where it is, and where its groups are.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1103},{"name":"Match.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Match.free()","doc":"Frees the positions of the groups.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1111},{"name":"Match.str","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Match.str()","doc":"The text of the whole match.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1116},{"name":"Match.group","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Match.group(i64 i)","doc":"The text of group i (0 is the whole match); \"\" when it didn't take part.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1119},{"name":"Match.span","kind":"method","sig":"pub (i64, i64) Match.span(i64 i)","doc":"Where group i is: its start and end, or -1, -1.","file":"regex/regex.iris","line":1129}]},{"name":"base64","title":"Base64","group":"Text and data","summary":"Base64, the standard and the URL-safe alphabet.","intro":"","doc":"Base64: bytes as text, standard (RFC 4648) and URL-safe (for URLs and\nJSON Web Tokens).","decls":[{"name":"Base64Error","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Base64Error { invalid }","doc":"What decoding fails with: invalid for text that isn't Base64.","file":"base64/base64.iris","line":5},{"name":"encode","kind":"func","sig":"pub String encode(str data)","doc":"The bytes of data as standard base64, with = at the end.","file":"base64/base64.iris","line":99},{"name":"decode","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String decode(str text)","doc":"The bytes of standard base64 text; = at the end and line breaks may be\nleft out.","file":"base64/base64.iris","line":103},{"name":"encodeUrl","kind":"func","sig":"pub String encodeUrl(str data)","doc":"The bytes of data as URL-safe base64 (- and _), without =.","file":"base64/base64.iris","line":106},{"name":"decodeUrl","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String decodeUrl(str text)","doc":"The bytes of URL-safe base64 text.","file":"base64/base64.iris","line":109}]},{"name":"hex","title":"Hex","group":"Text and data","summary":"Bytes as hexadecimal digits, and back.","intro":"","doc":"Hexadecimal: each byte as two digits, 0-9 and a-f.","decls":[{"name":"HexError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum HexError { invalid }","doc":"What decode fails with: invalid for text that isn't hex digits in pairs.","file":"hex/hex.iris","line":4},{"name":"encode","kind":"func","sig":"pub String encode(str data)","doc":"The bytes of data as hex: \"hi\" is \"6869\".","file":"hex/hex.iris","line":9},{"name":"decode","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String decode(str text)","doc":"The bytes of hex text, in either case.","file":"hex/hex.iris","line":33}]},{"name":"url","title":"URLs","group":"Text and data","summary":"Taking URLs apart, and the %XX encoding of their parts.","intro":"","doc":"URLs: taking them apart, and the %XX encoding of their parts.","decls":[{"name":"UrlError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum UrlError { invalid }","doc":"What parse and decode fail with: invalid for text that isn't a URL, or a %\nthat isn't followed by two hex digits.","file":"url/url.iris","line":5},{"name":"Url","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Url {\n    pub str scheme\n    pub str user\n    pub str host\n    pub i64 port\n    pub str path\n    pub str query\n    pub str fragment\n}","doc":"The parts of a URL, as views into the text it was read from:\nhttps://ada:pw@example.com:8443/docs/a?q=1#top has scheme \"https\", user\n\"ada:pw\", host \"example.com\", port 8443, path \"/docs/a\", query \"q=1\" and\nfragment \"top\".","file":"url/url.iris","line":11},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Url parse(str text)","doc":"Takes a URL apart. port is the scheme's usual one when none is written.","file":"url/url.iris","line":24},{"name":"encode","kind":"func","sig":"pub String encode(str text)","doc":"text with every character but letters, digits and -_.~ as %XX, for a\nquery value or a part of a path.","file":"url/url.iris","line":103},{"name":"decode","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String decode(str text)","doc":"The text with %XX turned back into bytes and + into spaces, as query\nvalues are sent.","file":"url/url.iris","line":134},{"name":"decodePath","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String decodePath(str text)","doc":"The text with %XX turned back into bytes, for a part of a path, where +\nis a +.","file":"url/url.iris","line":138}]},{"name":"compress","title":"Compression","group":"Text and data","summary":"Deflate in the gzip, zlib and raw formats.","intro":"<h2 id=\"whole-data\"><a href=\"#whole-data\">Whole data</a></h2>\n<p><code>gzip</code> compresses text or bytes in the gzip format, and <code>gunzip</code> gives them back. <code>compress</code> and <code>decompress</code> take the format as an argument: <code>.gzip</code>, <code>.zlib</code> or <code>.raw</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> compress, <span class=\"t\">str</span>\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = <span class=\"t\">str</span>.<span class=\"f\">repeat</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;the same words again and again. &quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">100</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> packed = <span class=\"k\">try</span> compress.<span class=\"f\">gzip</span>(text, level: <span class=\"n\">9</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> back = <span class=\"k\">try</span> compress.<span class=\"f\">gunzip</span>(packed)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>text.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes, packed under 100: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>packed.len &lt; <span class=\"n\">100</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, back the same: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>back == text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> z = <span class=\"k\">try</span> compress.<span class=\"f\">compress</span>(text, .zlib)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;zlib starts with </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>z[<span class=\"n\">0</span>]<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>3200 bytes, packed under 100: true, back the same: true\nzlib starts with 120</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"a-limit-for-untrusted-data\"><a href=\"#a-limit-for-untrusted-data\">A limit for untrusted data</a></h2>\n<p>A small input can decompress into a huge output. <code>gunzip</code> and <code>decompress</code> stop with <code>CompressError.tooLarge</code> when the output would pass a limit, which is 1 GiB unless given:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> compress, <span class=\"t\">str</span>\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> zeros = <span class=\"t\">str</span>.<span class=\"f\">repeat</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">1000000</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> packed = <span class=\"k\">try</span> compress.<span class=\"f\">gzip</span>(zeros)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> big = compress.<span class=\"f\">gunzip</span>(packed, limit: <span class=\"n\">4096</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;over the limit: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>big.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>over the limit: CompressError.tooLarge</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"a-part-at-a-time\"><a href=\"#a-part-at-a-time\">A part at a time</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>Writer</code> compresses data as it comes and adds the result to its <code>out</code>. <code>finish</code> ends the stream. A <code>Reader</code> does the opposite, and its <code>write</code> gives <code>true</code> when the stream has ended:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> compress\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> w := <span class=\"k\">try</span> compress.<span class=\"f\">writer</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> i <span class=\"k\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">0</span>..<span class=\"n\">3</span> {\n        <span class=\"k\">try</span> w.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;line </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>i<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> w.<span class=\"f\">finish</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> r := <span class=\"k\">try</span> compress.<span class=\"f\">reader</span>()\n    done := <span class=\"k\">try</span> r.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(w.out)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;ended </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>done<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.out<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>ended true: line 0\nline 1\nline 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"checksums\"><a href=\"#checksums\">Checksums</a></h2>\n<p><code>crc32</code> and <code>adler32</code> give the checksums that gzip and zlib use. Pass the previous checksum to continue from it, so a big input can be checked a part at a time:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> compress\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    whole := compress.<span class=\"f\">crc32</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;hello world&quot;</span>)\n    parts := compress.<span class=\"f\">crc32</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot; world&quot;</span>, compress.<span class=\"f\">crc32</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;hello&quot;</span>))\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>whole<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>whole == parts<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>222957957 true</code></pre>\n<p>zlib is loaded the first time it is needed. <code>IRIS_ZLIB</code> names another copy of it.</p>","doc":"Compression: deflate in the gzip, zlib and raw formats, with zlib, which\nis loaded the first time it is needed.\n\n    scoped String packed = try compress.gzip(text)\n    scoped String back = try compress.gunzip(packed)","decls":[{"name":"CompressError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum CompressError { broken, tooLarge, missing }","doc":"What compression fails with: broken for data that isn't valid, tooLarge when\nthe output would pass the limit, missing when zlib can't be loaded.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":9},{"name":"Format","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Format { gzip, zlib, raw }","doc":"The wrapping around deflate data: gzip (files, HTTP), zlib (PNG, some\nprotocols), or none.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":13},{"name":"compress","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String compress(str data, Format format = .gzip, i64 level = 6)","doc":"Compresses data, at a level from 1 (fastest) to 9 (smallest).","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":45},{"name":"decompress","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String decompress(str data, Format format = .gzip, i64 limit = 0)","doc":"Decompresses data. It fails when what comes out would be larger than\nlimit bytes (0: 1 GiB), so a small file can't fill the memory.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":57},{"name":"gzip","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String gzip(str data, i64 level = 6)","doc":"Compresses data in the gzip format, at a level from 1 (fastest) to 9\n(smallest).","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":71},{"name":"gunzip","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String gunzip(str data, i64 limit = 0)","doc":"Decompresses gzip data; zlib data works too.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":74},{"name":"crc32","kind":"func","sig":"pub u32 crc32(str data, u32 before = 0)","doc":"The CRC-32 of data, as gzip and zip use it; give the one before to go on\nfrom it.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":78},{"name":"adler32","kind":"func","sig":"pub u32 adler32(str data, u32 before = 1)","doc":"The Adler-32 checksum of data, as zlib uses it.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":81},{"name":"Writer","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Writer {\n    pub String out\n}","doc":"Compresses a part at a time: what comes out is added to out.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":84},{"name":"Writer.write","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Writer.write(str data)","doc":"Compresses data and adds what comes out to out.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":115},{"name":"Writer.finish","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Writer.finish()","doc":"Ends the stream: out is then complete.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":118},{"name":"Writer.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.free()","doc":"Frees the stream and out.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":121},{"name":"writer","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Writer writer(Format format = .gzip, i64 level = 6)","doc":"A Writer that compresses in format, at a level from 1 to 9.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":90},{"name":"Reader","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Reader {\n    pub String out\n}","doc":"Decompresses a part at a time: what comes out is added to out.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":128},{"name":"Reader.write","kind":"method","sig":"pub !bool Reader.write(str data)","doc":"Feeds compressed data: true when the stream has ended.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":146},{"name":"Reader.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Reader.free()","doc":"Frees the stream and out.","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":170},{"name":"reader","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Reader reader(Format format = .gzip, i64 limit = 0)","doc":"A Reader that decompresses format; it fails with tooLarge when out would\npass limit bytes (0: 1 GiB).","file":"compress/compress.iris","line":137}]},{"name":"tar","title":"Tar archives","group":"Text and data","summary":"Reading, writing and extracting tar archives, gzip-compressed ones too.","intro":"<h2 id=\"writing\"><a href=\"#writing\">Writing</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>tar.Writer</code> builds an archive in memory:</p>\n<ul><li><code>add</code> adds a file from its bytes, <code>addDir</code> a folder and <code>addLink</code> a symbolic link.</li><li><code>addFile</code> adds a file from the disk, and <code>addTree</code> a whole folder from the disk.</li><li><code>finish</code> ends the archive. <code>out</code> then holds it.</li></ul>\n<p>Names longer than the ustar header holds are written with a PAX header, so any length works:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> tar, compress, fs\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    tar.<span class=\"t\">Writer</span> w\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> w.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    w.<span class=\"f\">addDir</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;site&quot;</span>)\n    w.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;site/index.html&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;h1&gt;hello&lt;/h1&gt;</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>, mtime: <span class=\"n\">1760000000</span>)\n    w.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;site/run.sh&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;#!/bin/sh</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>, mode: <span class=\"n\">493</span>)\n    w.<span class=\"f\">addLink</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;site/home.html&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;index.html&quot;</span>)\n    w.<span class=\"f\">finish</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> packed = <span class=\"k\">try</span> compress.<span class=\"f\">gzip</span>(w.out)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">writeAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site.tar.gz&quot;</span>, packed)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>w.out.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>packed.len &lt; w.out.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>4096 bytes, true</code></pre>\n<p><code>mode</code> is the permission bits, 420 (0644) unless given. <code>mtime</code> is the time of change in seconds since 1970, 0 unless given, so the same files make the same archive.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading\"><a href=\"#reading\">Reading</a></h2>\n<p><code>tar.parse(data)</code> reads an archive from memory and <code>tar.open(path)</code> from a file. An archive compressed with gzip is decompressed first. Each <code>tar.Entry</code> has its <code>name</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>mode</code>, <code>mtime</code>, <code>link</code> and <code>data</code> (a file's bytes). <code>find(name)</code> gives the entry with that name, or <code>null</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> tar\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> tar.<span class=\"t\">Archive</span> a = <span class=\"k\">try</span> tar.<span class=\"f\">open</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site.tar.gz&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> e <span class=\"k\">in</span> a.entries {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.kind<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.size<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.mode<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(a.<span class=\"f\">find</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;site/index.html&quot;</span>).data)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>dir site/ 0 493\nfile site/index.html 15 420\nfile site/run.sh 10 493\nsymlink site/home.html 0 511\n&lt;h1&gt;hello&lt;/h1&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Data that isn't tar, a header whose checksum doesn't match, or an archive cut short fails with <code>TarError.broken</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"into-a-folder\"><a href=\"#into-a-folder\">Into a folder</a></h2>\n<p><code>extract(dir)</code> puts the files, folders and links into <code>dir</code>, with their modes. A name that is absolute or has a <code>..</code> part, or a link that points out of <code>dir</code>, fails with <code>TarError.unsafe</code> before anything is written. An archive from elsewhere can't write outside <code>dir</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> tar, fs\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> tar.<span class=\"t\">Archive</span> a = <span class=\"k\">try</span> tar.<span class=\"f\">open</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site.tar.gz&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> a.<span class=\"f\">extract</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> page = <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">readAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site/site/index.html&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(page)\n\n    tar.<span class=\"t\">Writer</span> w\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> w.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    w.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;../../etc/cron.d/job&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;* * * * * root sh</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    w.<span class=\"f\">finish</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> tar.<span class=\"t\">Archive</span> bad = <span class=\"k\">try</span> tar.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(w.out)\n    bad.<span class=\"f\">extract</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.message<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">removeAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-site.tar.gz&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>&lt;h1&gt;hello&lt;/h1&gt;\n\nTarError.unsafe: ../../etc/cron.d/job has .. in it, which would leave the folder</code></pre>","doc":"Tar archives: reading them from memory or a file (gzip-compressed ones\ntoo), writing them, and putting their files into a folder. Names longer\nthan the ustar header holds are written with a PAX header.\n\n    scoped tar.Archive a = try tar.open(\"site.tar.gz\")\n    try a.extract(\"/srv/site\")","decls":[{"name":"TarError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum TarError { broken, unsafe }","doc":"What tar fails with: broken for an archive that isn't tar or is cut short,\nunsafe for a name that would put a file outside the folder it goes into.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":12},{"name":"Kind","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Kind { file, dir, symlink, link, other }","doc":"What an entry is: a file, a folder, a symbolic link, a hard link, or\nsomething else (a device, a FIFO).","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":16},{"name":"Entry","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Entry {\n    pub String name\n    pub Kind kind\n    pub i64 size\n    pub i64 mode\n    pub i64 mtime\n    pub String link\n    pub str data\n}","doc":"One file, folder or link of an archive.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":19},{"name":"Entry.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Entry.free()","doc":"Frees the entry's name and link.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":34},{"name":"Archive","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Archive {\n    pub List[Entry] entries\n}","doc":"The entries of an archive, read from memory or a file.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":40},{"name":"Archive.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Archive.free()","doc":"Frees the entries, and the bytes the archive read or decompressed.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":46},{"name":"Archive.find","kind":"method","sig":"pub Entry *Archive.find(str name)","doc":"The entry called name, or null.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":52},{"name":"Archive.extract","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Archive.extract(str dir)","doc":"Puts the archive's files, folders and links into dir, made when it isn't\nthere. A name with .. in it, or an absolute one, or a link that points out\nof dir, fails with TarError.unsafe before anything is written.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":316},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Archive parse(str data)","doc":"Reads an archive from memory; one compressed with gzip is decompressed\nfirst. The entries' data points into data, which must outlive the archive,\nunless it was compressed.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":256},{"name":"open","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Archive open(str file)","doc":"Reads an archive from a file; one compressed with gzip is decompressed.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":267},{"name":"Writer","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Writer {\n    pub String out\n}","doc":"Builds an archive in memory: add entries, then finish. out is the\narchive, to write to a file or compress.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":366},{"name":"Writer.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.free()","doc":"Frees the archive's bytes.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":371},{"name":"Writer.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.add(str name, str data, i64 mode = 420, i64 mtime = 0)","doc":"Adds a file with data as its bytes. mode is the permission bits (420 is\n0644, 493 is 0755) and mtime the time of change in seconds since 1970.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":488},{"name":"Writer.addDir","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.addDir(str name, i64 mode = 493, i64 mtime = 0)","doc":"Adds a folder; its name gets a / at the end when it has none.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":493},{"name":"Writer.addLink","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.addLink(str name, str target, i64 mtime = 0)","doc":"Adds a symbolic link called name that points to target.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":503},{"name":"Writer.addFile","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Writer.addFile(str file, str name = \"\")","doc":"Adds the file at file as name (its own name when name is \"\"), with its\ntime of change, and mode 493 when it can be run, else 420.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":509},{"name":"Writer.addTree","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Writer.addTree(str dir, str prefix = \"\")","doc":"Adds the files and folders inside dir, with names relative to it, under\nprefix when that isn't \"\".","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":519},{"name":"Writer.finish","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.finish()","doc":"Ends the archive with the two empty blocks tar expects.","file":"tar/tar.iris","line":535}]},{"name":"zip","title":"Zip archives","group":"Text and data","summary":"Reading, writing and extracting zip archives, stored or deflated, with the CRC checked.","intro":"<h2 id=\"writing\"><a href=\"#writing\">Writing</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>zip.Writer</code> builds an archive in memory:</p>\n<ul><li><code>add</code> adds a file from its bytes. It is deflated unless that wouldn't make it smaller, or stored with <code>method: .stored</code>.</li><li><code>addDir</code> adds a folder, <code>addFile</code> a file from the disk and <code>addTree</code> a whole folder.</li><li><code>finish</code> writes the central directory. <code>out</code> then holds the archive.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> zip, fs, <span class=\"t\">str</span>\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    zip.<span class=\"t\">Writer</span> w\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> w.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> report = <span class=\"t\">str</span>.<span class=\"f\">repeat</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;region eu-west, 200 OK</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">200</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> w.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;report.txt&quot;</span>, report)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> w.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;logo.png&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">\\x89</span><span class=\"s\">PNG...&quot;</span>, method: .stored)\n    w.<span class=\"f\">addDir</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;empty&quot;</span>)\n    w.<span class=\"f\">finish</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">writeAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-report.zip&quot;</span>, w.out)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>report.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes of text, under 500 in the zip: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>w.out.len &lt; <span class=\"n\">500</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>4600 bytes of text, under 500 in the zip: true</code></pre>\n<p>Names are written as UTF-8. Each file keeps its mode, 420 (0644) unless <code>mode</code> gives another. Archives over 4 GiB need zip64, which is neither written nor read.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading\"><a href=\"#reading\">Reading</a></h2>\n<p><code>zip.parse(data)</code> reads an archive from memory and <code>zip.open(path)</code> from a file. Each <code>zip.Entry</code> has its <code>name</code>, <code>isDir</code>, <code>method</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>compressedSize</code>, <code>crc</code>, <code>mtime</code> and <code>mode</code>. <code>read(entry)</code> gives an entry's bytes, decompressed. It fails with <code>ZipError.crc</code> when they don't match the archive's CRC-32 for them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> zip, fs\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> zip.<span class=\"t\">Archive</span> a = <span class=\"k\">try</span> zip.<span class=\"f\">open</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-report.zip&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> e <span class=\"k\">in</span> a.entries {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.method<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.size<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes, smaller: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.compressedSize &lt; e.size<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = <span class=\"k\">try</span> a.<span class=\"f\">read</span>(a.<span class=\"f\">find</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;report.txt&quot;</span>))\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(text[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">22</span>])\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-report.zip&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>report.txt: deflated, 4600 bytes, smaller: true\nlogo.png: stored, 7 bytes, smaller: false\nempty/: stored, 0 bytes, smaller: false\nregion eu-west, 200 OK</code></pre>\n<p><code>extract(dir)</code> puts the files and folders into <code>dir</code>. A name that is absolute or has a <code>..</code> part fails with <code>ZipError.unsafe</code> before anything is written. An encrypted entry, or one compressed with another method, fails with <code>ZipError.unsupported</code>.</p>","doc":"Zip archives: reading them from memory or a file, with each entry's\nCRC-32 checked, writing them with stored or deflated entries, and putting\ntheir files into a folder. Archives over 4 GiB (zip64) are not read.\n\n    scoped zip.Archive a = try zip.open(\"site.zip\")\n    scoped String page = try a.read(a.find(\"index.html\"))","decls":[{"name":"ZipError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum ZipError { broken, crc, unsupported, unsafe }","doc":"What zip fails with: broken for data that isn't a zip archive, crc for\nan entry whose bytes don't match its CRC-32, unsupported for an entry that\nis encrypted or compressed another way, unsafe for a name that would put a\nfile outside the folder it goes into.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":14},{"name":"Method","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Method { stored, deflated }","doc":"How an entry's bytes are kept: as they are, or deflated.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":17},{"name":"Entry","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Entry {\n    pub String name\n    pub bool isDir\n    pub Method method\n    pub i64 size\n    pub i64 compressedSize\n    pub u32 crc\n    pub i64 mtime\n    pub i64 mode\n}","doc":"One file or folder of an archive.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":20},{"name":"Entry.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Entry.free()","doc":"Frees the entry's name.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":39},{"name":"Archive","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Archive {\n    pub List[Entry] entries\n}","doc":"The entries of an archive, read from memory or a file.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":42},{"name":"Archive.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Archive.free()","doc":"Frees the entries, and the bytes the archive read from a file.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":48},{"name":"Archive.find","kind":"method","sig":"pub Entry *Archive.find(str name)","doc":"The entry called name, or null.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":54},{"name":"Archive.read","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String Archive.read(Entry *e)","doc":"The bytes of an entry, decompressed, with their CRC-32 checked.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":180},{"name":"Archive.extract","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Archive.extract(str dir)","doc":"Puts the archive's files and folders into dir, made when it isn't there.\nA name with .. in it, or an absolute one, fails with ZipError.unsafe before\nanything is written.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":230},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Archive parse(str data)","doc":"Reads an archive from memory. The entries point into data, which must\noutlive the archive.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":169},{"name":"open","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Archive open(str file)","doc":"Reads an archive from a file.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":172},{"name":"Writer","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Writer {\n    pub String out\n    pub i64 level\n}","doc":"Builds an archive in memory: add entries, then finish. out is the\narchive, to write to a file.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":257},{"name":"Writer.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.free()","doc":"Frees the archive's bytes.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":266},{"name":"Writer.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Writer.add(str name, str data, Method method = .deflated, i64 mode = 420, i64 mtime = 0)","doc":"Adds a file with data as its bytes, deflated unless that wouldn't make it\nsmaller, or stored. mode is the permission bits (420 is 0644, 493 is\n0755) and mtime the time of change in seconds since 1970.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":338},{"name":"Writer.addDir","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.addDir(str name, i64 mode = 493, i64 mtime = 0)","doc":"Adds a folder; its name gets a / at the end when it has none.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":369},{"name":"Writer.addFile","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Writer.addFile(str file, str name = \"\", Method method = .deflated)","doc":"Adds the file at file as name (its own name when name is \"\"), with its\ntime of change, and mode 493 when it can be run, else 420.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":383},{"name":"Writer.addTree","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Writer.addTree(str dir, str prefix = \"\", Method method = .deflated)","doc":"Adds the files and folders inside dir, with names relative to it, under\nprefix when that isn't \"\".","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":393},{"name":"Writer.finish","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Writer.finish()","doc":"Ends the archive with its central directory: out is then complete.","file":"zip/zip.iris","line":409}]},{"name":"crypto","title":"Cryptography","group":"Text and data","summary":"SHA-256, SHA-512, HMAC, HKDF, PBKDF2, Ed25519 signatures, X25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, AES-GCM, SHA-1, MD5 and a constant-time compare.","intro":"<h2 id=\"hashes-and-hmac\"><a href=\"#hashes-and-hmac\">Hashes and HMAC</a></h2>\n<p><code>sha256</code> and <code>sha512</code> give a fixed-size hash of any text or bytes. <code>hmacSha256</code> and <code>hmacSha512</code> mix in a key, so only someone with the key can make the same value. <code>hex.encode</code> makes the result readable. <code>equal</code> compares two values in the same time wherever they differ:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> crypto, hex\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> h = hex.<span class=\"f\">encode</span>(crypto.<span class=\"f\">sha256</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;hello&quot;</span>))\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(h)\n\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] tag = crypto.<span class=\"f\">hmacSha256</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;server key&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;user=7&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] again = crypto.<span class=\"f\">hmacSha256</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;server key&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;user=7&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>crypto.<span class=\"f\">equal</span>(tag[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], again[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824\ntrue</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"keys-from-a-secret\"><a href=\"#keys-from-a-secret\">Keys from a secret</a></h2>\n<p><code>hkdfSha256</code> makes keys from a shared secret, such as the result of a key exchange. The <code>info</code> text says what a key is for, so one secret gives a different key for each use. <code>pbkdf2Sha256</code> makes a key from a password. It is slow on purpose, for storing passwords.</p>\n<h2 id=\"signatures\"><a href=\"#signatures\">Signatures</a></h2>\n<p>Ed25519 signs messages with a secret seed of 32 random bytes. <code>ed25519Public</code> gives the seed's public key, which can be shared. <code>ed25519Sign</code> makes a 64-byte signature, and <code>ed25519Verify</code> checks one with the public key alone:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> crypto, random\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] seed\n    random.<span class=\"f\">bytes</span>(seed[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] key = crypto.<span class=\"f\">ed25519Public</span>(seed[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">64</span>] sig = crypto.<span class=\"f\">ed25519Sign</span>(seed[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], <span class=\"s\">&quot;release 1.4.2&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>crypto.<span class=\"f\">ed25519Verify</span>(key[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], <span class=\"s\">&quot;release 1.4.2&quot;</span>, sig[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">64</span>])<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>crypto.<span class=\"f\">ed25519Verify</span>(key[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], <span class=\"s\">&quot;release 1.4.3&quot;</span>, sig[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">64</span>])<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>true\nfalse</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"encryption\"><a href=\"#encryption\">Encryption</a></h2>\n<p><code>seal</code> encrypts data with ChaCha20-Poly1305, using a 32-byte key and a 12-byte nonce, and adds a 16-byte tag. <code>unseal</code> gives the data back, or fails with <code>CryptoError.tampered</code> when a byte was changed or the key is wrong.</p>\n<ul><li>Never use a nonce twice with the same key. Count messages, or take each nonce from <code>random.bytes</code>.</li><li><code>extra</code> is data sent along unencrypted but checked too, such as a header.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> crypto, random\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] key\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">12</span>] nonce\n    random.<span class=\"f\">bytes</span>(key[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n    random.<span class=\"f\">bytes</span>(nonce[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">12</span>])\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> box = crypto.<span class=\"f\">seal</span>(key[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], nonce[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">12</span>], <span class=\"s\">&quot;db password: hunter2&quot;</span>, extra: <span class=\"s\">&quot;v1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> back = <span class=\"k\">try</span> crypto.<span class=\"f\">unseal</span>(key[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], nonce[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">12</span>], box, extra: <span class=\"s\">&quot;v1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>box.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes sealed, back: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>back<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> wrong = crypto.<span class=\"f\">unseal</span>(key[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], nonce[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">12</span>], box, extra: <span class=\"s\">&quot;v2&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>36 bytes sealed, back: db password: hunter2\nCryptoError.tampered</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"aes-gcm\"><a href=\"#aes-gcm\">AES-GCM</a></h2>\n<p><code>aesSeal</code> and <code>aesUnseal</code> work like <code>seal</code> and <code>unseal</code>, with AES-GCM and a 16, 24 or 32-byte key. Many protocols and file formats use AES-GCM.</p>\n<p>On CPUs with AES instructions, which x86_64 and most ARM64 CPUs have, those instructions do the work and AES-GCM is the fastest choice. Elsewhere it runs in plain code that takes the same time for any key. That is much slower, so <code>seal</code> is the better choice there.</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> crypto, hex, random\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] key\n    random.<span class=\"f\">bytes</span>(key)\n    nonce := <span class=\"s\">&quot;nonce 000001&quot;</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> sealed = crypto.<span class=\"f\">aesSeal</span>(key, nonce, <span class=\"s\">&quot;card 4242&quot;</span>, extra: <span class=\"s\">&quot;order 17&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>sealed.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> back = <span class=\"k\">try</span> crypto.<span class=\"f\">aesUnseal</span>(key, nonce, sealed, extra: <span class=\"s\">&quot;order 17&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(back)\n\n    _ = crypto.<span class=\"f\">aesUnseal</span>(key, nonce, sealed, extra: <span class=\"s\">&quot;order 18&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</span>)\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>25 bytes\ncard 4242\nCryptoError.tampered</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"agreeing-on-a-key\"><a href=\"#agreeing-on-a-key\">Agreeing on a key</a></h2>\n<p>X25519 lets two sides agree on a secret over a network anyone can read:</p>\n<ol><li>Each side makes a 32-byte secret with <code>random.bytes</code> and sends the other side <code>x25519Public</code> of it.</li><li><code>x25519</code> with its own secret and the other side's public key gives both sides the same 32 bytes.</li><li><code>hkdfSha256</code> turns those bytes into keys for <code>seal</code>.</li></ol>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> crypto, random\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] client\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] server\n    random.<span class=\"f\">bytes</span>(client[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n    random.<span class=\"f\">bytes</span>(server[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] clientPub = crypto.<span class=\"f\">x25519Public</span>(client[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] serverPub = crypto.<span class=\"f\">x25519Public</span>(server[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] a = <span class=\"k\">try</span> crypto.<span class=\"f\">x25519</span>(client[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], serverPub[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">32</span>] b = <span class=\"k\">try</span> crypto.<span class=\"f\">x25519</span>(server[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], clientPub[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> key = crypto.<span class=\"f\">hkdfSha256</span>(a[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], <span class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;chat v1 key&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;same secret: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>crypto.<span class=\"f\">equal</span>(a[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>], b[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">32</span>])<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, key of </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>key.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>same secret: true, key of 32 bytes</code></pre>\n<p>All of these run in the program itself and take the same time whatever the secret is. SHA-1 and MD5 are here for older protocols only.</p>","doc":"Hashes, signatures and encryption: SHA-256, SHA-512, HMAC, HKDF, PBKDF2,\nEd25519 signatures, X25519 key exchange, ChaCha20-Poly1305 and AES-GCM,\nand SHA-1 and MD5 for older protocols.","decls":[{"name":"CryptoError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum CryptoError {\n    badKey\n    tampered\n}","doc":"What can go wrong: a peer's key that isn't one, or sealed data that was\nchanged or sealed with another key.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":7},{"name":"sha256","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[32] sha256(str data)","doc":"The SHA-256 hash of data: 32 bytes. hex.encode makes it readable.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":55},{"name":"hmacSha256","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[32] hmacSha256(str key, str data)","doc":"The HMAC-SHA256 of data with key: 32 bytes that only someone with the\nkey can make.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":63},{"name":"sha512","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[64] sha512(str data)","doc":"The SHA-512 hash of data: 64 bytes.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":70},{"name":"hmacSha512","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[64] hmacSha512(str key, str data)","doc":"The HMAC-SHA512 of data with key: 64 bytes that only someone with the\nkey can make.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":78},{"name":"hkdfSha256","kind":"func","sig":"pub String hkdfSha256(str secret, str salt, str info, i64 size = 32)","doc":"HKDF with HMAC-SHA256 (RFC 5869): size bytes of keys made from a shared\nsecret. The salt can be empty; info says what the keys are for, so one\nsecret gives different keys for different uses. size is at most 8160.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":87},{"name":"ed25519Public","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[32] ed25519Public(str seed)","doc":"The Ed25519 public key for a secret seed of 32 bytes (RFC 8032). Make a\nseed with random.bytes and keep it secret; the public key can be shared.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":114},{"name":"ed25519Sign","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[64] ed25519Sign(str seed, str message)","doc":"The Ed25519 signature of message with the secret seed: 64 bytes that\nanyone with the public key can check, and only the seed can make.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":125},{"name":"ed25519Verify","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool ed25519Verify(str publicKey, str message, str signature)","doc":"Whether signature is the Ed25519 signature of message for the public\nkey. A key or signature of the wrong size is false.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":136},{"name":"x25519Public","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[32] x25519Public(str secret)","doc":"The X25519 public key for a secret of 32 random bytes (RFC 7748). Two\nsides each make a secret with random.bytes and send the other their\npublic key; x25519 then gives both of them the same shared secret.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":146},{"name":"x25519","kind":"func","sig":"pub !u8[32] x25519(str secret, str peerPublic)","doc":"The secret shared with the peer whose public key is peerPublic. Make\nkeys from it with hkdfSha256 rather than using it as one.\nCryptoError.badKey when peerPublic isn't 32 bytes or gives nothing\nsecret.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":159},{"name":"seal","kind":"func","sig":"pub String seal(str key, str nonce, str data, str extra = \"\")","doc":"Encrypts data with ChaCha20-Poly1305 (RFC 8439): a 32-byte key and a\n12-byte nonce. What it gives is data.len + 16 bytes: the data, hidden,\nand a tag that unseal checks. extra is data that isn't hidden but is\nchecked too, like a header sent alongside. A nonce must never be used\ntwice with one key: count them, or take each from random.bytes.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":178},{"name":"aesSeal","kind":"func","sig":"pub String aesSeal(str key, str nonce, str data, str extra = \"\")","doc":"Encrypts data with AES-GCM: a 16, 24 or 32-byte key (AES-128, AES-192 or\nAES-256) and a 12-byte nonce. It works as seal does and gives\ndata.len + 16 bytes. On CPUs with AES instructions (x86_64 and most\nARM64 ones) it is the fastest choice. A nonce must never be used twice\nwith one key.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":198},{"name":"aesUnseal","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String aesUnseal(str key, str nonce, str sealed, str extra = \"\")","doc":"The data that aesSeal hid, with the same key, nonce and extra.\nCryptoError.tampered when anything was changed, or the key, nonce or\nextra differ.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":208},{"name":"unseal","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String unseal(str key, str nonce, str sealed, str extra = \"\")","doc":"The data that seal hid, with the same key, nonce and extra.\nCryptoError.tampered when anything was changed, or the key, nonce or\nextra differ.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":226},{"name":"equal","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool equal(str a, str b)","doc":"Whether a and b are the same, taking the same time wherever they differ.\nUse it to compare hashes and tokens.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":239},{"name":"sha1","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[20] sha1(str data)","doc":"The SHA-1 hash of data: 20 bytes. Old and broken for signatures; it is\nhere for protocols that still use it, like the WebSocket handshake.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":243},{"name":"md5","kind":"func","sig":"pub u8[16] md5(str data)","doc":"The MD5 hash of data: 16 bytes. Broken for signatures too; for older\nprotocols and checksums only.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":251},{"name":"pbkdf2Sha256","kind":"func","sig":"pub String pbkdf2Sha256(str password, str salt, i64 iterations, i64 size = 32)","doc":"PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 (RFC 8018): a key of size bytes from a password\nand a salt, made slow by iterations. For storing passwords, and for\nlogins like SCRAM.","file":"crypto/crypto.iris","line":260}]},{"name":"jwt","title":"JSON Web Tokens","group":"Text and data","summary":"Signing and checking tokens with HS256 or EdDSA, with their claims in a @json struct.","intro":"<h2 id=\"signing-and-checking-tokens\"><a href=\"#signing-and-checking-tokens\">Signing and checking tokens</a></h2>\n<p>A server signs a token with the claims of a <code>@json</code> struct and gives it to a client. Later, <code>verify</code> reads the claims back and fails when the token was changed:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> jwt, time\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Claims</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> sub\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> role\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> exp\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    key := <span class=\"s\">&quot;at least 32 random bytes, kept on the server&quot;</span>\n    exp := time.<span class=\"f\">now</span>().<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"n\">1h</span>).<span class=\"f\">unix</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> token = jwt.<span class=\"f\">sign</span>(<span class=\"t\">Claims</span>{sub: <span class=\"s\">&quot;ada&quot;</span>.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(), role: <span class=\"s\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(), exp: exp}, key)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">Claims</span> c = <span class=\"k\">try</span> jwt.verify[<span class=\"t\">Claims</span>](token, key)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>c.sub<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> is </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>c.role<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    _ = jwt.verify[<span class=\"t\">Claims</span>](token, <span class=\"s\">&quot;another key&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"t\">Claims</span>{}\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>ada is admin\nJwtError.signature</code></pre>\n<p><code>verify</code> accepts only tokens signed with HS256, and <code>verifyEd25519</code> only EdDSA ones, whatever the token's header says.</p>\n<p><code>exp</code> and <code>nbf</code> claims are seconds since 1970. A token past its <code>exp</code> fails with <code>JwtError.expired</code>, and one before its <code>nbf</code> with <code>JwtError.early</code>. <code>leeway</code> gives some room to clocks that differ a little.</p>\n<p>With <code>signEd25519</code>, a service signs with its private seed and other services check with the public key alone. Those services can't make tokens of their own.</p>","doc":"JSON Web Tokens (RFC 7519), signed with HS256 (HMAC-SHA256 and a shared\nkey) or EdDSA (Ed25519 and a key pair). The claims are a @json struct:\n\n    @json\n    struct Claims {\n        String sub\n        i64 exp\n    }\n\n    token := jwt.sign(Claims{sub: \"ada\".copy(), exp: time.now().add(1h).unix()}, key)\n    scoped Claims c = try jwt.verify[Claims](token, key)\n\nverify checks the algorithm, the signature, and the exp and nbf claims\nwhen the token has them, before it reads the claims.","decls":[{"name":"JwtError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum JwtError { invalid, algorithm, signature, expired, early }","doc":"What verify fails with: invalid (not a token, or claims that don't\ndecode), algorithm (signed with another algorithm than the one asked\nfor), signature (the signature doesn't match: the token was changed or\nsigned with another key), expired (past its exp), early (before its nbf).","file":"jwt/jwt.iris","line":22},{"name":"sign","kind":"func","sig":"pub String sign[T](T claims, str key)","doc":"A token with claims, signed with HS256 and key. A key should be at\nleast 32 random bytes.","file":"jwt/jwt.iris","line":38},{"name":"verify","kind":"func","sig":"pub !T verify[T](str token, str key, Duration leeway = 0s)","doc":"The claims of an HS256 token signed with key. leeway is how far past\nits exp or before its nbf the token is still taken, for clocks that\ndiffer a little.","file":"jwt/jwt.iris","line":51},{"name":"signEd25519","kind":"func","sig":"pub String signEd25519[T](T claims, str seed)","doc":"A token with claims, signed with EdDSA (Ed25519) and the 32-byte private\nseed. Anyone with crypto.ed25519Public(seed) can verify it, without\nbeing able to sign.","file":"jwt/jwt.iris","line":67},{"name":"verifyEd25519","kind":"func","sig":"pub !T verifyEd25519[T](str token, str publicKey, Duration leeway = 0s)","doc":"The claims of an EdDSA token, checked with the signer's 32-byte public\nkey.","file":"jwt/jwt.iris","line":79}]},{"name":"uuid","title":"UUIDs","group":"Text and data","summary":"Random v4 and time-ordered v7 UUIDs, written as text and read back.","intro":"<h2 id=\"making-and-reading-uuids\"><a href=\"#making-and-reading-uuids\">Making and reading UUIDs</a></h2>\n<p><code>v4</code> makes a random UUID and <code>v7</code> one that starts with the time. <code>format</code> writes either as text. <code>parse</code> reads one back, in either case and with or without dashes:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> uuid\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    id := uuid.<span class=\"f\">v7</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = id.<span class=\"f\">format</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>text.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> characters, version </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>id.<span class=\"f\">version</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    known := <span class=\"k\">try</span> uuid.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;0190E5D4-8A2B-7C3D-9E4F-5A6B7C8D9E0F&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> back = known.<span class=\"f\">format</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> day = known.<span class=\"f\">time</span>().<span class=\"f\">dateText</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>back<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, made on </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>day<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>known.<span class=\"f\">equal</span>(id)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>known.<span class=\"f\">compare</span>(id) &lt; <span class=\"n\">0</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>36 characters, version 7\n0190e5d4-8a2b-7c3d-9e4f-5a6b7c8d9e0f, made on 2024-07-24\nfalse true</code></pre>\n<p>A v7 UUID made in a later millisecond sorts after one made earlier, both as bytes with <code>compare</code> and as text. Rows with v7 ids are therefore added at the end of a database index. Two UUIDs made in the same millisecond can come in either order.</p>","doc":"UUIDs (RFC 9562): 128-bit ids written as 36 characters,\n\"0190e5d4-8a2b-7c3d-9e4f-5a6b7c8d9e0f\". v4 is random. v7 starts with the\ntime in milliseconds, so an id made in a later millisecond sorts after\none made earlier, which keeps database indexes compact.","decls":[{"name":"UuidError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum UuidError { invalid }","doc":"What parse fails with: invalid for text that isn't a UUID.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":9},{"name":"Uuid","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Uuid {\n    pub u8[16] bytes\n}","doc":"A UUID: its 16 bytes.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":12},{"name":"Uuid.version","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Uuid.version()","doc":"The version: 4 or 7 for the ones made here, 0 for nil.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":43},{"name":"Uuid.time","kind":"method","sig":"pub time.Time Uuid.time()","doc":"The time a v7 UUID was made, to the millisecond.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":46},{"name":"Uuid.equal","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Uuid.equal(Uuid other)","doc":"Whether u and other are the same UUID.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":55},{"name":"Uuid.compare","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Uuid.compare(Uuid other)","doc":"Less than 0 when u sorts before other, 0 when they are the same, more\nthan 0 when it sorts after: the order of their text. v7 UUIDs sort by\nthe time they were made.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":67},{"name":"Uuid.isNil","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Uuid.isNil()","doc":"Whether the UUID is nil, all zeros.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":77},{"name":"Uuid.format","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Uuid.format()","doc":"The UUID as 36 characters, in lower case.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":80},{"name":"v4","kind":"func","sig":"pub Uuid v4()","doc":"A random UUID, version 4: 122 random bits from the system's secure\nsource.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":18},{"name":"v7","kind":"func","sig":"pub Uuid v7()","doc":"A UUID of version 7: the Unix time in milliseconds, then 74 random bits.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":27},{"name":"nil","kind":"func","sig":"pub Uuid nil()","doc":"The UUID with all bits zero.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":40},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Uuid parse(str text)","doc":"A UUID written as 36 characters with dashes, in upper or lower case,\nor as 32 hex digits without them.","file":"uuid/uuid.iris","line":96}]},{"name":"io","title":"Standard input and output","group":"System","summary":"Lines from standard input, and writing to standard output and error.","intro":"","doc":"Reading from standard input, and writing to standard output and error.","decls":[{"name":"IoError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum IoError { eof, other }","doc":"What reading fails with: eof at the end of input, other for anything else.","file":"io/io.iris","line":4},{"name":"readLine","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String readLine()","doc":"The next line of standard input, without its line break. IoError.eof\nwhen there is no more.","file":"io/io.iris","line":20},{"name":"readAll","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String readAll()","doc":"All of standard input.","file":"io/io.iris","line":33},{"name":"write","kind":"func","sig":"pub void write(str text)","doc":"Writes text to standard output, without a newline.","file":"io/io.iris","line":42},{"name":"ewrite","kind":"func","sig":"pub void ewrite(str text)","doc":"Writes text to standard error.","file":"io/io.iris","line":45},{"name":"eprint","kind":"func","sig":"pub void eprint(str text)","doc":"Writes text and a line break to standard error.","file":"io/io.iris","line":48}]},{"name":"fs","title":"Files","group":"System","summary":"Reading and writing files, folders, walking them and finding files by pattern, copies, temporary files, and files mapped into memory.","intro":"<h2 id=\"whole-files\"><a href=\"#whole-files\">Whole files</a></h2>\n<p><code>readAll</code> gives a file's contents as a <code>String</code>. <code>writeAll</code> replaces a file's contents and makes the file when it is missing. <code>append</code> adds to the end:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> fs\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    path := <span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-fs-notes.txt&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">writeAll</span>(path, <span class=\"s\">&quot;first</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">append</span>(path, <span class=\"s\">&quot;second</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> text = <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">readAll</span>(path)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>text.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    info := <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">stat</span>(path)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;size </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>info.size<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, folder </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>info.isDir<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(path)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;still there: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>fs.<span class=\"f\">exists</span>(path)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>13 bytes: first\nsecond\n\nsize 13, folder false\nstill there: false</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"line-by-line\"><a href=\"#line-by-line\">Line by line</a></h2>\n<p><code>open</code> opens a file for reading. <code>readLine</code> gives one line at a time and fails with <code>FsError.end</code> at the end. This reads a big file without holding all of it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> fs\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    path := <span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-fs-lines.txt&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">writeAll</span>(path, <span class=\"s\">&quot;ada 36</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">zoe 41</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    f := <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">open</span>(path)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> f.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> n = <span class=\"n\">0</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> {\n        line := f.<span class=\"f\">readLine</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n            <span class=\"k\">break</span>\n        }\n        n += <span class=\"n\">1</span>\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>n<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>line<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        line.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(path)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>1: ada 36\n2: zoe 41</code></pre>\n<p><code>create</code> opens a file for writing and empties it first. <code>openAppend</code> opens one for writing at its end. <code>File.write</code> writes to it, and <code>File.read</code> fills a buffer, for files that aren't text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mapping-a-file-into-memory\"><a href=\"#mapping-a-file-into-memory\">Mapping a file into memory</a></h2>\n<p><code>mapFile</code> gives a file's bytes as memory without reading them first. The system reads each part when it is first used, so a large file costs nothing until it is looked at. <code>free</code> gives the memory back.</p>\n<ul><li>Changes stay in memory unless <code>write</code> is set.</li><li>With <code>write</code>, changes go to the file, and every program that maps the same file with <code>write</code> sees them. Two processes can share memory this way.</li><li>With <code>write</code> and a <code>size</code>, the file first grows to that size.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> fs\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">writeAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-map.txt&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;hello, world&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> m := <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">mapFile</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-map.txt&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.bytes.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.bytes[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:<span class=\"n\">5</span>]<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> counters := <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">mapFile</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-counters.bin&quot;</span>, write: <span class=\"k\">true</span>, size: <span class=\"n\">64</span>)\n    counters.bytes[<span class=\"n\">0</span>] += <span class=\"n\">1</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> counters.<span class=\"f\">sync</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;runs: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>counters.bytes[<span class=\"n\">0</span>]<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-counters.bin&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-map.txt&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>12 bytes: hello\nruns: 1</code></pre>\n<p><code>mapMemory</code> gives zeroed memory of any size straight from the system. It suits a large buffer, which goes back to the system as soon as it is freed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"folders\"><a href=\"#folders\">Folders</a></h2>\n<p><code>mkdir</code> makes a folder, and <code>mkdirAll</code> also makes the folders above it. <code>readDir</code> lists a folder. <code>removeAll</code> removes a folder with everything in it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> fs\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">mkdirAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-fs-demo/logs&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">writeAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-fs-demo/a.txt&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> dir := <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">readDir</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-fs-demo&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> folders = <span class=\"n\">0</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> e <span class=\"k\">in</span> dir.entries {\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> e.isDir {\n            folders += <span class=\"n\">1</span>\n        }\n    }\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>dir.entries.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> entries, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>folders<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> a folder&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">removeAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-fs-demo&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>2 entries, 1 a folder</code></pre>\n<p>The entries are sorted by name, without <code>.</code> and <code>..</code>. The <code>path</code> module joins paths and takes them apart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"walking-finding-and-copying\"><a href=\"#walking-finding-and-copying\">Walking, finding and copying</a></h2>\n<p><code>walk</code> gives the path of every file under a folder. <code>glob</code> gives the files that match a pattern with <code>*</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>[abc]</code> and <code>**</code>. <code>copy</code> copies a file. <code>tempDir</code> and <code>tempFile</code> make a folder or a file with a unique name:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> fs, path\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> root = <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">tempDir</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;iris-fs&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> logs = path.<span class=\"f\">join</span>(root, <span class=\"s\">&quot;logs&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">mkdir</span>(logs)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> a = path.<span class=\"f\">join</span>(logs, <span class=\"s\">&quot;a.log&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> b = path.<span class=\"f\">join</span>(root, <span class=\"s\">&quot;b.log&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">writeAll</span>(a, <span class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(a, b)\n\n    all := <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">walk</span>(root)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> all.<span class=\"f\">freeEach</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> pattern = path.<span class=\"f\">join</span>(root, <span class=\"s\">&quot;**/*.log&quot;</span>)\n    logged := <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">glob</span>(pattern)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> logged.<span class=\"f\">freeEach</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>all.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> files, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>logged.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> logs, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>fs.<span class=\"f\">matches</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;*.log&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;a.log&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">removeAll</span>(root)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>2 files, 2 logs, true</code></pre>","doc":"Files and folders: reading, writing, listing, and files opened for\nreading or writing a part at a time.","decls":[{"name":"FsError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum FsError { notFound, permission, exists, isDir, other, end }","doc":"What file calls fail with: notFound, permission, exists (for a path that is\nthere already), isDir (for a folder where a file is wanted), end, and other.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":8},{"name":"readAll","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String readAll(str path)","doc":"The whole file, as text the caller owns.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":32},{"name":"writeAll","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void writeAll(str path, str data)","doc":"Replaces the file's contents, creating it if needed.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":42},{"name":"append","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void append(str path, str data)","doc":"Adds to the end of the file, creating it if needed.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":50},{"name":"exists","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool exists(str path)","doc":"Whether a file or folder exists at path.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":58},{"name":"remove","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void remove(str path)","doc":"Removes a file.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":61},{"name":"Info","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Info {\n    pub i64 size\n    pub bool isDir\n    pub time.Time modified\n}","doc":"What stat tells about a file or folder.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":105},{"name":"stat","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Info stat(str path)","doc":"The size, kind and time of change of what is at path.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":112},{"name":"mkdir","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void mkdir(str path)","doc":"Makes a folder.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":124},{"name":"mkdirAll","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void mkdirAll(str path)","doc":"Makes a folder and the folders above it that are missing. A folder that\nis there already is no error.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":133},{"name":"rename","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void rename(str from, str to)","doc":"Moves or renames a file or folder.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":141},{"name":"removeAll","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void removeAll(str path)","doc":"Removes a file, or a folder and everything in it. Nothing at path is no\nerror.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":150},{"name":"Entry","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Entry {\n    pub String name\n    pub bool isDir\n}","doc":"A name in a folder.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":158},{"name":"Dir","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Dir {\n    pub List[Entry] entries\n}","doc":"What is in a folder, sorted by name. free gives the names back.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":164},{"name":"Dir.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Dir.free()","doc":"Frees the names and the list.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":169},{"name":"readDir","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Dir readDir(str path)","doc":"The files and folders in a folder, without . and ..","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":179},{"name":"File","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct File { }","doc":"A file opened for reading or writing a part at a time.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":200},{"name":"File.read","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 File.read(u8[] buf)","doc":"Reads into buf: how many bytes, 0 at the end of the file.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":226},{"name":"File.readLine","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String File.readLine()","doc":"The next line, without its line break. At the end of the file it fails\nwith FsError.end.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":260},{"name":"File.readAll","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String File.readAll()","doc":"The rest of the file.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":289},{"name":"File.write","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void File.write(str data)","doc":"Writes data at the file's position.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":305},{"name":"File.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void File.close()","doc":"Closes the file.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":313},{"name":"open","kind":"func","sig":"pub !File open(str path)","doc":"Opens a file for reading.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":217},{"name":"create","kind":"func","sig":"pub !File create(str path)","doc":"Opens a file for writing, making it or emptying it.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":220},{"name":"openAppend","kind":"func","sig":"pub !File openAppend(str path)","doc":"Opens a file for writing at its end, making it if needed.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":223},{"name":"Mapped","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Mapped {\n    pub u8[] bytes\n}","doc":"Memory that the OS fills from a file, or zeroed memory, from mapFile and\nmapMemory. bytes is the memory. Free it with free.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":334},{"name":"Mapped.sync","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Mapped.sync()","doc":"Writes the changes made so far to the file now, for a mapFile with\nwrite. Without sync they reach the file at some time after the change.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":376},{"name":"Mapped.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Mapped.free()","doc":"Gives the memory back. A mapFile with write keeps its changes in the\nfile.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":385},{"name":"mapFile","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Mapped mapFile(str path, bool write = false, i64 size = 0)","doc":"Maps the whole file at path into memory. The OS reads each part of the\nfile when it is first used, so a large file costs nothing until it is\nread. With write, changes to bytes go to the file, and every program\nthat maps the same file with write sees them: two processes can share\nmemory this way. With write and a size larger than the file, the file\ngrows to size first, made if it isn't there. Without write, changes stay\nin this memory.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":346},{"name":"mapMemory","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Mapped mapMemory(i64 size)","doc":"size bytes of zeroed memory straight from the OS, for a large buffer\nthat is given back with free as soon as it isn't used.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":366},{"name":"copy","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void copy(str from, str to)","doc":"Copies the file at from to to, with its permissions. A file at to is\nreplaced.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":399},{"name":"tempFile","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String tempFile(str prefix = \"iris\", str dir = \"\")","doc":"Makes a new, empty file with a name no other file has, prefix followed\nby a dash and six random characters, and gives its path. It goes in dir,\nor in the system's folder for temporary files ($TMPDIR, or /tmp) when dir\nis \"\". The caller removes it when done.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":410},{"name":"tempDir","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String tempDir(str prefix = \"iris\", str dir = \"\")","doc":"Makes a new, empty folder with a name no other has, as tempFile does for\na file, and gives its path. The caller removes it with removeAll.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":421},{"name":"walk","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[String] walk(str root)","doc":"The path of every file under a folder and the folders in it, each joined\nto root, folder by folder in the order of their names. Links are listed\nas files and not followed. The caller frees the paths and the list, with\nfreeEach.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":434},{"name":"Found","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Found {\n    pub String path\n    pub Info info\n}","doc":"A file that find found: its path, joined to the root, and its size,\nkind and time of change.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":445},{"name":"Found.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Found.free()","doc":"Gives the path back.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":451},{"name":"find","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[Found] find(str root)","doc":"Every file under a folder and the folders in it, with its size and time,\nas walk finds them and in the same order. Each file's information is\nread relative to its open folder, which costs less than a stat of its\nwhole path. Links are listed as files and not followed. The caller frees\nthe list with freeEach.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":460},{"name":"glob","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[String] glob(str pattern)","doc":"The paths of the files that match a pattern, sorted. In the pattern, *\nmatches any run of characters in one part of a path, ? one character,\n[abc] one of those characters ([a-z] a range, [!abc] any other), and a\npart that is ** matches any number of folders, none included:\nglob(\"src/**/*.iris\"). A folder that isn't there matches nothing. The\ncaller frees the paths and the list, with freeEach.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":544},{"name":"matches","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool matches(str pattern, str name)","doc":"Whether name matches one part of a glob pattern.","file":"fs/fs.iris","line":657}]},{"name":"path","title":"Paths","group":"System","summary":"Joining file paths, taking them apart, cleaning them and making one relative to another.","intro":"<h2 id=\"taking-paths-apart\"><a href=\"#taking-paths-apart\">Taking paths apart</a></h2>\n<p>These functions work on the text of a path and never look at the disk. <code>join</code> and <code>clean</code> give a new <code>String</code>. <code>base</code>, <code>dir</code>, <code>ext</code>, <code>stem</code> and <code>split</code> give a part of the path they are given:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> path\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    dir, name := path.<span class=\"f\">split</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/var/log/proxy/access.log&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>dir<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>path.<span class=\"f\">ext</span>(name)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> c = path.<span class=\"f\">clean</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/var//log/./proxy/../app/&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> r = <span class=\"k\">try</span> path.<span class=\"f\">rel</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/var/log&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;/var/www/index.html&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>c<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>/var/log/proxy/ access.log .log\n/var/log/app ../www/index.html</code></pre>","doc":"Paths of files and folders, as text: joining them and taking them apart.\nThey use /, as Linux and macOS do.","decls":[{"name":"join","kind":"func","sig":"pub String join(str a, str b)","doc":"Two paths joined with a /. When b starts with /, it is b.","file":"path/path.iris","line":5},{"name":"base","kind":"func","sig":"pub str base(str p)","doc":"The last part: base(\"/home/ada/notes.txt\") is \"notes.txt\".","file":"path/path.iris","line":26},{"name":"dir","kind":"func","sig":"pub str dir(str p)","doc":"All but the last part: dir(\"/home/ada/notes.txt\") is \"/home/ada\".","file":"path/path.iris","line":39},{"name":"ext","kind":"func","sig":"pub str ext(str p)","doc":"The extension, with its dot: ext(\"notes.txt\") is \".txt\", and \"\" when\nthere is none.","file":"path/path.iris","line":56},{"name":"stem","kind":"func","sig":"pub str stem(str p)","doc":"The last part without its extension: stem(\"notes.txt\") is \"notes\".","file":"path/path.iris","line":66},{"name":"isAbs","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool isAbs(str p)","doc":"Whether the path starts at the root.","file":"path/path.iris","line":73},{"name":"split","kind":"func","sig":"pub (str, str) split(str p)","doc":"The folder and the name: split(\"/home/ada/notes.txt\") is (\"/home/ada/\",\n\"notes.txt\"). The folder keeps its /, so the two put back together are\nthe path. A path without a / is all name.","file":"path/path.iris","line":78},{"name":"clean","kind":"func","sig":"pub String clean(str p)","doc":"The shortest path to the same place: no . parts, no doubled /, no /\nat the end, and each .. with the part before it taken out.\nclean(\"a//b/./../c/\") is \"a/c\". An empty path is \".\".","file":"path/path.iris","line":89},{"name":"PathError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum PathError { notRelative }","doc":"What rel fails with: notRelative when one path starts at the root and the\nother doesn't, or the base has .. parts the target can't follow.","file":"path/path.iris","line":135},{"name":"rel","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String rel(str base, str target)","doc":"The path that leads from base to target: rel(\"/srv\", \"/srv/www/index.html\")\nis \"www/index.html\", and rel(\"/srv/www\", \"/srv/logs\") is \"../logs\". Both\npaths are cleaned first. Joining base and the result gives target.","file":"path/path.iris","line":140}]},{"name":"os","title":"The program","group":"System","summary":"Its arguments, its environment, the working folder, the machine, and leaving it.","intro":"<h2 id=\"the-program-and-the-machine\"><a href=\"#the-program-and-the-machine\">The program and the machine</a></h2>\n<p><code>arg</code> and <code>env</code> read the program's arguments and environment. <code>cwd</code> and <code>chdir</code> get and change the working folder. <code>home</code>, <code>hostname</code> and <code>pid</code> give the user's home folder, the machine's name and the program's process id:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> os\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> start = <span class=\"k\">try</span> os.<span class=\"f\">cwd</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> os.<span class=\"f\">chdir</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> root = <span class=\"k\">try</span> os.<span class=\"f\">cwd</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> os.<span class=\"f\">chdir</span>(start)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> home = <span class=\"k\">try</span> os.<span class=\"f\">home</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>root<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>home.len &gt; <span class=\"n\">0</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>os.<span class=\"f\">pid</span>() &gt; <span class=\"n\">0</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>os.<span class=\"f\">argc</span>() &gt;= <span class=\"n\">1</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>/ true true true</code></pre>","doc":"The program's arguments and environment, its working folder, the\nmachine it runs on, and leaving the program.","decls":[{"name":"OsError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum OsError { notSet, invalid, notFound, permission, other }","doc":"What env fails with: notSet for a variable that isn't set; setenv fails\nwith invalid for a name that can't be one. chdir fails with notFound for\na folder that isn't there, permission for one the program may not enter,\nand other for the rest.","file":"os/os.iris","line":8},{"name":"argc","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 argc()","doc":"How many arguments the program got, counting its own name.","file":"os/os.iris","line":27},{"name":"arg","kind":"func","sig":"pub str arg(i64 i)","doc":"Argument i; 0 is the program's name. \"\" past the end.","file":"os/os.iris","line":30},{"name":"env","kind":"func","sig":"pub !str env(str name)","doc":"The value of an environment variable.","file":"os/os.iris","line":41},{"name":"setenv","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void setenv(str name, str value)","doc":"Sets an environment variable, for this program and the programs it runs.\nSet variables before starting threads: the environment isn't safe to\nchange while another thread reads it.","file":"os/os.iris","line":53},{"name":"unsetenv","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void unsetenv(str name)","doc":"Removes an environment variable; one that isn't set is no error.","file":"os/os.iris","line":60},{"name":"exit","kind":"func","sig":"@noreturn\npub void exit(i32 code)","doc":"Ends the program with an exit status. Deferred statements don't run. A call\nto it never comes back, so it can end a catch block or a branch that would\notherwise need a value.","file":"os/os.iris","line":70},{"name":"cwd","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String cwd()","doc":"The working folder: where paths that don't start with / are taken from.","file":"os/os.iris","line":94},{"name":"chdir","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void chdir(str path)","doc":"Changes the working folder, for the whole program and its threads.","file":"os/os.iris","line":104},{"name":"hostname","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String hostname()","doc":"The name of the machine.","file":"os/os.iris","line":112},{"name":"pid","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 pid()","doc":"The process id of the program.","file":"os/os.iris","line":121},{"name":"home","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String home()","doc":"The user's home folder: $HOME, or the one the system has for the user\nwhen HOME isn't set.","file":"os/os.iris","line":125}]},{"name":"process","title":"Processes","group":"System","summary":"Running other programs and reading what they print.","intro":"","doc":"Running other programs.","decls":[{"name":"ProcessError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum ProcessError { notFound, failed }","doc":"What run fails with: notFound for a program that isn't there, failed when it\ncan't be started.","file":"process/process.iris","line":5},{"name":"Output","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Output {\n    pub String stdout\n    pub String stderr\n    pub i32 status\n}","doc":"What a program wrote, and its exit status. free gives the text back.","file":"process/process.iris","line":8},{"name":"Output.ok","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Output.ok()","doc":"Whether the program ended with status 0.","file":"process/process.iris","line":15},{"name":"Output.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Output.free()","doc":"Frees what the program printed.","file":"process/process.iris","line":18},{"name":"run","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Output run(str[] argv, str input = \"\")","doc":"Runs argv[0] with the arguments that follow, finds it on PATH when it\nhas no /, gives it input on its standard input, and waits for it to end.\nThe other tasks of the thread run meanwhile. A program that ends with a\nstatus other than 0 is no error: see status.","file":"process/process.iris","line":30}]},{"name":"env","title":"Settings","group":"System","summary":"Environment variables into an @env struct.","intro":"","doc":"Settings from environment variables, into an @env struct.","decls":[{"name":"EnvError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum EnvError { missing, invalid }","doc":"What load fails with: missing for a required variable that isn't set,\ninvalid for a value that doesn't fit its field.","file":"env/env.iris","line":7},{"name":"load","kind":"builtin","sig":"load","doc":"Fills an @env struct from the environment: env.load(Settings). Each\nfield reads the variable named after it in capitals with _ between the\nwords (dbUrl reads DB_URL), or the one @env(\"NAME\") gives; @env(\"APP\")\non the struct puts APP_ in front of every name. A variable that isn't\nset leaves the field's default, unless the field is @env(required).\nNumbers, bool (true, false, 1, 0, yes, no, on, off), str, String, enums\nby name, and Duration (30s, 1500ms, 1m30s) can be read.","file":"env/env.iris","line":17},{"name":"loadWith","kind":"builtin","sig":"loadWith","doc":"Reads the file at path with loadFile and then fills an @env struct like\nload: env.loadWith(Settings, \".env\"). A file that isn't there is\nskipped, so the environment alone is enough.","file":"env/env.iris","line":23},{"name":"loadFile","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void loadFile(str path)","doc":"Puts the variables of a file into the environment, one KEY=value a line,\nso env.load can read them. Blank lines and lines that start with # are\nskipped, and a line may start with export. A # after a space starts a\ncomment. A value may be wrapped in double or single quotes, which are\ntaken off, and then # is part of it. A variable that is already set\nkeeps its value, so the environment wins over the file. A line with no\n=, a name that isn't letters, digits and _, or a quote that isn't closed\nis an EnvError.invalid; a file that can't be read gives the fs error.","file":"env/env.iris","line":42},{"name":"loadInto","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void loadInto(u8 *table, u8 *out)","doc":"What env.load calls.","file":"env/env.iris","line":102},{"name":"loadFileInto","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void loadFileInto(u8 *table, u8 *out, str path)","doc":"What env.loadWith calls.","file":"env/env.iris","line":115}]},{"name":"flags","title":"Flags","group":"System","summary":"Command-line flags into a @flags struct, with --help made from it.","intro":"","doc":"Command-line flags, into a @flags struct.","decls":[{"name":"FlagError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum FlagError { help, invalid }","doc":"What parseArgs fails with: help for -h or --help, with the help as the\nmessage; invalid for a wrong flag or value.","file":"flags/flags.iris","line":7},{"name":"parse","kind":"builtin","sig":"parse","doc":"Fills a @flags struct from the program's arguments: flags.parse(Options).\nEach field is a flag named after it, with - between the words (dryRun is\n--dry-run). @flag on a field gives a letter for the short form, the help\nand required: @flag('p', \"the port to listen on\"). A str[] field with\n@flag(args) gets the arguments that aren't flags. A flag that isn't given\nkeeps the field's default.\n\nValues come as --port 8080, --port=8080 or -p 8080. A bool flag needs no\nvalue (--verbose, or --verbose=false), and one-letter bool flags can be\njoined: -vq. After --, every argument is an argument.\n\n-h and --help print the help made from the struct and end the program.\nWhen something is wrong, it prints what and how to get the help, and the\nprogram ends with status 2.","file":"flags/flags.iris","line":24},{"name":"parseArgs","kind":"builtin","sig":"parseArgs","doc":"Like parse, from the arguments given, and without ending the program:\nflags.parseArgs(AddOptions, args, \"tool add\"). name is shown in the help;\n\"\" uses the program's name. str values point into args. With -h or\n--help it fails with FlagError.help and the help as the message.","file":"flags/flags.iris","line":31},{"name":"parseMain","kind":"func","sig":"pub void parseMain(u8 *table, u8 *out)","doc":"What flags.parse calls.","file":"flags/flags.iris","line":43},{"name":"parseInto","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void parseInto(u8 *table, u8 *out, str[] args, str name)","doc":"What flags.parseArgs calls.","file":"flags/flags.iris","line":58}]},{"name":"signal","title":"Signals","group":"System","summary":"Waiting for Ctrl-C and SIGTERM while the other tasks run.","intro":"","doc":"Signals from the system, like Ctrl-C: a task waits for one while the\nother tasks run, so a server can stop cleanly.","decls":[{"name":"Signal","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Signal { interrupt, terminate, hangup }","doc":"interrupt is Ctrl-C (SIGINT), terminate is a request to stop (SIGTERM,\nwhat systemd and Docker send), hangup is SIGHUP.","file":"signal/signal.iris","line":6},{"name":"watch","kind":"func","sig":"pub void watch(Signal[] which = [])","doc":"From now on these signals no longer stop the program: wait gives them.\nThe start is interrupt and terminate.","file":"signal/signal.iris","line":22},{"name":"wait","kind":"func","sig":"pub Signal wait()","doc":"Waits for the next signal that is watched; watches interrupt and\nterminate when nothing is watched yet.","file":"signal/signal.iris","line":35}]},{"name":"log","title":"Logging","group":"System","summary":"Lines with the time and a level, as text or JSON.","intro":"","doc":"Log lines on standard error, with the time and a level:\n2026-10-17T09:30:00.250Z INFO listening on 8080\nEach line is written at once, so lines from several threads don't mix.","decls":[{"name":"Level","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Level { debug, info, warn, error }","doc":"How much a line matters. setLevel hides the lines below a level.","file":"log/log.iris","line":8},{"name":"Value","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Value { int(i64 v), float(f64 v), text(str v), bool(bool v) }","doc":"The value of a field: log.info(\"signed in\", [(\"user\", .text(name)), (\"id\", .int(7))]).","file":"log/log.iris","line":11},{"name":"setLevel","kind":"func","sig":"pub void setLevel(Level level)","doc":"Lines below level are left out. The start is info.","file":"log/log.iris","line":20},{"name":"useJson","kind":"func","sig":"pub void useJson(bool on = true)","doc":"Writes each line as a JSON object instead:\n{\"time\": \"...\", \"level\": \"info\", \"message\": \"...\", \"user\": \"ada\"}.","file":"log/log.iris","line":26},{"name":"showTime","kind":"func","sig":"pub void showTime(bool on = true)","doc":"Whether lines start with the time. Off suits a program run by systemd or\nDocker, which add the time themselves.","file":"log/log.iris","line":32},{"name":"toFile","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void toFile(str path)","doc":"Writes the lines at the end of the file at path, making it if needed,\ninstead of on standard error.","file":"log/log.iris","line":38},{"name":"toStderr","kind":"func","sig":"pub void toStderr()","doc":"Writes the lines on standard error again, closing the file of toFile.","file":"log/log.iris","line":48},{"name":"write","kind":"func","sig":"pub void write(Level level, str message, (str, Value)[] fields = [])","doc":"Writes a line at level, if the level isn't left out. Each field goes after\nthe message as key=value, text in quotes when it has spaces.","file":"log/log.iris","line":107},{"name":"debug","kind":"func","sig":"pub void debug(str message, (str, Value)[] fields = [])","doc":"Writes message at the debug level: details for finding a problem.","file":"log/log.iris","line":149},{"name":"info","kind":"func","sig":"pub void info(str message, (str, Value)[] fields = [])","doc":"Writes message at the info level: what the program is doing.","file":"log/log.iris","line":152},{"name":"warn","kind":"func","sig":"pub void warn(str message, (str, Value)[] fields = [])","doc":"Writes message at the warn level: something went wrong, and the program goes\non.","file":"log/log.iris","line":156},{"name":"error","kind":"func","sig":"pub void error(str message, (str, Value)[] fields = [])","doc":"Writes message at the error level: something failed.","file":"log/log.iris","line":159}]},{"name":"term","title":"Terminal","group":"System","summary":"Colors as markup, status lines, tables, a progress bar, and lines read with editing.","intro":"<h2 id=\"colors-as-markup\"><a href=\"#colors-as-markup\">Colors as markup</a></h2>\n<p><code>term.print</code> writes a line and turns its markup into colors when standard output is a terminal. Sent to a pipe or a file, the same line is written without the markup, so other programs can read the output:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> term\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    name := <span class=\"s\">&quot;report.pdf&quot;</span>\n    term.<span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;[green]ok[/] saved [bold]</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">[/]&quot;</span>)\n    term.<span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;[yellow on blue] note [/] [[brackets] stay&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> s = term.<span class=\"f\">plain</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;[red]3 failed[/]&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>s<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>term.<span class=\"f\">width</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;\u65e5\u672c&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> columns wide&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>ok saved report.pdf\n note  [brackets] stay\n3 failed, 4 columns wide</code></pre>\n<p><code>[[</code> writes a <code>[</code>. Text from users can hold brackets that look like markup, so pass it through <code>term.escape</code> first: <code>term.print(&quot;hello [bold]{term.escape(name)}[/]&quot;)</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"status-lines\"><a href=\"#status-lines\">Status lines</a></h2>\n<p><code>success</code> and <code>info</code> write a line with a sign in front to standard output. <code>warn</code> and <code>error</code> write to standard error. <code>title</code> writes a bold line that starts a part of the output:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>term.<span class=\"f\">title</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Deploy&quot;</span>)\nterm.<span class=\"f\">info</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;3 services to update&quot;</span>)\nterm.<span class=\"f\">success</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;web is up&quot;</span>)\nterm.<span class=\"f\">warn</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;db took 12s&quot;</span>)\nterm.<span class=\"f\">error</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;cache didn&#x27;t answer&quot;</span>)</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"tables\"><a href=\"#tables\">Tables</a></h2>\n<p><code>term.table</code> takes the headers, <code>row</code> adds a row, and <code>print</code> writes the table with each column as wide as its widest cell. Columns of numbers line up on the right. <code>align</code> sets a column's alignment, and <code>border</code> sets the frame: <code>.none</code>, <code>.box</code> or <code>.markdown</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> term\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> t := term.<span class=\"f\">table</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;NAME&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;STATUS&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;CPU&quot;</span>])\n    t.<span class=\"f\">row</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;web&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;[green]running[/]&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;12.5&quot;</span>])\n    t.<span class=\"f\">row</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;db&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;[red]stopped[/]&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;3&quot;</span>])\n    t.<span class=\"f\">print</span>()\n\n    t.border = .box\n    t.<span class=\"f\">align</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>, .center)\n    t.<span class=\"f\">print</span>()\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>NAME  STATUS    CPU\nweb   running  12.5\ndb    stopped     3\n\u256d\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256e\n\u2502 NAME \u2502 STATUS  \u2502  CPU \u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n\u2502 web  \u2502 running \u2502 12.5 \u2502\n\u2502 db   \u2502 stopped \u2502    3 \u2502\n\u2570\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u256f</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"progress\"><a href=\"#progress\">Progress</a></h2>\n<p><code>term.progress</code> shows a bar on standard error. <code>add</code> or <code>set</code> moves it and <code>done</code> ends it. Lines written with term's functions while it runs appear above it. When standard error isn't a terminal, only the last line is written:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>bar := term.<span class=\"f\">progress</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;downloading&quot;</span>, files.len)\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> f <span class=\"k\">in</span> files {\n    <span class=\"f\">fetch</span>(f)\n    bar.<span class=\"f\">add</span>()\n}\nbar.<span class=\"f\">done</span>()</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"reading-lines\"><a href=\"#reading-lines\">Reading lines</a></h2>\n<p><code>term.readLine</code> shows a prompt and reads a line, with editing and a history:</p>\n<ul><li>Left and Right move in the line. Up and Down go through earlier lines.</li><li>Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-K and Ctrl-W work as in a shell.</li><li><code>historyFile</code> keeps the history between runs.</li><li>At the end of input, or on Ctrl-D, it fails with <code>IoError.eof</code>.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> term\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    term.<span class=\"f\">historyFile</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;.notes_history&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> {\n        line := term.<span class=\"f\">readLine</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;&gt; &quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n            <span class=\"k\">break</span>\n        }\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;you wrote </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>line<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        line.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>readPassword</code> reads a line without showing it. When standard input isn't a terminal, both read plain lines.</p>","doc":"Output for command-line programs: colors written as markup, status\nlines, tables and a progress bar.\n\n    term.print(\"[green]ok[/] saved [bold]{name}[/]\")\n    term.success(\"deployed {n} services\")     // a green \u2713, then the line\n    term.error(\"can't read {path}\")           // a red \u2717, to stderr\n\nA tag is a list of words in brackets: colors (black, red, green, yellow,\nblue, magenta, cyan, white, gray), \"on\" and a color for the background,\nand bold, dim, italic, underline, reverse and strike. [/] closes the last\ntag, and [[ is a [. Brackets around anything else stay as they are.\n\nColors are on when the stream is a terminal, and off for a pipe or a\nfile, when NO_COLOR is set or TERM is dumb; FORCE_COLOR turns them on.\nWith colors off the markup is removed and the text stays.","decls":[{"name":"historyAdd","kind":"func","sig":"pub void historyAdd(str line)","doc":"Adds line to the history, and to the history file, unless it is empty\nor the same as the last one. readLine does this for every line it gives,\nunless historyAuto(false) was called.","file":"term/line.iris","line":45},{"name":"historyFile","kind":"func","sig":"pub void historyFile(str path)","doc":"Keeps the history in the file at path: what is in it now is read, and\neach new line is added to it.","file":"term/line.iris","line":58},{"name":"historyMax","kind":"func","sig":"pub void historyMax(i64 n)","doc":"Keeps at most n lines (1000 to start with).","file":"term/line.iris","line":75},{"name":"historyAuto","kind":"func","sig":"pub void historyAuto(bool on)","doc":"Whether readLine adds each line to the history itself.","file":"term/line.iris","line":84},{"name":"historyLen","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 historyLen()","doc":"How many lines the history has.","file":"term/line.iris","line":89},{"name":"historyAt","kind":"func","sig":"pub str historyAt(i64 i)","doc":"History line i, 0 the oldest.","file":"term/line.iris","line":92},{"name":"historyClear","kind":"func","sig":"pub void historyClear()","doc":"Empties the history; the file stays as it is.","file":"term/line.iris","line":95},{"name":"clear","kind":"func","sig":"pub void clear()","doc":"Clears the screen.","file":"term/line.iris","line":100},{"name":"readLine","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String readLine(str prompt)","doc":"Shows prompt (markup allowed) and reads one line, without its line\nbreak. At the end of input, or Ctrl-D on an empty line, it fails with\nio.IoError.eof.","file":"term/line.iris","line":360},{"name":"readPassword","kind":"func","sig":"pub !String readPassword(str prompt)","doc":"readLine for a secret: nothing is shown as it is typed, and the line\nisn't added to the history. Ctrl-C or Ctrl-D fail with io.IoError.eof.","file":"term/line.iris","line":399},{"name":"Align","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Align { left, right, center }","doc":"How a table column lines up. Without Table.align, a column of numbers\nis on the right and any other on the left.","file":"term/term.iris","line":21},{"name":"Border","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Border { none, box, markdown }","doc":"How a table is drawn: plain columns, lines around every cell, or a\nMarkdown table.","file":"term/term.iris","line":25},{"name":"colors","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool colors()","doc":"Whether stdout gets colors.","file":"term/term.iris","line":76},{"name":"setColors","kind":"func","sig":"pub void setColors(bool on)","doc":"Colors on or off for stdout and stderr, whatever they are.","file":"term/term.iris","line":79},{"name":"interactive","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool interactive()","doc":"Whether stdin and stdout are a terminal a person types at.","file":"term/term.iris","line":84},{"name":"size","kind":"func","sig":"pub (i64, i64) size()","doc":"The terminal's size in columns and rows; 80 by 24 when it isn't one.","file":"term/term.iris","line":90},{"name":"print","kind":"func","sig":"pub void print(str text)","doc":"Writes text with markup and a newline to stdout. Values put into the\ntext with {} are markup too: [[ in them writes a [.","file":"term/term.iris","line":260},{"name":"write","kind":"func","sig":"pub void write(str text)","doc":"print, without the newline.","file":"term/term.iris","line":263},{"name":"eprint","kind":"func","sig":"pub void eprint(str text)","doc":"print, to stderr.","file":"term/term.iris","line":266},{"name":"format","kind":"func","sig":"pub String format(str text)","doc":"The text with its markup made into escape codes when stdout has colors,\nor taken out.","file":"term/term.iris","line":270},{"name":"plain","kind":"func","sig":"pub String plain(str text)","doc":"The text without its markup.","file":"term/term.iris","line":273},{"name":"escape","kind":"func","sig":"pub String escape(str text)","doc":"Text with [ written as [[, so markup shows it as it is.","file":"term/term.iris","line":280},{"name":"success","kind":"func","sig":"pub void success(str text)","doc":"One line on stdout after a green \u2713.","file":"term/term.iris","line":289},{"name":"info","kind":"func","sig":"pub void info(str text)","doc":"One line on stdout after a blue \u2022.","file":"term/term.iris","line":292},{"name":"warn","kind":"func","sig":"pub void warn(str text)","doc":"One line on stderr after a yellow !.","file":"term/term.iris","line":295},{"name":"error","kind":"func","sig":"pub void error(str text)","doc":"One line on stderr after a red \u2717.","file":"term/term.iris","line":298},{"name":"title","kind":"func","sig":"pub void title(str text)","doc":"A bold line on stdout, to start a part of the output.","file":"term/term.iris","line":301},{"name":"width","kind":"func","sig":"pub i64 width(str text)","doc":"The columns text takes on a terminal: its characters without the\nmarkup.","file":"term/term.iris","line":358},{"name":"Table","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Table {\n    pub Border border\n}","doc":"A table: rows of cells under a header, in lined-up columns.\n\n    scoped t := term.table([\"NAME\", \"STATUS\", \"CPU\"])\n    t.row([\"web\", \"[green]running[/]\", \"12.5\"])\n    t.print()","file":"term/term.iris","line":376},{"name":"Table.row","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Table.row(str[] cells)","doc":"Adds a row. Cells may hold markup. A column whose cells are all numbers\nlines up on the right.","file":"term/term.iris","line":399},{"name":"Table.align","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Table.align(i64 col, Align a)","doc":"Lines column col (from 0) up on the left, the right or the center.","file":"term/term.iris","line":423},{"name":"Table.len","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Table.len()","doc":"How many rows were added; the header isn't one.","file":"term/term.iris","line":435},{"name":"Table.format","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Table.format(bool color = false)","doc":"The table as text. With colors, the header is bold and the markup\nbecomes escape codes; without, the markup is taken out.","file":"term/term.iris","line":489},{"name":"Table.print","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Table.print()","doc":"Writes the table to stdout.","file":"term/term.iris","line":558},{"name":"Table.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Table.free()","doc":"Frees the table's cells.","file":"term/term.iris","line":561},{"name":"table","kind":"func","sig":"pub Table table(str[] headers)","doc":"A table with these column headers ([] for one without a header).","file":"term/term.iris","line":386},{"name":"Progress","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Progress { }","doc":"A progress bar on stderr, drawn again in place on a terminal. Lines\nwritten with term's functions while it runs go above it. Not on a\nterminal, only the last line is written.","file":"term/term.iris","line":575},{"name":"Progress.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Progress.set(i64 n)","doc":"Sets how many steps are done, and draws the bar again when its percent\nchanged or 100 ms went by.","file":"term/term.iris","line":630},{"name":"Progress.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Progress.add(i64 n = 1)","doc":"n more steps done.","file":"term/term.iris","line":647},{"name":"Progress.done","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Progress.done()","doc":"Draws the last line, with the time it took.","file":"term/term.iris","line":650},{"name":"progress","kind":"func","sig":"pub Progress progress(str label, i64 total)","doc":"A progress bar for total steps of work called label. Keep it in one\nplace, and call done at the end.","file":"term/term.iris","line":587}]},{"name":"net","title":"Networking","group":"Network","summary":"TCP, UDP, TLS, addresses and looking up names.","intro":"<h2 id=\"a-tcp-server-and-client\"><a href=\"#a-tcp-server-and-client\">A TCP server and client</a></h2>\n<p><code>net.listen</code> takes a port and gives a <code>Listener</code>, whose <code>accept</code> waits for the next connection. Each connection usually gets a task of its own, so one slow client doesn't hold up the others. <code>net.dial</code> connects to a server. A <code>Conn</code> reads with <code>read</code>, <code>readLine</code> and <code>readExactly</code>, and writes with <code>write</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">handle</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Conn</span> c) {\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> c.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> {\n        line := c.<span class=\"f\">readLine</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n        }\n        c.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(<span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;echo: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>line<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n            line.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n        }\n        line.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">server</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Listener</span> ln) {\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> {\n        conn := ln.<span class=\"f\">accept</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n        }\n        <span class=\"k\">spawn</span> <span class=\"f\">handle</span>(conn)\n    }\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    ln := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">spawn</span> <span class=\"f\">server</span>(ln)\n\n    c := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, ln.port)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> c.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;hello</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">again</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> a = <span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">readLine</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> b = <span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">readLine</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>a<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> | </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>b<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>echo: hello | echo: again</code></pre>\n<p>Port 0 lets the system pick a free port, which <code>ln.port</code> then holds. A host of <code>&quot;&quot;</code> listens on every address. Calls wait as any wait in a task does, and other tasks run until data comes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-is-connected\"><a href=\"#who-is-connected\">Who is connected</a></h2>\n<p><code>remoteAddr</code> gives the address of the other side of a connection, which on a server is the client. <code>localAddr</code> gives this side's address. Both are an <code>Addr</code>, with <code>ipText</code> and <code>port</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    ln := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    c := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, ln.port)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> c.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    s := <span class=\"k\">try</span> ln.<span class=\"f\">accept</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> s.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    client := s.<span class=\"f\">remoteAddr</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> ip = client.<span class=\"f\">ipText</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ip<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>client.port == c.<span class=\"f\">localAddr</span>().port<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>127.0.0.1 true</code></pre>\n<p><code>fd</code> gives a connection's file descriptor, for calls through <code>import c</code>. The event loop owns it, so reading, writing or closing it directly breaks the connection. <code>received</code> and <code>sent</code> count the bytes that came in and went out.</p>\n<h2 id=\"timeouts-and-errors\"><a href=\"#timeouts-and-errors\">Timeouts and errors</a></h2>\n<p><code>setTimeout</code> sets how long each read or write on a connection may wait. Past it, the call fails with <code>NetError.timeout</code>. <code>dial</code> takes a timeout for connecting. A connection to a port where nothing listens fails with <code>NetError.refused</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    _ = net.<span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">1</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        net.<span class=\"t\">Conn</span>{}\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>NetError.refused</code></pre>\n<p><code>readLine</code> takes a limit on a line's length and fails with <code>NetError.tooLong</code> past it, so a client can't make a server hold an endless line.</p>\n<h2 id=\"socket-options\"><a href=\"#socket-options\">Socket options</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>Conn</code> sets the common TCP options with methods:</p>\n<ul><li><code>setNoDelay</code>, which is on for every connection from <code>net</code>, so small writes go out at once.</li><li><code>setKeepAlive</code>, with the idle time, the interval between probes, and the number of unanswered probes before the connection drops.</li><li><code>setBuffers</code>, for the kernel's buffers.</li></ul>\n<p><code>setOption</code> and <code>option</code> set and read any int option by the level and name C uses, taken from the system's headers with <code>import c</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n<span class=\"k\">import</span> c <span class=\"s\">&quot;sys/socket.h&quot;</span>\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    ln := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    conn := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, ln.port)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> conn.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> conn.<span class=\"f\">setKeepAlive</span>(<span class=\"n\">30s</span>, <span class=\"n\">10s</span>, <span class=\"n\">3</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;SO_KEEPALIVE: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span><span class=\"k\">try</span> conn.<span class=\"f\">option</span>(c.SOL_SOCKET, c.SO_KEEPALIVE) != <span class=\"n\">0</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> conn.<span class=\"f\">setOption</span>(c.SOL_SOCKET, c.SO_KEEPALIVE, <span class=\"n\">0</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;SO_KEEPALIVE: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span><span class=\"k\">try</span> conn.<span class=\"f\">option</span>(c.SOL_SOCKET, c.SO_KEEPALIVE) != <span class=\"n\">0</span><span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>SO_KEEPALIVE: true\nSO_KEEPALIVE: false</code></pre>\n<p><code>Listener</code> has <code>setOption</code> and <code>option</code> too. <code>setOptionBytes</code> and <code>optionBytes</code> take an option whose value is a struct, such as <code>SO_LINGER</code> or <code>TCP_INFO</code>, as bytes. <code>net.setOption(fd, ...)</code> works on any socket's descriptor.</p>\n<h2 id=\"setting-up-a-socket\"><a href=\"#setting-up-a-socket\">Setting up a socket</a></h2>\n<p>Some options only take effect when set before a socket binds, listens or connects: <code>SO_REUSEPORT</code>, <code>IP_TRANSPARENT</code>, <code>TCP_FASTOPEN</code>, and the buffer sizes that set TCP's window. <code>net.socket</code> gives a <code>Socket</code> for that:</p>\n<ol><li>Set the options on the <code>Socket</code>.</li><li><code>bind</code> gives it an address. On a client, <code>bind</code> picks the address and port the connection comes from.</li><li><code>listen</code> turns it into a <code>Listener</code>, or <code>connect</code> into a <code>Conn</code>.</li></ol>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    s := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">socket</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> s.<span class=\"f\">setBuffers</span>(read: <span class=\"n\">1MiB</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> s.<span class=\"f\">bind</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Addr</span>{port: <span class=\"n\">0</span>})\n    ln := <span class=\"k\">try</span> s.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(backlog: <span class=\"n\">4096</span>)\n\n    cs := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">socket</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Family</span>.v4)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> cs.<span class=\"f\">bind</span>(<span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">ip</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">0</span>))\n    conn := <span class=\"k\">try</span> cs.<span class=\"f\">connect</span>(<span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">ip</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, ln.port))\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> conn.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    srv := <span class=\"k\">try</span> ln.<span class=\"f\">accept</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> srv.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>srv.<span class=\"f\">remoteAddr</span>().port == conn.<span class=\"f\">localAddr</span>().port<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>true</code></pre>\n<p><code>net.Addr{port: 8080}</code> is every address. A <code>Socket</code> is IPv6 and also takes IPv4, unless made with <code>Family.v4</code> or <code>Family.v6</code> for one of them only. <code>net.listen</code> with a host of <code>&quot;&quot;</code> listens on IPv6 and IPv4 at once.</p>\n<h2 id=\"half-closing-and-copying\"><a href=\"#half-closing-and-copying\">Half-closing and copying</a></h2>\n<p><code>closeWrite</code> ends this side's writes. The other side reads the end of the data, and this side can still read what the other side sends. A request that means \"everything until you close\" works this way, and so does a proxy, which passes on the end of each direction separately.</p>\n<p><code>net.copy(to, from)</code> copies what one connection reads to another until the first one's data ends, and gives the number of bytes. Small messages go through a buffer. On Linux, when neither connection uses TLS, a steady stream goes from socket to socket inside the kernel with <code>splice</code>, without passing through the program. A TCP proxy is two copies, one each way:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">upper</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Listener</span> ln) {\n    c := ln.<span class=\"f\">accept</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> c.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> line = c.<span class=\"f\">readLine</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    c.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>line<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> (from the backend)</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e { }\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">oneWay</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Conn</span> *to, net.<span class=\"t\">Conn</span> *from, <span class=\"k\">chan</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>] done) {\n    n := net.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(to, from) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        done &lt;- -<span class=\"n\">1</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    to.<span class=\"f\">closeWrite</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e { }\n    done &lt;- n\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">proxy</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Listener</span> ln, <span class=\"t\">u16</span> backend) {\n    client := ln.<span class=\"f\">accept</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> client.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    server := net.<span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, backend, timeout: <span class=\"n\">2s</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> server.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    done := <span class=\"k\">chan</span>[<span class=\"t\">i64</span>](<span class=\"n\">2</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> done.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">spawn</span> <span class=\"f\">oneWay</span>(&amp;server, &amp;client, done)\n    <span class=\"f\">oneWay</span>(&amp;client, &amp;server, done)\n    _ = &lt;-done\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    back := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">spawn</span> <span class=\"f\">upper</span>(back)\n    front := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">spawn</span> <span class=\"f\">proxy</span>(front, back.port)\n\n    c := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, front.port)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> c.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;hello</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">closeWrite</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> reply = <span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">readLine</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(reply)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>hello (from the backend)</code></pre>\n<p>Each copy waits for data as long as its connection's <code>setTimeout</code> allows, so that timeout works as an idle timeout. <code>limit</code> stops a copy after that many bytes. <code>received</code> and <code>sent</code> keep counting through an error, so a log can show what a connection moved.</p>\n<h2 id=\"looking-at-the-first-bytes\"><a href=\"#looking-at-the-first-bytes\">Looking at the first bytes</a></h2>\n<p><code>peek(n)</code> gives the next n bytes without taking them, so a server can tell what a connection is before it decides who handles it. On a connection without TLS the bytes stay in the socket, so <code>acceptTls</code> and <code>copy</code> still get them.</p>\n<p><code>peekServerName</code> reads a TLS client's first message and gives the host name it asks for (SNI). One port can then send each name to its own backend with TLS still in place, or end TLS for some names and pass others on:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">route</span>(net.<span class=\"t\">Conn</span> client) {\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> client.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    client.<span class=\"f\">setTimeout</span>(<span class=\"n\">5s</span>)\n\n    name := client.<span class=\"f\">peekServerName</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    port := <span class=\"k\">if</span> name == <span class=\"s\">&quot;api.example.com&quot;</span> { <span class=\"n\">9001</span> } <span class=\"k\">else</span> { <span class=\"n\">9002</span> }\n\n    backend := net.<span class=\"f\">dial</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"t\">u16</span>(port)) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> backend.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    <span class=\"c\">// copies both ways, as in the proxy above</span>\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>net.helloServerName(record)</code> reads the name from a TLS record held elsewhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tls\"><a href=\"#tls\">TLS</a></h2>\n<p><code>net.dialTls</code> connects and starts TLS. It checks the server's certificate against the system's certificates, or the ones in a <code>ca</code> file. The host is the name or IP the certificate must hold, so it can't be empty. A server makes a <code>TlsConfig</code> from a certificate and its key and calls <code>acceptTls</code> on each connection:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>c := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dialTls</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;example.com&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">443</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">defer</span> c.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n<span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">write</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;HEAD / HTTP/1.1</span><span class=\"e\">\\r</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">Host: example.com</span><span class=\"e\">\\r</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">Connection: close</span><span class=\"e\">\\r</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"e\">\\r</span><span class=\"e\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)</code></pre>\n<p><code>addCert</code> gives a server more certificates. Each client gets the certificate for the name it asks for, wildcards like <code>*.example.com</code> included. A client that asks for a name no certificate holds gets the first one. <code>serverName</code> says which name a client asked for.</p>\n<p><code>reload</code> reads every certificate and key again from their files while the server runs, as after a renewal. Open connections keep what they had, and a file that fails to read leaves the old certificates in place:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>config := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">tlsConfig</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;certs/example.com.pem&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;certs/example.com.key&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">try</span> config.<span class=\"f\">addCert</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;certs/api.example.org.pem&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;certs/api.example.org.key&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">try</span> config.<span class=\"f\">addCert</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;certs/wildcard.example.net.pem&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;certs/wildcard.example.net.key&quot;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"c\">// later, from a task that waits for SIGHUP</span>\nconfig.<span class=\"f\">reload</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n    log.<span class=\"f\">error</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;certificates: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.message<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>verifyClients(ca)</code> makes a server ask each client for a certificate signed by one in <code>ca</code>, and turn away a client without one. With <code>required: false</code> a client may send none. <code>peerSubject</code> gives the subject of the other side's certificate. A client shows its own certificate with <code>cert</code> and <code>key</code> on <code>dialTls</code> or <code>startTls</code>. <code>offerProtocols</code> sets the protocols a server offers with ALPN.</p>\n<h2 id=\"udp\"><a href=\"#udp\">UDP</a></h2>\n<p><code>listenUdp</code> gives a socket that receives on a port. <code>readFrom</code> gives each packet with the address it came from, and <code>sendTo</code> sends one. <code>dialUdp</code> gives a socket that sends to one address with <code>send</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    srv := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listenUdp</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> srv.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    to := srv.<span class=\"f\">localAddr</span>()\n\n    cl := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dialUdp</span>(to)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> cl.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> cl.<span class=\"f\">send</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;ping&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">64</span>] buf\n    n, from := <span class=\"k\">try</span> srv.<span class=\"f\">readFrom</span>(buf)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>buf[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:n]<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> from 127.0.0.1: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>from.<span class=\"f\">isV4</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>ping from 127.0.0.1: true</code></pre>\n<p>Several sockets can listen on the same UDP port, such as one per thread. The kernel gives each datagram to one of them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"many-datagrams-at-once\"><a href=\"#many-datagrams-at-once\">Many datagrams at once</a></h2>\n<p>A busy UDP server spends much of its time in system calls, one per datagram. <code>readMany</code> waits for a datagram and then takes all waiting ones, up to the number of <code>Datagram</code>s it is given, with one system call on Linux. Each <code>Datagram</code> has room for one datagram in <code>buf</code>, and <code>readMany</code> sets its <code>len</code> and the <code>addr</code> it came from. <code>sendMany</code> sends a batch the same way, so a batch that was read can be sent back as it is:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> net\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    srv := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listenUdp</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> srv.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    cl := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dialUdp</span>(srv.<span class=\"f\">localAddr</span>())\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> cl.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> word <span class=\"k\">in</span> [<span class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;bb&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;ccc&quot;</span>] {\n        <span class=\"k\">try</span> cl.<span class=\"f\">send</span>(word)\n    }\n    <span class=\"f\">sleep</span>(<span class=\"n\">20ms</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">4096</span>] space\n    net.<span class=\"t\">Datagram</span>[<span class=\"n\">4</span>] batch\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> i <span class=\"k\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">0</span>..<span class=\"n\">4</span> {\n        batch[i].buf = space[i * <span class=\"n\">1024</span>:(i + <span class=\"n\">1</span>) * <span class=\"n\">1024</span>]\n    }\n\n    n := <span class=\"k\">try</span> srv.<span class=\"f\">readMany</span>(batch)\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> d <span class=\"k\">in</span> batch[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:n] {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>d.<span class=\"f\">data</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> (</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>d.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes)&quot;</span>)\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> srv.<span class=\"f\">sendMany</span>(batch[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:n])\n    <span class=\"f\">sleep</span>(<span class=\"n\">20ms</span>)\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">64</span>] buf\n    k := <span class=\"k\">try</span> cl.<span class=\"f\">read</span>(buf)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;back: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>buf[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:k]<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>a (1 bytes)\nbb (2 bytes)\nccc (3 bytes)\nback: a</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"names\"><a href=\"#names\">Names</a></h2>\n<p><code>net.lookup</code> gives the addresses of a host name with the system's resolver. <code>net.query</code> asks DNS for records of a kind, such as MX or TXT. A <code>Resolver</code> asks given servers instead of the system's. The <a href=\"#dns\">dns module</a> reads and writes the messages themselves, for a program that is a DNS server:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>addrs := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">lookup</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;example.com&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">defer</span> addrs.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> a <span class=\"k\">in</span> addrs {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> t = a.<span class=\"f\">ipText</span>()\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(t)\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"unix-sockets-and-passing-sockets-on\"><a href=\"#unix-sockets-and-passing-sockets-on\">Unix sockets and passing sockets on</a></h2>\n<p><code>listenUnix</code> and <code>dialUnix</code> work like <code>listen</code> and <code>dial</code>, on a path. On Linux, a path that starts with <code>@</code> has no file.</p>\n<p>Over a Unix socket, <code>sendFds</code> sends open file descriptors along with some bytes, and <code>readFds</code> receives them as new descriptors. <code>adoptListener</code> and <code>adoptConn</code> turn a descriptor from elsewhere into a <code>Listener</code> or a <code>Conn</code>. A program can hand its listening sockets to the next version of itself this way, so a restart drops no connection:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> fs, net, os\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> path = <span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/tmp/iris-doc-</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>os.<span class=\"f\">pid</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">.sock&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(path) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e { }\n    handover := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listenUnix</span>(path)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> handover.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    tcp := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n\n    sender := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">dialUnix</span>(path)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> sender.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> sender.<span class=\"f\">sendFds</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;listener&quot;</span>, [tcp.<span class=\"f\">fd</span>()])\n\n    receiver := <span class=\"k\">try</span> handover.<span class=\"f\">accept</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> receiver.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    <span class=\"t\">u8</span>[<span class=\"n\">64</span>] buf\n    <span class=\"t\">i32</span>[<span class=\"n\">1</span>] fds\n    n, k := <span class=\"k\">try</span> receiver.<span class=\"f\">readFds</span>(buf, fds)\n\n    took := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">adoptListener</span>(fds[<span class=\"n\">0</span>])\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>buf[<span class=\"n\">0</span>:n]<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>k<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> descriptor, port kept: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>took.port == tcp.port<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>listener: 1 descriptor, port kept: true</code></pre>\n<p><code>net.waitRead</code> and <code>net.waitWrite</code> wait in the event loop until any descriptor is ready, and let the other tasks run meanwhile. They are for calls through <code>import c</code> that would otherwise block the thread.</p>","doc":"TCP and UDP: listening, connecting, reading and writing, also over TLS,\nand looking up names. A task that waits for the network lets the other\ntasks run.","decls":[{"name":"RecordType","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum RecordType { a, ns, cname, soa, ptr, mx, txt, aaaa, srv }","doc":"The kinds of DNS record query can ask for.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":8},{"name":"Record","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Record {\n    pub String name\n    pub RecordType kind\n    pub Duration ttl\n    pub Addr ip\n    pub String target\n    pub i64 priority\n    pub i64 weight\n    pub u16 port\n}","doc":"One answer to a query.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":11},{"name":"Record.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Record.free()","doc":"Frees the record's name and target.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":28},{"name":"Resolver","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Resolver {\n    pub List[Addr] servers\n    pub Duration timeout\n    pub i64 attempts\n    pub List[String] search\n    pub i64 ndots\n}","doc":"Where names are looked up.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":59},{"name":"Resolver.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Resolver.free()","doc":"Frees the resolver's servers, search domains and hosts.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":100},{"name":"Resolver.query","kind":"method","sig":"pub !List[Record] Resolver.query(str name, RecordType t)","doc":"The records of a kind for name. A name that isn't there fails with\nNetError.notFound.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":365},{"name":"Resolver.lookup","kind":"method","sig":"pub !List[Addr] Resolver.lookup(str host)","doc":"The addresses of host: an IP as it is, then the hosts file, then DNS,\nasking for IPv4 and IPv6 at once. The IPv4 addresses come first.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":405},{"name":"resolver","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Resolver resolver(str[] servers = [], str hostsFile = \"\")","doc":"A resolver for servers (\"1.1.1.1\", \"[2001:db8::53]:5353\"); with none,\nthe ones in /etc/resolv.conf. hostsFile is read for names it knows\nalready (\"\" for /etc/hosts).","file":"net/dns.iris","line":78},{"name":"lookup","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[Addr] lookup(str host)","doc":"The addresses of host, with the system's resolver.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":474},{"name":"query","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[Record] query(str name, RecordType t)","doc":"The records of a kind for name, with the system's resolver.","file":"net/dns.iris","line":477},{"name":"NetError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum NetError { refused, closed, timeout, notFound, inUse, other, tls, tooLong }","doc":"What network calls fail with: refused, closed, timeout, notFound (for a name\nthat doesn't resolve), inUse (for an address taken already), tls, tooLong\n(for a line past its limit), and other.","file":"net/net.iris","line":10},{"name":"Listener","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Listener {\n    pub u16 port\n}","doc":"A socket that accepts connections.","file":"net/net.iris","line":117},{"name":"Listener.accept","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Conn Listener.accept(Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Waits for the next connection, for at most timeout when it isn't 0.","file":"net/net.iris","line":168},{"name":"Listener.fd","kind":"method","sig":"pub i32 Listener.fd()","doc":"The listener's file descriptor, for socket options and calls through\nimport c. Accepting on it or closing it directly breaks the listener.","file":"net/net.iris","line":179},{"name":"Listener.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Listener.close()","doc":"Stops listening and closes the socket.","file":"net/net.iris","line":182},{"name":"Listener.interrupt","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Listener.interrupt()","doc":"Wakes a task that waits in accept, which then fails: for stopping a\nserver.","file":"net/net.iris","line":186},{"name":"Listener.setOption","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Listener.setOption(i32 level, i32 name, i32 value)","doc":"Sets an option of the listening socket that is an int.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":196},{"name":"Listener.option","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i32 Listener.option(i32 level, i32 name)","doc":"The value of an option of the listening socket that is an int.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":199},{"name":"Conn","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Conn {\n    pub i64 received\n    pub i64 sent\n}","doc":"A connection. Reads go through a buffer, so lines can be read one at a time.\nreceived and sent count the bytes that came in and went out, over TLS the\nbytes of the data.","file":"net/net.iris","line":125},{"name":"Conn.setTimeout","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Conn.setTimeout(Duration d)","doc":"Each read and write after this fails with NetError.timeout when it waits\nlonger than d; 0 is the start, no limit.","file":"net/net.iris","line":138},{"name":"Conn.read","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Conn.read(u8[] buf)","doc":"Reads into buf: the number of bytes, 0 when the other side has closed.","file":"net/net.iris","line":234},{"name":"Conn.readLine","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String Conn.readLine(i64 limit = 0)","doc":"The next line, without its line break (\\n or \\r\\n). NetError.closed at the\nend; NetError.tooLong when limit isn't 0 and the line is longer.","file":"net/net.iris","line":266},{"name":"Conn.readExactly","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String Conn.readExactly(i64 n)","doc":"Reads exactly n bytes into a new String.","file":"net/net.iris","line":303},{"name":"Conn.readAll","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String Conn.readAll(i64 limit = 0)","doc":"Everything the other side sends until it closes, as a new String.\nNetError.tooLong when limit isn't 0 and more than limit bytes come.","file":"net/net.iris","line":320},{"name":"Conn.buffered","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Conn.buffered()","doc":"The bytes read from the connection and not taken yet, as a view of its\nbuffer: no copy, valid until the next read. With readMore and skip, a\nprotocol can look at what came before it takes it.","file":"net/net.iris","line":336},{"name":"Conn.readMore","kind":"method","sig":"pub !bool Conn.readMore()","doc":"Reads more into the buffer and keeps what buffered() had, moved to the\nfront when the buffer's end is reached. False when the other side has\nclosed or the buffer is full.","file":"net/net.iris","line":341},{"name":"Conn.skip","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Conn.skip(i64 n)","doc":"Takes the first n bytes of buffered(), at most all of them.","file":"net/net.iris","line":362},{"name":"Conn.bufferSize","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Conn.bufferSize()","doc":"The size of the read buffer: how much buffered() can hold.","file":"net/net.iris","line":367},{"name":"Conn.write","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.write(str data)","doc":"Writes all of data.","file":"net/net.iris","line":370},{"name":"Conn.remoteAddr","kind":"method","sig":"pub Addr Conn.remoteAddr()","doc":"The address of the other side: the client on a server, the server on a\nclient. All zeros once the connection is gone.","file":"net/net.iris","line":384},{"name":"Conn.localAddr","kind":"method","sig":"pub Addr Conn.localAddr()","doc":"The address of this side of the connection.","file":"net/net.iris","line":393},{"name":"Conn.fd","kind":"method","sig":"pub i32 Conn.fd()","doc":"The connection's file descriptor, for socket options and calls through\nimport c. It belongs to the event loop and doesn't block: reading,\nwriting or closing it directly, or making it blocking, breaks the\nconnection and can stall every task on the thread.","file":"net/net.iris","line":405},{"name":"Conn.interrupt","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Conn.interrupt()","doc":"Ends the connection's reads and writes from another task: a task that\nwaits on it wakes up as if the other side had closed. close still has to\nbe called.","file":"net/net.iris","line":410},{"name":"Conn.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Conn.close()","doc":"Closes the connection.","file":"net/net.iris","line":413},{"name":"Conn.startTls","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.startTls(str host, str ca = \"\", str protocols = \"\", str cert = \"\", str key = \"\")","doc":"Starts TLS as the client on a connection that is open, as dialTls does.\nhost is the name, or the IP, the server's certificate must have; it\ncan't be empty. A name is also sent to the server (SNI).\nprotocols are offered to the server with ALPN, each with its length in\nfront: \"\\x02h2\\x08http/1.1\"; protocol() says which one it took. cert and\nkey are the client's own certificate and key; key \"\" means the key is in\ncert.","file":"net/net.iris","line":445},{"name":"Conn.protocol","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Conn.protocol()","doc":"The protocol the two sides of a TLS connection agreed on with ALPN, as\n\"h2\"; \"\" when they didn't.","file":"net/net.iris","line":456},{"name":"Conn.acceptTls","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.acceptTls(TlsConfig *config)","doc":"Starts TLS as the server on an accepted connection.","file":"net/net.iris","line":530},{"name":"Conn.serverName","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Conn.serverName()","doc":"The name the client asked for in its TLS handshake (SNI), on a server:\n\"\" when it asked for none.","file":"net/net.iris","line":540},{"name":"Conn.peerSubject","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Conn.peerSubject()","doc":"The subject of the certificate the other side showed, as\n\"/CN=client.example.com\"; \"\" when it showed none.","file":"net/net.iris","line":549},{"name":"Conn.setOption","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.setOption(i32 level, i32 name, i32 value)","doc":"Sets an option of the connection that is an int, as setOption does:\nconn.setOption(c.IPPROTO_TCP, c.TCP_KEEPIDLE, 30).","file":"net/socket.iris","line":203},{"name":"Conn.option","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i32 Conn.option(i32 level, i32 name)","doc":"The value of an option of the connection that is an int.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":206},{"name":"Conn.setNoDelay","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.setNoDelay(bool on)","doc":"Turns TCP_NODELAY on or off. net's connections start with it on, so\nsmall writes go out at once.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":210},{"name":"Conn.setKeepAlive","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.setKeepAlive(Duration idle, Duration interval = 0s, i64 count = 0)","doc":"Turns TCP keepalive on: probes after idle without traffic, every\ninterval, count of them unanswered before the connection is dropped. 0\nfor interval or count keeps the system's. idle 0s turns keepalive off.\nTimes are in whole seconds.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":218},{"name":"Conn.setBuffers","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.setBuffers(i64 read = 0, i64 write = 0)","doc":"The kernel's buffers for reading and writing, in bytes; 0 leaves one as\nit is.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":225},{"name":"Conn.peek","kind":"method","sig":"pub !str Conn.peek(i64 n)","doc":"The next n bytes, without taking them: the reads after still get them,\nand so do acceptTls and copy. Fewer than n only when the other side has\nended its writes, or the timeout passed. They stay valid until the next\nread. For deciding what a connection is from its first bytes.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":246},{"name":"Conn.peekServerName","kind":"method","sig":"pub !str Conn.peekServerName()","doc":"The name a TLS client asks for (SNI), read from its first message with\npeek, so the connection can be routed with TLS still in it. \"\" when the\nconnection doesn't start with a TLS ClientHello, or it names no host.\nValid until the next read.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":289},{"name":"Conn.closeWrite","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.closeWrite()","doc":"Ends this side's writes, so the other side reads the end of the data,\nwhile reads here go on until the other side ends its own. Over TLS it\nsends close_notify first. close still has to be called.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":352},{"name":"Conn.sendFds","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.sendFds(str data, i32[] fds)","doc":"Sends data, and file descriptors with it, over a Unix socket: the other\nside gets copies of them with readFds. data can't be empty. At most 250\ndescriptors.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":445},{"name":"Conn.readFds","kind":"method","sig":"pub !(i64, i64) Conn.readFds(u8[] buf, i32[] fds)","doc":"Reads into buf, and the descriptors that came with the bytes into fds:\nthe number of bytes (0 when the other side has closed) and of\ndescriptors. Descriptors past fds's length are closed. Bytes taken in\nbefore, with peek or readLine, come first, without descriptors.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":460},{"name":"listen","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Listener listen(u16 port, str host = \"\", bool shared = false)","doc":"Listens on port (0 picks a free one; see .port) on host (\"\" for all\naddresses, IPv6 and IPv4). shared lets several threads, or programs,\nlisten on the same port. A Socket sets up the rest: options before it\nbinds, the backlog.","file":"net/net.iris","line":158},{"name":"dial","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Conn dial(str host, u16 port, Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Connects to host:port, waiting at most timeout when it isn't 0. host is\na name or an IP. Each of the name's addresses is tried in turn until one\nanswers.","file":"net/net.iris","line":191},{"name":"dialAddr","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Conn dialAddr(Addr addr, Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Connects to an address, waiting at most timeout when it isn't 0.","file":"net/net.iris","line":223},{"name":"dialTls","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Conn dialTls(str host, u16 port, str ca = \"\", str cert = \"\", str key = \"\")","doc":"Connects to host:port over TLS. The server's certificate must be valid\nfor host and signed by a certificate the system trusts, or one in the\nfile ca (PEM) when ca is given. cert and key are the client's own\ncertificate and key (PEM files), for servers that ask for one.","file":"net/net.iris","line":429},{"name":"TlsConfig","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct TlsConfig { }","doc":"A server's certificates and keys, for accepting TLS connections. It can be\nshared by every thread, and changed while they use it, one change at a\ntime: a handshake uses what it was when the handshake started. A change\nof settings keeps the certificates loaded already; only addCert, for its\nnew one, and reload read files.","file":"net/net.iris","line":468},{"name":"TlsConfig.offerHttp2","kind":"method","sig":"pub void TlsConfig.offerHttp2()","doc":"Lets clients ask for HTTP/2 with ALPN; http's servers call it.","file":"net/net.iris","line":484},{"name":"TlsConfig.addCert","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void TlsConfig.addCert(str certFile, str keyFile)","doc":"Adds a certificate and its key (PEM files), used for the clients that ask\nfor a name it has (SNI). Its names come from the certificate, wildcards\nlike *.example.com too.","file":"net/net.iris","line":489},{"name":"TlsConfig.reload","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void TlsConfig.reload()","doc":"Reads every certificate and key again from their files, as after they\nwere renewed. Connections that are open keep the ones they had. On an\nerror the config keeps the ones it had.","file":"net/net.iris","line":498},{"name":"TlsConfig.verifyClients","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void TlsConfig.verifyClients(str ca, bool required = true)","doc":"Asks each client for a certificate signed by one in the file ca (PEM).\nWith required, a client without one fails the handshake; without it, the\nclient may send none. A certificate that isn't signed by ca always fails.\npeerSubject says who the client is.","file":"net/net.iris","line":508},{"name":"TlsConfig.offerProtocols","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void TlsConfig.offerProtocols(str protocols)","doc":"The protocols offered with ALPN, each with its length in front:\n\"\\x02h2\\x08http/1.1\". The server takes the first of them that the client\noffers too.","file":"net/net.iris","line":517},{"name":"TlsConfig.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void TlsConfig.free()","doc":"Frees the certificate and the key.","file":"net/net.iris","line":524},{"name":"tlsConfig","kind":"func","sig":"pub !TlsConfig tlsConfig(str certFile, str keyFile)","doc":"Reads a certificate chain and its private key from PEM files. This is\nthe certificate for clients that ask for no name, or for a name no other\ncertificate has.","file":"net/net.iris","line":475},{"name":"yield","kind":"func","sig":"pub void yield()","doc":"Lets the other tasks run.","file":"net/net.iris","line":556},{"name":"setOption","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void setOption(i32 fd, i32 level, i32 name, i32 value)","doc":"Sets an option of the socket fd that is an int, as setsockopt does:\nlevel and name are C's, from import c (\"sys/socket.h\",\n\"netinet/tcp.h\"...).","file":"net/socket.iris","line":82},{"name":"option","kind":"func","sig":"pub !i32 option(i32 fd, i32 level, i32 name)","doc":"The value of an option of the socket fd that is an int.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":87},{"name":"setOptionBytes","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void setOptionBytes(i32 fd, i32 level, i32 name, u8[] value)","doc":"Sets an option that isn't an int, such as SO_LINGER's struct, from its\nbytes.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":95},{"name":"optionBytes","kind":"func","sig":"pub !i64 optionBytes(i32 fd, i32 level, i32 name, u8[] buf)","doc":"Reads an option that isn't an int into buf: the number of bytes it took.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":100},{"name":"waitRead","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool waitRead(i32 fd, Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Waits until fd can be read, letting the other tasks run: false when\ntimeout (0 for none) passed first. For sockets and pipes used through\nimport c, after a call on them failed with EAGAIN.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":109},{"name":"waitWrite","kind":"func","sig":"pub bool waitWrite(i32 fd, Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Waits until fd can be written, as waitRead does.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":112},{"name":"Family","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Family { any, v4, v6 }","doc":"The kind of address a Socket has: any is IPv6 that takes IPv4 too\n(IPv4 alone where there is no IPv6), v4 and v6 one of them only.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":116},{"name":"Socket","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Socket { }","doc":"A TCP socket that doesn't listen or connect yet, for the options that\nhave to be set before: SO_REUSEPORT, IP_TRANSPARENT, TCP_FASTOPEN,\nbuffer sizes, a source address with bind. listen or connect turns it\ninto a Listener or a Conn. It starts with SO_REUSEADDR and TCP_NODELAY\non.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":123},{"name":"Socket.fd","kind":"method","sig":"pub i32 Socket.fd()","doc":"The socket's file descriptor, for options and calls through import c.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":143},{"name":"Socket.setOption","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Socket.setOption(i32 level, i32 name, i32 value)","doc":"Sets an option that is an int, as setOption does.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":146},{"name":"Socket.option","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i32 Socket.option(i32 level, i32 name)","doc":"The value of an option that is an int.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":149},{"name":"Socket.setNoDelay","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Socket.setNoDelay(bool on)","doc":"Turns TCP_NODELAY on or off.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":152},{"name":"Socket.setBuffers","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Socket.setBuffers(i64 read = 0, i64 write = 0)","doc":"The kernel's buffers for reading and writing, in bytes; 0 leaves one as\nit is. Set before listening, they also set the TCP window scale.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":158},{"name":"Socket.bind","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Socket.bind(Addr addr)","doc":"Binds the socket to addr: Addr{port: 8080} for every address, or an IP\nof this machine. On a client, the address its connection comes from.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":164},{"name":"Socket.listen","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Listener Socket.listen(i64 backlog = 1024)","doc":"Listens, with room for backlog connections that wait to be accepted.\nThe Listener owns the socket from then on.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":176},{"name":"Socket.connect","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Conn Socket.connect(Addr addr, Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Connects to addr, waiting at most timeout when it isn't 0. The Conn owns\nthe socket from then on.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":183},{"name":"Socket.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Socket.close()","doc":"Closes a socket that didn't listen or connect.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":193},{"name":"socket","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Socket socket(Family family = Family.any)","doc":"A new TCP socket of family.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":128},{"name":"adoptListener","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Listener adoptListener(i32 fd)","doc":"A listening socket made elsewhere, inherited from the program that\nstarted this one or received with readFds, as a Listener.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":231},{"name":"adoptConn","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Conn adoptConn(i32 fd)","doc":"A connected socket made elsewhere as a Conn.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":237},{"name":"helloServerName","kind":"func","sig":"pub str helloServerName(str record)","doc":"The host name in a TLS record that holds a ClientHello; \"\" when it has\nnone.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":300},{"name":"copy","kind":"func","sig":"pub !i64 copy(Conn *to, Conn *from, i64 limit = 0)","doc":"Copies what from reads to to, until from's other side ends its writes,\nor limit bytes (0 for none): the number of bytes copied. Bytes from\nalready took in go first. On Linux, without TLS on either, a stream of\nbytes goes from socket to socket in the kernel (splice). Each wait\nfor from to have data, or for to to take it, is limited by that\nconnection's timeout. It doesn't end to's writes; closeWrite does.\nreceived and sent keep counting through an error.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":370},{"name":"listenUnix","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Listener listenUnix(str path)","doc":"Listens on a Unix socket at path, a file the socket makes; it must not\nexist yet. On Linux, a path starting with @ is a name with no file.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":422},{"name":"dialUnix","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Conn dialUnix(str path, Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Connects to the Unix socket at path, waiting at most timeout when it\nisn't 0.","file":"net/socket.iris","line":433},{"name":"Addr","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Addr {\n    pub u8[16] ip\n    pub u16 port\n}","doc":"An IP address and a port. An IPv4 address is kept as an IPv6 one:\n::ffff:a.b.c.d.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":35},{"name":"Addr.isV4","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Addr.isV4()","doc":"Whether a is an IPv4 address.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":41},{"name":"Addr.ipText","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Addr.ipText()","doc":"The address's IP as text: \"192.0.2.1\" or \"2001:db8::1\".","file":"net/udp.iris","line":51},{"name":"Addr.format","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Addr.format()","doc":"\"192.0.2.1:53\", or \"[2001:db8::1]:53\" for IPv6.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":58},{"name":"Addr.equal","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Addr.equal(Addr other)","doc":"Whether the two addresses have the same IP and port.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":67},{"name":"ip","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Addr ip(str text, u16 port = 0)","doc":"The address of an IP written as text, \"192.0.2.1\" or \"2001:db8::1\",\nwith port.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":81},{"name":"parseAddr","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Addr parseAddr(str text, u16 port = 0)","doc":"An address written as text: \"192.0.2.1:53\", \"[2001:db8::1]:53\", or an\nIP without a port, which gets port.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":92},{"name":"Udp","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Udp { }","doc":"A UDP socket. Each read and write sends or gets one datagram.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":139},{"name":"Udp.setTimeout","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Udp.setTimeout(Duration d)","doc":"Each read and write after this fails with NetError.timeout when it waits\nlonger than d; 0 is the start, no limit.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":148},{"name":"Udp.sendTo","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Udp.sendTo(str data, Addr addr)","doc":"Sends data as one datagram to addr.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":192},{"name":"Udp.send","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Udp.send(str data)","doc":"Sends data as one datagram to where a dialUdp socket is connected.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":201},{"name":"Udp.readFrom","kind":"method","sig":"pub !(i64, Addr) Udp.readFrom(u8[] buf)","doc":"Waits for a datagram, puts it in buf and gives its length and who sent\nit. A datagram longer than buf is cut to fit.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":210},{"name":"Udp.read","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Udp.read(u8[] buf)","doc":"readFrom, without who sent it.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":223},{"name":"Udp.readMany","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Udp.readMany(Datagram[] msgs)","doc":"Waits for a datagram, then reads as many as are already waiting, up to\nmsgs.len, with one system call on Linux. Each one goes into the buf of\nthe next item of msgs, which gets its len and addr. Gives how many it\nread. A datagram longer than its buf is cut to fit.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":244},{"name":"Udp.sendMany","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Udp.sendMany(Datagram[] msgs)","doc":"Sends each item of msgs, the first len bytes of its buf, to its addr,\nwith one system call per 64 on Linux. On a dialUdp socket each goes to\nwhere the socket is connected and addr is not used.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":255},{"name":"Udp.localAddr","kind":"method","sig":"pub Addr Udp.localAddr()","doc":"The address the socket is bound to: its port for listenUdp(0).","file":"net/udp.iris","line":267},{"name":"Udp.fd","kind":"method","sig":"pub i32 Udp.fd()","doc":"The socket's file descriptor, for socket options and calls through\nimport c. Reading, writing or closing it directly breaks the socket.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":277},{"name":"Udp.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Udp.close()","doc":"Closes the socket.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":280},{"name":"listenUdp","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Udp listenUdp(u16 port = 0, str host = \"\")","doc":"A UDP socket on port (0 picks a free one) of host, an IP (\"\" for all\naddresses), for reading datagrams from anywhere and sending them\nanywhere. Several sockets can listen on one port, such as one per\nthread; each datagram goes to one of them.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":156},{"name":"dialUdp","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Udp dialUdp(Addr addr)","doc":"A UDP socket that sends to addr and only reads what comes from it.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":173},{"name":"Datagram","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Datagram {\n    pub u8[] buf\n    pub i64 len\n    pub Addr addr\n}","doc":"One datagram of a batch for readMany and sendMany: buf is the space\nfor it, len how many bytes of buf it uses, and addr where it came from\nor goes to.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":231},{"name":"Datagram.data","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Datagram.data()","doc":"The bytes of the datagram: buf cut to len.","file":"net/udp.iris","line":238}]},{"name":"http","title":"HTTP","group":"Network","summary":"HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2: servers, a Router, clients and WebSockets.","intro":"<h2 id=\"a-server-with-a-router\"><a href=\"#a-server-with-a-router\">A server with a Router</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>Router</code> sends each request to the handler for its method and path. In a pattern, <code>:name</code> takes one part of the path and <code>*name</code> takes the rest. The handler reads them with <code>req.param</code>. <code>r.serve(8080)</code> answers on port 8080 with every CPU core until the program is stopped.</p>\n<p>Here the server listens in a task on a port the system picks, and the same program calls it with the client:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> http, net\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">hello</span>(http.<span class=\"t\">Request</span> *req, http.<span class=\"t\">Response</span> *res) {\n    res.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;hello, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>req.<span class=\"f\">param</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">addItem</span>(http.<span class=\"t\">Request</span> *req, http.<span class=\"t\">Response</span> *res) {\n    res.status = <span class=\"n\">201</span>\n    res.<span class=\"f\">json</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{{</span><span class=\"e\">\\&quot;</span><span class=\"s\">got</span><span class=\"e\">\\&quot;</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>req.body.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"e\">}}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">serve</span>(http.<span class=\"t\">Router</span> *r, net.<span class=\"t\">Listener</span> ln) {\n    r.<span class=\"f\">serveOn</span>(ln) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;server: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.message<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    http.<span class=\"t\">Router</span> r\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> r.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    r.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/hello/:name&quot;</span>, hello)\n    r.<span class=\"f\">post</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/items&quot;</span>, addItem)\n\n    ln := <span class=\"k\">try</span> net.<span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">spawn</span> <span class=\"f\">serve</span>(&amp;r, ln)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> a = <span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;http://127.0.0.1:</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ln.port<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">/hello/ada&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> http.<span class=\"t\">Reply</span> one = <span class=\"k\">try</span> http.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(a)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>one.status<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>one.body<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> (</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>one.<span class=\"f\">header</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Content-Type&quot;</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">)&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> b = <span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;http://127.0.0.1:</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ln.port<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">/items&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> http.<span class=\"t\">Reply</span> two = <span class=\"k\">try</span> http.<span class=\"f\">post</span>(b, <span class=\"s\">&quot;text/plain&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;pear&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>two.status<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>two.body<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> c = <span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;http://127.0.0.1:</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>ln.port<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">/nope&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> http.<span class=\"t\">Reply</span> three = <span class=\"k\">try</span> http.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(c)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>three.status<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>200 hello, ada (text/plain; charset=utf-8)\n201 {&quot;got&quot;: 4}\n404</code></pre>\n<p>Each connection gets a task of its own, so a handler can wait for a database or another server while the others go on.</p>\n<ul><li>A path that no pattern matches gets 404. A path that exists only for other methods gets 405.</li><li>When several patterns match, the one with the most fixed parts wins.</li><li>A handler that returns an error is answered with 500 and the error's message.</li><li><code>res.error(404, &quot;no such user&quot;)</code> answers with a status and a message. <code>res.header</code> adds a header.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-request-holds\"><a href=\"#what-a-request-holds\">What a request holds</a></h2>\n<p><code>req.method</code>, <code>req.path</code> and <code>req.body</code> are the request's parts. <code>req.route()</code> is the path without its query. <code>req.query(&quot;page&quot;)</code> gives a decoded value from the query, and <code>req.header(&quot;Authorization&quot;)</code> a header by its name, in any case.</p>\n<p><code>req.remote</code> is the <code>net.Addr</code> the request came from. Behind a proxy it is the proxy's address, and the client's address is in a header such as <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"checks-and-logging\"><a href=\"#checks-and-logging\">Checks and logging</a></h2>\n<p><code>use</code> runs a check before the handlers, on every path or on the paths that start with a prefix. A check that answers the request itself returns <code>false</code>, and the handler isn't called. <code>after</code> runs after each request, for logging:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"t\">bool</span> <span class=\"f\">needKey</span>(http.<span class=\"t\">Request</span> *req, http.<span class=\"t\">Response</span> *res) {\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> req.<span class=\"f\">header</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;X-Key&quot;</span>) != <span class=\"s\">&quot;secret&quot;</span> {\n        res.<span class=\"f\">error</span>(<span class=\"n\">401</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;a key is needed&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">false</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">true</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">logLine</span>(http.<span class=\"t\">Request</span> *req, http.<span class=\"t\">Response</span> *res) {\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>req.method<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>req.path<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>res.status<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    http.<span class=\"t\">Router</span> r\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> r.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    r.<span class=\"f\">use</span>(needKey, <span class=\"s\">&quot;/admin&quot;</span>)\n    r.<span class=\"f\">after</span>(logLine)\n    r.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;/admin/stats&quot;</span>, stats)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> r.<span class=\"f\">serve</span>(<span class=\"n\">8080</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The Router's fields set its limits. <code>timeout</code> is for a slow client, <code>maxBody</code> and <code>maxHeader</code> limit the size of a request, and <code>gzip</code> compresses replies for clients that accept it. <code>shutdown</code> stops taking new connections and waits for the running requests.</p>\n<h2 id=\"https-and-http-2\"><a href=\"#https-and-http-2\">HTTPS and HTTP/2</a></h2>\n<p><code>serveTls</code> takes a certificate file and its key, in PEM. Clients that offer HTTP/2 get it, and the others get HTTP/1.1. The client does the same for <code>https://</code> addresses:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">try</span> r.<span class=\"f\">serveTls</span>(<span class=\"n\">8443</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;cert.pem&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;key.pem&quot;</span>)</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"a-client-that-keeps-connections\"><a href=\"#a-client-that-keeps-connections\">A client that keeps connections</a></h2>\n<p><code>http.get</code>, <code>post</code> and <code>send</code> open a connection for each request. A <code>Client</code> keeps connections open between requests, which makes many calls to one host faster. Tasks and threads can share one:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>http.<span class=\"t\">Client</span> c\n<span class=\"k\">defer</span> c.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\nc.timeout = <span class=\"n\">10s</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> id <span class=\"k\">in</span> ids {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> url = <span class=\"f\">format</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;https://api.example.com/items/</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>id<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> http.<span class=\"t\">Reply</span> r = <span class=\"k\">try</span> c.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(url)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>id<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.status<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"websockets\"><a href=\"#websockets\">WebSockets</a></h2>\n<p><code>r.websocket(path, handler)</code> answers WebSockets at a path. The handler gets the open socket and reads and sends messages until it returns. <code>http.dialWebSocket</code> opens one as a client:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">echo</span>(http.<span class=\"t\">WebSocket</span> *ws, http.<span class=\"t\">Request</span> *req) {\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> {\n        <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> http.<span class=\"t\">Message</span> m = <span class=\"k\">try</span> ws.<span class=\"f\">read</span>()\n        <span class=\"k\">try</span> ws.<span class=\"f\">send</span>(m.data)\n    }\n}</code></pre>","doc":"HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2: a server, where each connection gets a task and\nits requests are answered while the client keeps it open; a Router that\nsends each request to the handler for its method and path; a client,\nwith a Client that keeps connections open; and WebSockets. https works on\nboth sides, with TLS from OpenSSL; over TLS, HTTP/2 is used when the\nother side offers it.","decls":[{"name":"SameSite","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum SameSite { unset, lax, strict, none }","doc":"Which cross-site requests a browser sends a cookie with: unset leaves\nthe attribute out, so the browser's default (lax) applies.","file":"http/cookie.iris","line":7},{"name":"Cookie","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Cookie {\n    pub str name\n    pub str value\n    pub str path = \"/\"\n    pub str domain\n    pub Duration maxAge\n    pub bool secure\n    pub bool httpOnly = true\n    pub SameSite sameSite = .lax\n}","doc":"A cookie to set. The value is sent as it is, so it should hold no spaces,\nquotes, commas, semicolons or backslashes; url.encode or base64 makes any\nvalue safe.","file":"http/cookie.iris","line":12},{"name":"HttpError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum HttpError { badUrl, badReply, tooBig, badRequest }","doc":"What HTTP calls fail with: badUrl for an address that isn't http:// or\nhttps://, badReply for a reply that isn't HTTP, tooBig for a body over the\nlimit, badRequest for a request line that isn't HTTP/1.x.","file":"http/http.iris","line":13},{"name":"Handler","kind":"type","sig":"pub type Handler = !void(Request *, Response *)","doc":"What answers a request. It can fail: an error that leaves it is\nanswered with 500 and the error's message, as res.error does. A function\nthat returns void can be given as well.","file":"http/http.iris","line":18},{"name":"Request","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Request {\n    pub String method\n    pub String path\n    pub String body\n    pub str protocol\n    pub net.Addr remote\n    pub Duration received\n}","doc":"A request, as the handler sees it. It and its text are gone when the\nhandler returns: copy what is kept longer, as req.path.copy().","file":"http/http.iris","line":22},{"name":"Request.cookie","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Request.cookie(str name)","doc":"The value of the cookie called name that the request sent, \"\" when it\nsent none.","file":"http/cookie.iris","line":66},{"name":"Request.param","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Request.param(str name)","doc":"The value of :name or *name in the Router's pattern that matched, with\n%XX decoded, \"\" when there is none.","file":"http/http.iris","line":68},{"name":"Request.route","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Request.route()","doc":"The path without the query: /users/7 for /users/7?full=1.","file":"http/http.iris","line":78},{"name":"Request.query","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Request.query(str name)","doc":"A value from the query, with %XX and + decoded: query(\"q\") is \"tea &\ncake\" for /search?q=tea+%26+cake, and \"\" when it isn't there.","file":"http/http.iris","line":85},{"name":"Request.header","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Request.header(str name)","doc":"The value of a header, \"\" when the request has none. Names match in any case.","file":"http/http.iris","line":119},{"name":"Request.form","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Request.form(str name)","doc":"A field of a form the request sent, url-encoded or multipart, \"\" when it\nhas none. For a file field it is \"\": file gives those.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":116},{"name":"Request.file","kind":"method","sig":"pub (Part, bool) Request.file(str name)","doc":"A file the request sent in a multipart form field, and whether there is\none. Its text points into the request's body.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":152},{"name":"Response","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Response {\n    pub i32 status\n}","doc":"The answer the handler fills in. It is sent when the handler returns.","file":"http/http.iris","line":45},{"name":"Response.setCookie","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.setCookie(Cookie c)","doc":"Adds a Set-Cookie header for c to the answer.","file":"http/cookie.iris","line":27},{"name":"Response.clearCookie","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.clearCookie(str name, str path = \"/\")","doc":"Tells the browser to remove the cookie called name, set for path.","file":"http/cookie.iris","line":60},{"name":"Response.header","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.header(str name, str value)","doc":"Adds a header to the answer: res.header(\"Cache-Control\", \"no-store\").\nContent-Type replaces the type the answer has, as send does.","file":"http/http.iris","line":54},{"name":"Response.text","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.text(str body)","doc":"Answers with text.","file":"http/http.iris","line":164},{"name":"Response.html","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.html(str body)","doc":"Answers with HTML.","file":"http/http.iris","line":172},{"name":"Response.send","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.send(str body, str contentType)","doc":"Answers with body as contentType, for any type text, json and html\ndon't cover: res.send(csv, \"text/csv\").","file":"http/http.iris","line":181},{"name":"Response.error","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.error(i32 status, str message)","doc":"Answers with an error, as JSON: {\"error\": \"message\"}.","file":"http/http.iris","line":189},{"name":"Response.json","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Response.json(str body)","doc":"Answers with JSON text.","file":"http/http.iris","line":197},{"name":"Response.file","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Response.file(str file)","doc":"Answers with the file at file, its Content-Type from its extension. A\nfile that isn't there, or a folder, is answered with 404.","file":"http/mime.iris","line":59},{"name":"Response.fileFrom","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Response.fileFrom(str dir, str name)","doc":"Answers with the file called name inside dir, as file does, for a route\nlike \"/static/*path\". A name with a .. part, or an absolute one, is\nanswered with 404, so no file outside dir is sent.","file":"http/mime.iris","line":77},{"name":"serve","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void serve(u16 port, Handler handler, str host = \"\", i64 threads = 0)","doc":"Answers HTTP requests on port with handler, each connection in its own\ntask. threads event loops share the work, one per thread; 0 means one\nper CPU core. With more than one, handler runs on several threads at once.","file":"http/http.iris","line":765},{"name":"serveOn","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void serveOn(net.Listener ln, Handler handler, i64 threads = 1)","doc":"Like serve, on a listener that is already open (for port 0). Its\nthreads all accept on ln; the default is one.","file":"http/http.iris","line":771},{"name":"Router","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Router {\n    pub Duration timeout\n    pub i64 maxBody\n    pub i64 maxHeader\n    pub bool gzip\n}","doc":"Sends each request to the handler for its method and path. Patterns are\npaths with :name for one part and *name for the rest: \"/users/:id\",\n\"/files/*path\". When several match, the one with the most fixed parts\nwins. A path no pattern has gets 404; a path that is\nthere for other methods gets 405 with an Allow header.","file":"http/http.iris","line":790},{"name":"Router.shutdown","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.shutdown(Duration wait = 10s)","doc":"Stops the server: no new connections, and serve returns once the\nrequests that are being answered are done, or wait has passed.","file":"http/http.iris","line":811},{"name":"Router.use","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.use(bool(Request *, Response *) check, str prefix = \"\")","doc":"Runs check before the handler of every request whose path starts with\nprefix (\"\" for all), in the order they were added. When check gives\nfalse, the request is answered with what check put in the response, and\nno handler runs: an auth check, a rate limit.","file":"http/http.iris","line":835},{"name":"Router.after","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.after(void(Request *, Response *) done)","doc":"Runs done after every request, answered or not: an access log.","file":"http/http.iris","line":840},{"name":"Router.handle","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.handle(str pattern, Handler handler)","doc":"Adds a handler for a pattern, with or without a method in front:\n\"GET /users/:id\" or \"/health\" for every method.","file":"http/http.iris","line":862},{"name":"Router.get","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.get(str path, Handler handler)","doc":"Adds a handler for GET requests to path.","file":"http/http.iris","line":877},{"name":"Router.post","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.post(str path, Handler handler)","doc":"Adds a handler for POST requests to path.","file":"http/http.iris","line":880},{"name":"Router.put","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.put(str path, Handler handler)","doc":"Adds a handler for PUT requests to path.","file":"http/http.iris","line":883},{"name":"Router.delete","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.delete(str path, Handler handler)","doc":"Adds a handler for DELETE requests to path.","file":"http/http.iris","line":886},{"name":"Router.patch","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.patch(str path, Handler handler)","doc":"Adds a handler for PATCH requests to path.","file":"http/http.iris","line":889},{"name":"Router.websocket","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.websocket(str path, SocketHandler handler)","doc":"Answers WebSockets at path with handler, which gets the open WebSocket.\nThe connection is closed when handler returns.","file":"http/http.iris","line":893},{"name":"Router.serve","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Router.serve(u16 port, str host = \"\", i64 threads = 0)","doc":"Answers HTTP requests on port with the router, as serve does.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1045},{"name":"Router.serveOn","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Router.serveOn(net.Listener ln, i64 threads = 1)","doc":"Like serve, on a listener that is already open.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1050},{"name":"Router.serveTls","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Router.serveTls(u16 port, str certFile, str keyFile, str host = \"\", i64 threads = 0)","doc":"Answers HTTPS requests on port with the router, as serveTls does.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1055},{"name":"Router.serveTlsOn","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Router.serveTlsOn(net.Listener ln, net.TlsConfig *config, i64 threads = 1)","doc":"Answers HTTPS requests on a listener that is already open.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1063},{"name":"Router.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Router.free()","doc":"Frees the routes and hooks.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1068},{"name":"serveTls","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void serveTls(u16 port, Handler handler, str certFile, str keyFile, str host = \"\", i64 threads = 0)","doc":"Answers HTTPS requests on port with handler, as serve does, with the\ncertificate chain and key in PEM files.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1030},{"name":"serveTlsOn","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void serveTlsOn(net.Listener ln, Handler handler, net.TlsConfig *config, i64 threads = 1)","doc":"Answers HTTPS requests on a listener that is already open.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1039},{"name":"Reply","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Reply {\n    pub i32 status\n    pub String body\n    pub str protocol\n}","doc":"The answer to a request the client made. free gives it back.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1086},{"name":"Reply.header","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Reply.header(str name)","doc":"The value of a header of the reply, \"\" when it has none.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1118},{"name":"Reply.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Reply.free()","doc":"Frees the body and the headers.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1128},{"name":"get","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Reply get(str url)","doc":"Asks for url with GET.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1141},{"name":"post","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Reply post(str url, str contentType, str body)","doc":"Sends body to url with POST.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1144},{"name":"send","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Reply send(str method, str url, str body = \"\", str contentType = \"\")","doc":"Sends a request and reads the reply, on a connection of its own, waiting\nat most 30 seconds for each step. https:// addresses check the server's\ncertificate against the ones the system trusts. A Client keeps\nconnections open between requests.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1213},{"name":"Client","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Client {\n    pub Duration timeout\n    pub i64 maxIdle\n    pub bool noHttp2\n    pub bool priorKnowledge\n    pub str ca\n}","doc":"A client that keeps connections to each host open between requests, and\ncan be shared by tasks and threads.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1225},{"name":"Client.send","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Reply Client.send(str method, str url, str body = \"\", str contentType = \"\")","doc":"Sends a request on a connection the client keeps, or a new one.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1315},{"name":"Client.get","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Reply Client.get(str url)","doc":"Sends a GET request to url.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1386},{"name":"Client.post","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Reply Client.post(str url, str contentType, str body)","doc":"Sends body to url with POST, as contentType.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1389},{"name":"Client.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Client.close()","doc":"Closes the connections the client keeps.","file":"http/http.iris","line":1392},{"name":"mimeType","kind":"func","sig":"pub str mimeType(str name)","doc":"The MIME type for a file's name, by its extension in any case:\nmimeType(\"app.js\") is \"text/javascript; charset=utf-8\". An extension\nit doesn't know gives \"application/octet-stream\".","file":"http/mime.iris","line":8},{"name":"Part","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Part {\n    pub str name\n    pub str filename\n    pub str contentType\n    pub str data\n}","doc":"One part of a multipart/form-data body. The text points into the body.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":7},{"name":"parseMultipart","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[Part] parseMultipart(str body, str contentType)","doc":"The parts of a multipart/form-data body, contentType being the request's\nContent-Type with its boundary. They point into body.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":57},{"name":"Multipart","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Multipart {\n    pub String body\n}","doc":"A multipart/form-data body being written, for a client to send: add the\nfields and files, finish, then send body with contentType().","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":171},{"name":"Multipart.field","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Multipart.field(str name, str value)","doc":"Adds a plain field.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":193},{"name":"Multipart.file","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Multipart.file(str name, str filename, str contentType, str data)","doc":"Adds a file: its field's name, its file name, its type and its bytes.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":200},{"name":"Multipart.finish","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Multipart.finish()","doc":"Ends the body.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":207},{"name":"Multipart.contentType","kind":"method","sig":"pub String Multipart.contentType()","doc":"The Content-Type to send the body with.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":212},{"name":"Multipart.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Multipart.free()","doc":"Frees the body.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":215},{"name":"multipart","kind":"func","sig":"pub Multipart multipart()","doc":"A new multipart body with a random boundary.","file":"http/multipart.iris","line":177},{"name":"Message","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Message {\n    pub bool binary\n    pub String data\n}","doc":"One message: text, or bytes when binary is true. free gives it back.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":7},{"name":"Message.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Message.free()","doc":"Frees the message's data.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":13},{"name":"WebSocket","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct WebSocket { }","doc":"An open WebSocket. Pings are answered on their own while reading.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":16},{"name":"WebSocket.send","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void WebSocket.send(str text)","doc":"Sends a text message.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":73},{"name":"WebSocket.sendBinary","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void WebSocket.sendBinary(str data)","doc":"Sends a message of bytes.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":76},{"name":"WebSocket.read","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Message WebSocket.read()","doc":"The next message. NetError.closed when the other side has closed.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":79},{"name":"WebSocket.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void WebSocket.close()","doc":"Closes the WebSocket: the other side is told, and the connection goes.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":143},{"name":"dialWebSocket","kind":"func","sig":"pub !WebSocket dialWebSocket(str url)","doc":"Opens a WebSocket to a ws:// or wss:// address.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":154},{"name":"SocketHandler","kind":"type","sig":"pub type SocketHandler = !void(WebSocket *, Request *)","doc":"What answers a WebSocket of a Router.","file":"http/websocket.iris","line":204}]},{"name":"template","title":"HTML templates","group":"Network","summary":"Templates with values, if, for and include, escaped for HTML, filled from a struct or a Map.","intro":"<h2 id=\"filling-in-a-template\"><a href=\"#filling-in-a-template\">Filling in a template</a></h2>\n<p><code>template.parse(text)</code> reads a template once, and <code>render(data)</code> fills it in from a <code>@json</code> struct as often as needed.</p>\n<ul><li><code>{{ name }}</code> writes a value, escaped for HTML.</li><li><code>{{ user.name }}</code> writes a field of a struct inside the data.</li><li><code>{{ raw name }}</code> writes a value as it is, for HTML the program made itself.</li></ul>\n<p>Write templates in raw strings, <code>r&quot;...&quot;</code> or <code>r&quot;&quot;&quot;...&quot;&quot;&quot;</code>, where braces are plain characters:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> template\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Item</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> name\n    <span class=\"t\">f64</span> price\n}\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">Page</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> shop\n    <span class=\"t\">bool</span> open\n    <span class=\"t\">List</span>[<span class=\"t\">Item</span>] items\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> template.<span class=\"t\">Template</span> t = <span class=\"k\">try</span> template.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;\n        &lt;h1&gt;{{ shop }}&lt;/h1&gt;\n        {{ if open }}\n        &lt;ul&gt;\n          {{ for i, item in items }}\n          &lt;li&gt;{{ i }}. {{ item.name }}: {{ item.price }}&lt;/li&gt;\n          {{ else }}\n          &lt;li&gt;nothing yet&lt;/li&gt;\n          {{ end }}\n        &lt;/ul&gt;\n        {{ else }}\n        &lt;p&gt;closed&lt;/p&gt;\n        {{ end }}\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"t\">Page</span> p = <span class=\"t\">Page</span>{shop: <span class=\"s\">&quot;Tea &amp; &lt;Cake&gt;&quot;</span>.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(), open: <span class=\"k\">true</span>}\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> p.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    p.items.<span class=\"f\">push</span>(<span class=\"t\">Item</span>{name: <span class=\"s\">&quot;green tea&quot;</span>.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(), price: <span class=\"n\">3.5</span>})\n    p.items.<span class=\"f\">push</span>(<span class=\"t\">Item</span>{name: <span class=\"s\">&quot;lemon cake&quot;</span>.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(), price: <span class=\"n\">4.25</span>})\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> html = <span class=\"k\">try</span> t.<span class=\"f\">render</span>(p)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(html)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>&lt;h1&gt;Tea &amp;amp; &amp;lt;Cake&amp;gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;0. green tea: 3.5&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;1. lemon cake: 4.25&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>A block tag (<code>{{ if }}</code>, <code>{{ else }}</code>, <code>{{ end }}</code>, <code>{{ for }}</code> or <code>{{# comment }}</code>) alone on its line removes the whole line, so the output has no blank lines where the tags were.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-template-can-say\"><a href=\"#what-a-template-can-say\">What a template can say</a></h2>\n<div class=\"table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tag</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>{{ name }}</code>, <code>{{ a.b.c }}</code></td><td>The value, escaped for HTML.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ raw name }}</code></td><td>The value as it is.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ list.len }}</code></td><td>The number of values in a list, or of bytes in a text.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ if name }}</code> \u2026 <code>{{ else if other }}</code> \u2026 <code>{{ else }}</code> \u2026 <code>{{ end }}</code></td><td>A part kept when the value is true, not zero and not empty.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ if not name }}</code></td><td>The opposite test.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ if state == &quot;open&quot; }}</code>, <code>{{ if n != 0 }}</code></td><td>Compares with text, a number, <code>true</code>, <code>false</code> or another value.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ for x in list }}</code> \u2026 <code>{{ else }}</code> \u2026 <code>{{ end }}</code></td><td>The part once for each value, or the else part when there are none.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ for i, x in list }}</code></td><td>Also gives the place of each value, from 0. Over a struct, each field's name and value.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{ include &quot;name&quot; }}</code></td><td>Another template, filled from the same data.</td></tr><tr><td><code>{{# a comment }}</code></td><td>Nothing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A value missing from the data fails with <code>TemplateError.missing</code> and the line, so a misspelled name shows up. Inside <code>{{ if }}</code> a missing value counts as false. A list or a struct written as text fails with <code>TemplateError.wrongType</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-map-and-other-templates\"><a href=\"#a-map-and-other-templates\">A Map and other templates</a></h2>\n<p><code>renderMap(map)</code> fills a template from a <code>Map</code> with <code>str</code> keys. Its values can be text, numbers, lists or <code>@json</code> structs. <code>add(name, text)</code> adds a template for <code>{{ include &quot;name&quot; }}</code>. <code>template.load(dir, main)</code> reads every file in a folder as a template named by its path inside the folder, and <code>render</code> fills the one named <code>main</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> template\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> template.<span class=\"t\">Template</span> t = <span class=\"k\">try</span> template.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(<span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;{{ include &quot;header&quot; }}&lt;p&gt;{{ count }} new messages&lt;/p&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> t.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;header&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;&lt;header&gt;Hello, {{ user }}&lt;/header&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"t\">Map</span>[<span class=\"t\">str</span>, <span class=\"t\">str</span>] data\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> data.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    data.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;user&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</span>)\n    data.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;count&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;3&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> html = <span class=\"k\">try</span> t.<span class=\"f\">renderMap</span>(data)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(html)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> header = <span class=\"k\">try</span> t.<span class=\"f\">renderMap</span>(data, <span class=\"s\">&quot;header&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(header)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>&lt;header&gt;Hello, Ada&lt;/header&gt;&lt;p&gt;3 new messages&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;header&gt;Hello, Ada&lt;/header&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><code>template.escape(text)</code> escapes text for HTML the same way: <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&quot;</code> and <code>&#x27;</code>.</p>","doc":"HTML templates: text with {{ name }} for values, {{ if }}, {{ for }} and\n{{ include }}, filled from a @json struct or a Map. Values are escaped for\nHTML unless written {{ raw name }}.\n\n    scoped template.Template t = try template.parse(text)\n    scoped String html = try t.render(page)","decls":[{"name":"TemplateError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum TemplateError { syntax, missing, wrongType }","doc":"What templates fail with: syntax for a template that can't be read,\nmissing for a value or a template that isn't there when filling it in,\nwrongType for a value that can't go where it is used.","file":"template/template.iris","line":13},{"name":"Template","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Template { }","doc":"A template, with the others it can include by name.","file":"template/template.iris","line":78},{"name":"Template.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Template.free()","doc":"Frees the template and the ones added to it.","file":"template/template.iris","line":84},{"name":"Template.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Template.add(str name, str text)","doc":"Adds a template that {{ include \"name\" }} puts in, from text.","file":"template/template.iris","line":367},{"name":"Template.render","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String Template.render[T](T data, str name = \"\")","doc":"Fills in the template with data, a @json struct; name picks one added\nwith add, \"\" the main one.","file":"template/template.iris","line":856},{"name":"Template.renderMap","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String Template.renderMap[V](Map[str, V] data, str name = \"\")","doc":"Fills in the template with the keys and values of a Map. The values can\nbe text, numbers, lists or @json structs.","file":"template/template.iris","line":869},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Template parse(str text)","doc":"Reads a template. Blocks ({{ if }}, {{ else }}, {{ end }}, {{ for }} and\n{{# comments }}) that are alone on a line take that line out with them.","file":"template/template.iris","line":358},{"name":"load","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Template load(str dir, str main = \"\")","doc":"Reads every file in dir, and the folders in it, as templates named by\ntheir path inside dir (\"layout.html\", \"parts/nav.html\"); the main one is\nthe file called main, which is \"\" when there is none.","file":"template/template.iris","line":383},{"name":"escape","kind":"func","sig":"pub String escape(str text)","doc":"text with &, <, >, \" and ' written as entities, for HTML.","file":"template/template.iris","line":698}]},{"name":"dns","title":"DNS messages","group":"Network","summary":"Reading DNS queries and replies from bytes, and writing them.","intro":"<p><code>dns.parse</code> reads the bytes of a DNS message into a <code>Message</code>: its header, its questions, and its answer, authority and additional records. <code>encode</code> writes a <code>Message</code> as bytes. A name that appears more than once is written once and pointed at after that.</p>\n<p>A record keeps its data in the fields for its kind:</p>\n<ul><li><code>ip</code> for <code>a</code> and <code>aaaa</code>.</li><li><code>target</code> for a host name.</li><li><code>text</code> for <code>txt</code>.</li><li>The numbers of <code>mx</code>, <code>srv</code> and <code>soa</code> in fields of their own.</li><li>A kind without a name here is <code>other</code>, with its number in <code>code</code> and its bytes in <code>data</code>.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> dns\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> q := dns.<span class=\"f\">query</span>(<span class=\"n\">7</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;example.com&quot;</span>, .mx)\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> bytes = <span class=\"k\">try</span> q.<span class=\"f\">encode</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> m := <span class=\"k\">try</span> dns.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(bytes)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>bytes.len<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> bytes, id </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.header.id<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">, </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.questions.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>).name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.questions.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>).kind<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> reply := m.<span class=\"f\">reply</span>()\n    reply.answers.<span class=\"f\">push</span>(.{name: <span class=\"s\">&quot;example.com&quot;</span>.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>(), kind: .mx, ttl: <span class=\"n\">1h</span>, priority: <span class=\"n\">10</span>, target: <span class=\"s\">&quot;mail.example.com&quot;</span>.<span class=\"f\">copy</span>()})\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> out = <span class=\"k\">try</span> reply.<span class=\"f\">encode</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> back := <span class=\"k\">try</span> dns.<span class=\"f\">parse</span>(out)\n    r := back.answers.<span class=\"f\">at</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.kind<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.priority<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.target<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.ttl<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>29 bytes, id 7, example.com mx\nexample.com mx 10 mail.example.com 1h0m0s</code></pre>\n<p>A server answers a query with <code>reply</code>, which keeps the query's id and questions. To look names up, use <code>net.lookup</code> and <code>net.query</code>, which ask DNS servers through this module.</p>","doc":"DNS messages: reading the bytes of a query or a reply into a Message,\nand writing a Message as bytes, with its names compressed. A resolver\nthat asks servers is net.lookup and net.query.","decls":[{"name":"DnsError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum DnsError { short, broken, badName }","doc":"What parse and encode fail with: short for a message that ends too soon,\nbroken for one whose parts don't fit together, and badName for a name\nthat can't be written (an empty label, or one longer than 63 bytes).","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":10},{"name":"Type","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Type { a = 1, ns = 2, cname = 5, soa = 6, ptr = 12, mx = 15, txt = 16, aaaa = 28, srv = 33, opt = 41, any = 255, other = 0 }","doc":"The kinds of record, with their numbers. other is any kind without a\nname here; its number is in the record's code.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":14},{"name":"typeOf","kind":"func","sig":"pub Type typeOf(i64 code)","doc":"The kind with this number: other when it has no name here.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":17},{"name":"Header","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Header {\n    pub u16 id\n    pub bool response\n    pub i64 opcode\n    pub bool authoritative\n    pub bool truncated\n    pub bool recursionWanted\n    pub bool recursionAvailable\n    pub bool authenticData\n    pub bool checkingDisabled\n    pub i64 rcode\n}","doc":"The flags and counts at the start of a message. rcode is 0 for no error,\n1 for a malformed query, 2 for a server failure, 3 for a name that isn't\nthere, 4 for a kind of query the server doesn't do and 5 for refused.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":35},{"name":"Question","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Question {\n    pub String name\n    pub Type kind\n    pub u16 code\n    pub u16 class = 1\n}","doc":"A question: the name asked about and the kind of record wanted.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":49},{"name":"Question.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Question.free()","doc":"Frees the question's name.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":59},{"name":"Record","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Record {\n    pub String name\n    pub Type kind\n    pub u16 code\n    pub u16 class = 1\n    pub Duration ttl\n    pub u8[16] ip\n    pub String target\n    pub String text\n    pub i64 priority\n    pub i64 weight\n    pub u16 port\n    pub String mailbox\n    pub i64 serial\n    pub Duration refresh\n    pub Duration retry\n    pub Duration expire\n    pub Duration minimum\n    pub String data\n}","doc":"One record. Which fields hold its data depends on its kind.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":62},{"name":"Record.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Record.free()","doc":"Frees the record's name and data.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":96},{"name":"Message","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Message {\n    pub Header header\n    pub List[Question] questions\n    pub List[Record] answers\n    pub List[Record] authorities\n    pub List[Record] additionals\n}","doc":"A whole message: a header and four lists of what follows it.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":105},{"name":"Message.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Message.free()","doc":"Frees the message's questions and records.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":114},{"name":"Message.reply","kind":"method","sig":"pub Message Message.reply()","doc":"The start of a reply to the query m: its id, opcode and questions, with\nresponse set. The caller adds the records and sets rcode.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":142},{"name":"Message.encode","kind":"method","sig":"pub !String Message.encode()","doc":"The message as bytes to send.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":470},{"name":"query","kind":"func","sig":"pub Message query(u16 id, str name, Type kind)","doc":"A query for the records of a kind for name, with recursion wanted.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":132},{"name":"parse","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Message parse(str bytes)","doc":"The message in bytes, as it comes from the network.","file":"dns/dns.iris","line":299}]},{"name":"smtp","title":"Mail","group":"Network","summary":"Sending mail over SMTP, with STARTTLS or TLS and a login.","intro":"<h2 id=\"sending-mail\"><a href=\"#sending-mail\">Sending mail</a></h2>\n<p><code>smtp.send</code> connects to the server, sends one mail and closes the connection. To send several mails, <code>connect</code> gives a <code>Client</code> that sends each one on the same connection:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> smtp\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    mail := smtp.<span class=\"t\">Mail</span>{\n        from: <span class=\"s\">&quot;Alerts &lt;alerts@example.com&gt;&quot;</span>,\n        to: [<span class=\"s\">&quot;ops@example.com&quot;</span>],\n        subject: <span class=\"s\">&quot;disk 91% full on db1&quot;</span>,\n        text: <span class=\"s\">&quot;/var on db1 is at 91%.&quot;</span>,\n        html: <span class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;p&gt;/var on &lt;b&gt;db1&lt;/b&gt; is at 91%.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;</span>,\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> smtp.<span class=\"f\">send</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;smtps://alerts:secret@mail.example.com&quot;</span>, mail)\n}</code></pre>\n<p>A mail with both <code>text</code> and <code>html</code> carries both, and the mail program shows the one it can. A subject with characters beyond ASCII is encoded as mail headers need. <code>bcc</code> recipients get the mail without being named in it.</p>\n<p>When the server refuses a recipient, the send fails with <code>SmtpError.rejected</code> and the server's reply, and the <code>Client</code> can still send the next mail. A line break in <code>from</code>, <code>subject</code> or an address is an error, so text from users can't add headers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"attachments\"><a href=\"#attachments\">Attachments</a></h2>\n<p><code>attachments</code> sends files with the mail. Each <code>Attachment</code> has a <code>name</code>, its bytes in <code>data</code> and a <code>contentType</code>, which is <code>application/octet-stream</code> when empty. An attachment with an <code>id</code> is shown inside the HTML as <code>&lt;img src=&quot;cid:id&quot;&gt;</code> instead of as a file to save:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">String</span> pdf = <span class=\"k\">try</span> fs.<span class=\"f\">readAll</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;invoice.pdf&quot;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"k\">try</span> smtp.<span class=\"f\">send</span>(server, smtp.<span class=\"t\">Mail</span>{\n    from: <span class=\"s\">&quot;billing@example.com&quot;</span>,\n    to: [<span class=\"s\">&quot;ada@example.com&quot;</span>],\n    subject: <span class=\"s\">&quot;Invoice 1041&quot;</span>,\n    html: <span class=\"s\">r&quot;&quot;&quot;&lt;img src=&quot;cid:logo&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Your invoice is attached.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>,\n    attachments: [\n        smtp.<span class=\"t\">Attachment</span>{name: <span class=\"s\">&quot;logo.png&quot;</span>, data: logo, contentType: <span class=\"s\">&quot;image/png&quot;</span>, id: <span class=\"s\">&quot;logo&quot;</span>},\n        smtp.<span class=\"t\">Attachment</span>{name: <span class=\"s\">&quot;invoice.pdf&quot;</span>, data: pdf, contentType: <span class=\"s\">&quot;application/pdf&quot;</span>},\n    ],\n})</code></pre>","doc":"Sending mail over SMTP. The server is given as an address:\n\n    smtp://user:password@mail.example.com:587    STARTTLS, then the login\n    smtps://user:password@mail.example.com       TLS from the start, port 465\n\nwith ?ca=file to trust other certificates than the system's, and\n?helo=name for the name the client gives itself. On smtp:// the\nconnection switches to TLS whenever the server offers it, and a\npassword is never sent without TLS.\n\n    try smtp.send(\"smtps://alerts:secret@mail.example.com\", smtp.Mail{\n        from: \"alerts@example.com\",\n        to: [\"ops@example.com\"],\n        subject: \"disk 91% full on db1\",\n        text: \"/var is at 91%.\",\n    })\n\nFiles go along as attachments, read with fs.readAll:\n\n    scoped String pdf = try fs.readAll(\"report.pdf\")\n    mail.attachments = [smtp.Attachment{name: \"report.pdf\", data: pdf, contentType: \"application/pdf\"}]","decls":[{"name":"SmtpError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum SmtpError { connect, auth, rejected, protocol }","doc":"What sending fails with: connect (the server can't be reached, or TLS\nfails), auth (the login is refused, or would have to go out without\nTLS), rejected (the server refused a sender, a recipient or the mail,\nwith its reply in the message), protocol (the server said something\nthat isn't SMTP).","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":30},{"name":"Mail","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Mail {\n    pub str from\n    pub str[] to\n    pub str[] cc\n    pub str[] bcc\n    pub str replyTo\n    pub str subject\n    pub str text\n    pub str html\n    pub Attachment[] attachments\n}","doc":"A mail. from, to and subject are needed, and text, html or both: with\nboth, the mail program shows the one it can. cc goes in the header for\neveryone to see; bcc gets the mail without being shown. attachments are\nfiles sent along with it.","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":36},{"name":"Attachment","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Attachment {\n    pub str name\n    pub str data\n    pub str contentType\n    pub str id\n}","doc":"A file sent with a mail: its name as the reader sees it, its bytes, and\nits type (\"\" is application/octet-stream; http.mimeType gives one from\nthe name). With an id, the file is part of the HTML instead of a file\nto save: <img src=\"cid:logo\"> shows the attachment whose id is \"logo\".","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":52},{"name":"Client","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Client { }","doc":"A connection to a mail server, for sending several mails. One task\nuses it at a time.","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":61},{"name":"Client.send","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Client.send(Mail m)","doc":"Sends a mail.","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":221},{"name":"Client.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Client.close()","doc":"Says goodbye to the server and closes the connection.","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":454},{"name":"connect","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Client connect(str address)","doc":"Connects to the server at address, starts TLS and logs in.","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":171},{"name":"send","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void send(str address, Mail m)","doc":"Sends a mail through the server at address: connect, send and close.","file":"smtp/smtp.iris","line":447}]},{"name":"sqlite","title":"SQLite","group":"Databases","summary":"SQLite databases, with rows read into @json structs.","intro":"<h2 id=\"opening-and-changing\"><a href=\"#opening-and-changing\">Opening and changing</a></h2>\n<p><code>sqlite.open</code> opens a database file and makes it when missing. <code>&quot;:memory:&quot;</code> is a database that lives only while the program runs. <code>exec</code> runs SQL that gives no rows. Each <code>?</code> takes the next value from the list after the SQL, so values never become part of the SQL text:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> sqlite\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    db := <span class=\"k\">try</span> sqlite.<span class=\"f\">open</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;:memory:&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> db.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL, age INTEGER)&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES (?, ?)&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</span>), .<span class=\"f\">int</span>(<span class=\"n\">36</span>)])\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;id </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>db.<span class=\"f\">lastId</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES (?, ?)&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Zoe&quot;</span>), .none])\n\n    db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO users (name) VALUES (?)&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</span>)]) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;UPDATE users SET age = age + 1 WHERE age IS NOT NULL&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>db.<span class=\"f\">changes</span>()<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> changed&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>id 1\nSqliteError.constraint\n1 changed</code></pre>\n<p>The values are <code>.int</code>, <code>.float</code>, <code>.text</code>, and <code>.none</code> for NULL. Without values, the SQL given to <code>exec</code> can hold several statements, which suits making the tables.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rows-into-structs\"><a href=\"#rows-into-structs\">Rows into structs</a></h2>\n<p><code>sqlite.all</code> runs a query and gives a <code>List</code> of <code>@json</code> structs, each field filled from the column of the same name. <code>sqlite.one</code> gives the first row, or fails with <code>SqliteError.noRows</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> sqlite\n\n<span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">User</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> id\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> name\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> age\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    db := <span class=\"k\">try</span> sqlite.<span class=\"f\">open</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;:memory:&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> db.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, age INTEGER)&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES (&#x27;Ada&#x27;, 36), (&#x27;Zoe&#x27;, 41), (&#x27;Bo&#x27;, 7)&quot;</span>)\n\n    users := <span class=\"k\">try</span> sqlite.all[<span class=\"t\">User</span>](&amp;db, <span class=\"s\">&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE age &gt; ? ORDER BY name&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">int</span>(<span class=\"n\">18</span>)])\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> u <span class=\"k\">in</span> users {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.id<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.age<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        u.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n    users.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> <span class=\"t\">User</span> bo = <span class=\"k\">try</span> sqlite.one[<span class=\"t\">User</span>](&amp;db, <span class=\"s\">&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Bo&quot;</span>)])\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;one: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>bo.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>1 Ada 36\n2 Zoe 41\none: Bo</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"row-by-row\"><a href=\"#row-by-row\">Row by row</a></h2>\n<p><code>query</code> gives <code>Rows</code>, read one row at a time with <code>next</code>. <code>int</code>, <code>float</code> and <code>text</code> read a column by its number, from 0, and <code>isNull</code> says whether it is NULL. A column's text stays valid until the next call to <code>next</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code run\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> sqlite\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    db := <span class=\"k\">try</span> sqlite.<span class=\"f\">open</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;:memory:&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> db.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;CREATE TABLE t (name TEXT, score REAL); INSERT INTO t VALUES (&#x27;a&#x27;, 1.5), (&#x27;b&#x27;, NULL)&quot;</span>)\n\n    rows := <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">query</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;SELECT name, score FROM t ORDER BY name&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> rows.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"k\">try</span> rows.<span class=\"f\">next</span>() {\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> rows.<span class=\"f\">isNull</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>) {\n            <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>rows.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: no score&quot;</span>)\n        } <span class=\"k\">else</span> {\n            <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>rows.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"n\">0</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>rows.<span class=\"f\">float</span>(<span class=\"n\">1</span>)<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"out\"><code>a: 1.5\nb: no score</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"transactions\"><a href=\"#transactions\">Transactions</a></h2>\n<p><code>begin</code>, <code>commit</code> and <code>rollback</code> group several changes, so either all of them happen or none does:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">begin</span>()\n\ndb.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE id = ?&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">int</span>(from)]) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">rollback</span>()\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> e\n}\n<span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10 WHERE id = ?&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">int</span>(to)])\n\n<span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">commit</span>()</code></pre>\n<p>SQLite is loaded the first time a database is opened. <code>IRIS_SQLITE</code> names another copy of it.</p>","doc":"SQLite databases. The system's SQLite is loaded when a program first\nopens one. Calls wait for the database on the calling thread; a Db can be\nshared by threads.","decls":[{"name":"SqliteError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum SqliteError { failed, busy, constraint, noRows }","doc":"What database calls fail with: failed, busy (the database is locked),\nconstraint (a UNIQUE, NOT NULL or other constraint), and noRows (one found\nnothing).","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":8},{"name":"Value","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Value { int(i64 v), float(f64 v), text(str v), blob(u8[] v), none }","doc":"A value for a ? in SQL: .int(36), .float(1.5), .text(\"ada\"), .blob(bytes)\nfor bytes kept as they are, or .none for NULL.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":12},{"name":"Db","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Db { }","doc":"An open database.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":83},{"name":"Db.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Db.close()","doc":"Closes the database.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":99},{"name":"Db.exec","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Db.exec(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs SQL that gives no rows: CREATE, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE. Each ? takes\nthe next of args. Without args, sql can hold several statements; with\nargs, only one, and more after it is an error.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":137},{"name":"Db.query","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Rows Db.query(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs a query; its rows come one at a time from Rows.next.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":175},{"name":"Db.lastId","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Db.lastId()","doc":"The rowid of the last row INSERT added.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":185},{"name":"Db.changes","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Db.changes()","doc":"How many rows the last INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE changed.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":188},{"name":"Db.begin","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Db.begin()","doc":"Starts a transaction.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":191},{"name":"Db.commit","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Db.commit()","doc":"Makes the transaction's changes last.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":194},{"name":"Db.rollback","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Db.rollback()","doc":"Drops the transaction's changes.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":197},{"name":"open","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Db open(str path)","doc":"Opens the database in the file at path, making it if needed.\n\":memory:\" is a database that lives in memory until it is closed.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":89},{"name":"Rows","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Rows { }","doc":"The rows of a query. close gives it back.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":200},{"name":"Rows.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub !bool Rows.next()","doc":"Moves to the next row: false when there are no more.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":206},{"name":"Rows.columns","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Rows.columns()","doc":"The number of columns.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":222},{"name":"Rows.name","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Rows.name(i64 col)","doc":"A column's name.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":225},{"name":"Rows.int","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Rows.int(i64 col)","doc":"A column as an integer.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":232},{"name":"Rows.float","kind":"method","sig":"pub f64 Rows.float(i64 col)","doc":"A column as a float.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":235},{"name":"Rows.text","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Rows.text(i64 col)","doc":"A column as text, valid until the next call to next.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":238},{"name":"Rows.bytes","kind":"method","sig":"pub u8[] Rows.bytes(i64 col)","doc":"A column's bytes as they are, for a BLOB; valid until the next call to\nnext. A String field of a @json struct gets them too.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":246},{"name":"Rows.isNull","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Rows.isNull(i64 col)","doc":"Whether a column is NULL.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":253},{"name":"Rows.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Rows.close()","doc":"Ends the query; the row's text is gone after it.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":256},{"name":"Rows.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Rows.free()","doc":"close, for scoped.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":262},{"name":"scan","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void scan[T](Rows *rows, T *out)","doc":"Fills a @json struct from the row: each field from the column of the\nsame name (or its @json name). A NULL column leaves the field as it is.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":266},{"name":"all","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[T] all[T](Db *db, str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Every row of a query, as @json structs: sqlite.all[User](&db, \"SELECT ...\").\nThe values' Strings are the caller's to free.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":274},{"name":"one","kind":"func","sig":"pub !T one[T](Db *db, str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"The first row of a query, as a @json struct. A query with no rows fails\nwith SqliteError.noRows.","file":"sqlite/sqlite.iris","line":298}]},{"name":"pg","title":"PostgreSQL","group":"Databases","summary":"A PostgreSQL driver in Iris, with a pool and transactions.","intro":"<h2 id=\"connecting\"><a href=\"#connecting\">Connecting</a></h2>\n<p><code>pg.pool</code> gives a <code>Pool</code> for a server's address, with at most a given number of connections open (10 unless given). It connects once to check the address and the login, then opens connections as tasks need them. Every task and thread of a program can share one pool:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> pg\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    db := <span class=\"k\">try</span> pg.<span class=\"f\">pool</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;postgres://ada:secret@localhost:5432/shop&quot;</span>, <span class=\"n\">20</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> db.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The address is <code>postgres://user:password@host:port/database</code>. <code>?sslmode=require</code> makes the connection use TLS, and <code>?sslmode=verify-full&amp;ca=root.pem</code> also checks the server's certificate. <code>pg.connect</code> gives a single connection, for a program that does one thing at a time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"changing-and-querying\"><a href=\"#changing-and-querying\">Changing and querying</a></h2>\n<p><code>exec</code> runs a statement and gives the number of rows it changed. Values are sent apart from the SQL, as <code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code> and so on, so they can't change what the SQL means. <code>execScript</code> runs SQL without values, which may hold several statements, as a schema does:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">execScript</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (\n        id serial PRIMARY KEY,\n        name text UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n        age int\n    );\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>)\n\nn := <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES ($1, $2)&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</span>), .<span class=\"f\">int</span>(<span class=\"n\">36</span>)])\n\n_ = db.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO users (name) VALUES ($1)&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">text</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</span>)]) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.code<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>) <span class=\"c\">// PgError.constraint: the name is taken</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">0</span>\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The values are <code>.int</code>, <code>.float</code>, <code>.text</code>, <code>.bool</code>, and <code>.none</code> for NULL.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rows-into-structs\"><a href=\"#rows-into-structs\">Rows into structs</a></h2>\n<p><code>pg.all</code> runs a query and reads every row into a <code>@json</code> struct, each field from the column of the same name. <code>pg.one</code> reads the first row, or fails with <code>PgError.noRows</code>. Both take a <code>Pool</code>, a <code>Conn</code> or a <code>Tx</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"a\">@json</span>\n<span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"t\">User</span> {\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> id\n    <span class=\"t\">String</span> name\n    <span class=\"t\">i64</span> age\n}\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">printAdults</span>(pg.<span class=\"t\">Pool</span> *db) {\n    users := <span class=\"k\">try</span> pg.all[<span class=\"t\">User</span>](&amp;db, <span class=\"s\">&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE age &gt;= $1 ORDER BY name&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">int</span>(<span class=\"n\">18</span>)])\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> {\n        <span class=\"k\">for</span> u <span class=\"k\">in</span> users {\n            u.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n        }\n        users.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> u <span class=\"k\">in</span> users {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>u.age<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>query</code> gives <code>Rows</code>, which can also be read one row at a time. <code>next</code> moves to the next row, and <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>text</code> and <code>isNull</code> read a column by its number, from 0. <code>close</code> ends them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transactions\"><a href=\"#transactions\">Transactions</a></h2>\n<p><code>begin</code> takes a connection from the pool for a transaction. Its <code>query</code> and <code>exec</code> run in the transaction. <code>commit</code> or <code>rollback</code> ends it and gives the connection back. With <code>scoped</code>, a transaction left without <code>commit</code>, by an error or a <code>return</code>, rolls back:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">transfer</span>(pg.<span class=\"t\">Pool</span> *db, <span class=\"t\">i64</span> from, <span class=\"t\">i64</span> to, <span class=\"t\">i64</span> amount) {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> tx := <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">begin</span>()\n\n    _ = <span class=\"k\">try</span> tx.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - $1 WHERE id = $2&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">int</span>(amount), .<span class=\"f\">int</span>(from)])\n    _ = <span class=\"k\">try</span> tx.<span class=\"f\">exec</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + $1 WHERE id = $2&quot;</span>, [.<span class=\"f\">int</span>(amount), .<span class=\"f\">int</span>(to)])\n\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span> tx.<span class=\"f\">commit</span>()\n}</code></pre>\n<p>A transaction fails with <code>PgError.retry</code> when it clashes with another one, through a serialization failure or a deadlock. Run it again in that case.</p>\n<h2 id=\"errors\"><a href=\"#errors\">Errors</a></h2>\n<p>A failed statement gives <code>PgError.failed</code>, or <code>PgError.constraint</code> for a broken UNIQUE, NOT NULL, foreign key or CHECK constraint. The message holds the server's text and its SQLSTATE code in brackets, such as <code>(23505)</code>.</p>","doc":"PostgreSQL, spoken over the network by Iris itself: a connection waits\nlike any other network call, so the program's other tasks keep running.\n\n    db := try pg.pool(\"postgres://ada:secret@localhost/shop\")\n    defer db.close()\n    try db.exec(\"INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES ($1, $2)\", [.text(\"Ada\"), .int(36)])\n    users := try pg.all[User](&db, \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE age > $1\", [.int(30)])\n\nValues go apart from the SQL ($1, $2, ...), so they can't change what the\nSQL means. Results come back as text and are read with Rows.int, text\nand so on, or into @json structs by column name with all and one.","decls":[{"name":"PgError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum PgError { connect, auth, failed, constraint, retry, noRows, closed }","doc":"What PostgreSQL calls fail with: connect, auth, failed, constraint (SQLSTATE\nclass 23), retry (a serialization failure or a deadlock: try again), noRows\n(one found nothing), closed.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":18},{"name":"Value","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Value { int(i64 v), float(f64 v), text(str v), bool(bool v), none }","doc":"A value for a $n in SQL: .int(36), .float(1.5), .text(\"ada\"),\n.bool(true) or .none for NULL.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":22},{"name":"Conn","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Conn { }","doc":"One connection to a server. It is used by one task at a time; a Pool\nshares connections between tasks and threads.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":95},{"name":"Conn.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Conn.close()","doc":"Logs out and closes the connection.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":439},{"name":"Conn.query","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Rows Conn.query(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs one statement with values for its $1, $2, ...","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":578},{"name":"Conn.exec","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Conn.exec(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs a statement: how many rows it changed.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":684},{"name":"Conn.execScript","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Conn.execScript(str sql)","doc":"Runs SQL with no values, which may hold several statements: a schema.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":690},{"name":"connect","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Conn connect(str address)","doc":"Connects to the server at address and logs in.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":237},{"name":"Rows","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Rows {\n    pub i64 affected\n}","doc":"The rows of a query, all read in. free gives them back.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":452},{"name":"Rows.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Rows.close()","doc":"Gives the rows' memory back, as sqlite's Rows.close does.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":463},{"name":"Rows.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Rows.free()","doc":"close, for scoped.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":470},{"name":"Rows.len","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Rows.len()","doc":"How many rows there are.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":473},{"name":"Rows.columns","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Rows.columns()","doc":"How many columns each row has.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":476},{"name":"Rows.name","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Rows.name(i64 col)","doc":"A column's name, from 0.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":479},{"name":"Rows.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Rows.next()","doc":"Moves to the next row: false when there are no more. The first call\nmoves to the first row.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":483},{"name":"Rows.text","kind":"method","sig":"pub str Rows.text(i64 col)","doc":"A column of the row as text: \"\" for NULL.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":494},{"name":"Rows.isNull","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Rows.isNull(i64 col)","doc":"Whether a column of the row is NULL.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":502},{"name":"Rows.int","kind":"method","sig":"pub i64 Rows.int(i64 col)","doc":"A column as a number: 0 for NULL or text that isn't one.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":505},{"name":"Rows.float","kind":"method","sig":"pub f64 Rows.float(i64 col)","doc":"A column as a float: 0 for NULL or text that isn't one.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":508},{"name":"Rows.bool","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Rows.bool(i64 col)","doc":"A column as a bool: true for t.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":511},{"name":"scan","kind":"func","sig":"pub !void scan[T](Rows *rows, T *out)","doc":"Fills a @json struct from the row, each field from the column of the same\nname. A NULL column leaves the field as it is.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":515},{"name":"Querier","kind":"interface","sig":"pub interface Querier {\n    !Rows query(str sql, Value[] args)\n}","doc":"What all and one run a query on: a Pool, a Conn or a Tx.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":534},{"name":"all","kind":"func","sig":"pub !List[T] all[T, D: Querier](D *db, str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Every row of a query as @json structs: pg.all[User](&db, \"SELECT ...\",\n[.int(18)]), on a Pool, a Conn or a Tx. The values' Strings are the\ncaller's to free.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":541},{"name":"one","kind":"func","sig":"pub !T one[T, D: Querier](D *db, str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"The first row of a query as a @json struct: pg.one[User](&db, \"SELECT\n... WHERE id = $1\", [.int(7)]). No rows fails with PgError.noRows.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":558},{"name":"Pool","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Pool { }","doc":"Connections to a server, shared by tasks and threads: each call takes\none, and gives it back when it is done.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":713},{"name":"Pool.query","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Rows Pool.query(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs a statement on a connection from the pool, with values for its $1, $2,\n...","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":776},{"name":"Pool.exec","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Pool.exec(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs a statement: how many rows it changed.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":787},{"name":"Pool.execScript","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Pool.execScript(str sql)","doc":"Runs SQL with no values, which may hold several statements.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":793},{"name":"Pool.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Pool.close()","doc":"Closes every connection. Calls that are running keep theirs until they\nend.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":804},{"name":"Pool.begin","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Tx Pool.begin()","doc":"Starts a transaction. Call commit or rollback at the end; a Tx that is\nfreed without either rolls back.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":828},{"name":"pool","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Pool pool(str address, i64 max = 10)","doc":"A pool for the server at address, with at most max connections open.\nIt connects once to check the address and the login.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":719},{"name":"Tx","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Tx { }","doc":"A transaction: its statements run on one connection, and take effect\ntogether at commit.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":820},{"name":"Tx.query","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Rows Tx.query(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs a statement in the transaction.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":838},{"name":"Tx.exec","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Tx.exec(str sql, Value[] args = [])","doc":"Runs a statement in the transaction: how many rows it changed.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":841},{"name":"Tx.commit","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Tx.commit()","doc":"Makes the transaction's changes last, and gives the connection back.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":844},{"name":"Tx.rollback","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Tx.rollback()","doc":"Drops the transaction's changes, and gives the connection back.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":854},{"name":"Tx.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Tx.free()","doc":"Rolls back, unless commit or rollback was called: for scoped.","file":"pg/pg.iris","line":864}]},{"name":"redis","title":"Redis","group":"Databases","summary":"A Redis and Valkey client, with pipelines and pub/sub.","intro":"<h2 id=\"connecting\"><a href=\"#connecting\">Connecting</a></h2>\n<p><code>redis.connect</code> gives a <code>Client</code> for a server's address. It keeps up to a given number of connections open (10 unless given), and every task and thread of a program can share it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">import</span> redis\n\n!<span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">main</span>() {\n    db := <span class=\"k\">try</span> redis.<span class=\"f\">connect</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;redis://localhost:6379&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"k\">defer</span> db.<span class=\"f\">close</span>()\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The address is <code>redis://user:password@host:port/database</code>. <code>rediss://</code> uses TLS, and <code>?ca=file</code> trusts certificates beyond the system's.</p>\n<h2 id=\"keys-and-values\"><a href=\"#keys-and-values\">Keys and values</a></h2>\n<p><code>set</code> stores a value, with a time to live when <code>ttl</code> is given. <code>get</code> gives a value and whether the key is there:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">set</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;session:42&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;ada&quot;</span>, ttl: <span class=\"n\">30m</span>)\n\nname, found := <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;session:42&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">defer</span> name.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> found {\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;signed in as </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>name<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n}\n\nvisits := <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">incr</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;visits&quot;</span>)\nleft := <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">ttl</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;session:42&quot;</span>)\nremoved := <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">del</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;session:42&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;visits&quot;</span>])</code></pre>\n<p>Hashes, lists and sets have their own calls:</p>\n<ul><li><code>hset</code>, <code>hget</code> and <code>hgetAll</code> for hashes.</li><li><code>lpush</code>, <code>rpush</code> and <code>lrange</code> for lists.</li><li><code>sadd</code> and <code>smembers</code> for sets.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"any-command\"><a href=\"#any-command\">Any command</a></h2>\n<p><code>do</code> sends any command, given as a list of words, and gives the server's <code>Reply</code>. An error reply fails with <code>RedisError.server</code> and the server's message:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">scoped</span> redis.<span class=\"t\">Reply</span> r = <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">do</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;ZADD&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;scores&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;10&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;ada&quot;</span>])\n<span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.int<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> added&quot;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"k\">scoped</span> redis.<span class=\"t\">Reply</span> top = <span class=\"k\">try</span> db.<span class=\"f\">do</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;ZRANGE&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;scores&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;2&quot;</span>])\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> item <span class=\"k\">in</span> top.items {\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(item.text)\n}</code></pre>\n<p>A <code>Reply</code> holds <code>text</code> for strings, <code>int</code> for numbers and <code>items</code> for lists. <code>kind</code> says which it is, and <code>isNil()</code> says whether the key was missing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pipelines\"><a href=\"#pipelines\">Pipelines</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>Pipeline</code> sends several commands together and waits once for all their replies, which saves a round trip per command:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"k\">scoped</span> p := db.<span class=\"f\">pipeline</span>()\np.<span class=\"f\">add</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;SET&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;1&quot;</span>])\np.<span class=\"f\">add</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;INCR&quot;</span>, <span class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</span>])\np.<span class=\"f\">add</span>([<span class=\"s\">&quot;PING&quot;</span>])\n\nreplies := <span class=\"k\">try</span> p.<span class=\"f\">run</span>()\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> r <span class=\"k\">in</span> replies {\n    <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.kind<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.text<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\"> </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>r.int<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    r.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()\n}\nreplies.<span class=\"f\">free</span>()</code></pre>\n<p>An error reply in a pipeline is a <code>Reply</code> of kind <code>.error</code>, so the other replies still come back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"publish-and-subscribe\"><a href=\"#publish-and-subscribe\">Publish and subscribe</a></h2>\n<p><code>publish</code> sends a message on a channel. <code>redis.subscribe</code> opens a connection of its own that receives the messages of some channels. With <code>pattern: true</code> it takes channel patterns like <code>room.*</code>. <code>next</code> waits for the next message:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code><span class=\"t\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">listen</span>(<span class=\"t\">str</span> address) {\n    <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> sub := redis.<span class=\"f\">subscribe</span>(address, [<span class=\"s\">&quot;room.*&quot;</span>], pattern: <span class=\"k\">true</span>) <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;subscribe: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>e.message<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> {\n        <span class=\"k\">scoped</span> m := sub.<span class=\"f\">next</span>() <span class=\"k\">catch</span> e {\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span>\n        }\n        <span class=\"f\">print</span>(<span class=\"s\">&quot;</span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.channel<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">: </span><span class=\"e\">{</span>m.data<span class=\"e\">}</span><span class=\"s\">&quot;</span>)\n    }\n}</code></pre>","doc":"Redis (and Valkey), spoken over the network by Iris itself. A Client\nkeeps a few connections open and can be shared by tasks and threads; a\ncall waits like any other network call, so other tasks run meanwhile.\n\n    db := try redis.connect(\"redis://localhost:6379\")\n    defer db.close()\n    try db.set(\"greeting\", \"hello\", ttl: 1h)\n    value, found := try db.get(\"greeting\")\n\ndo sends any command: try db.do([\"ZADD\", \"scores\", \"10\", \"ada\"]).","decls":[{"name":"RedisError","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum RedisError { connect, server, closed, protocol }","doc":"What Redis calls fail with: connect, server (an error reply, with the\nserver's message), closed, protocol (the server sent something that isn't\nRESP).","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":17},{"name":"Kind","kind":"enum","sig":"pub enum Kind { text, error, int, bulk, array, nil, double, bool, map, set, push }","doc":"What kind of value a reply is.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":20},{"name":"Reply","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Reply {\n    pub Kind kind\n    pub String text\n    pub i64 int\n    pub f64 double\n    pub List[Reply] items\n}","doc":"A reply from the server: text for simple and bulk strings (and errors),\nint for integers and booleans (1 or 0), items for arrays, maps (keys and\nvalues one after the other) and sets.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":25},{"name":"Reply.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Reply.free()","doc":"Frees the reply's text and items.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":34},{"name":"Reply.isNil","kind":"method","sig":"pub bool Reply.isNil()","doc":"Whether the reply is nil: a key that isn't there.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":43},{"name":"Client","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Client { }","doc":"Connections to a server, shared by tasks and threads.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":253},{"name":"Client.do","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Reply Client.do(str[] args)","doc":"Sends any command: db.do([\"HSET\", \"user:1\", \"name\", \"Ada\"]). An error\nreply fails with RedisError.server and the server's message.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":284},{"name":"Client.get","kind":"method","sig":"pub !(String, bool) Client.get(str key)","doc":"The value of key, and whether there is one.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":300},{"name":"Client.set","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Client.set(str key, str value, Duration ttl = 0s)","doc":"Sets key to value; with a ttl, it goes away after that time.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":312},{"name":"Client.del","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Client.del(str[] keys)","doc":"Removes keys: how many were there.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":322},{"name":"Client.exists","kind":"method","sig":"pub !bool Client.exists(str key)","doc":"Whether key is there.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":333},{"name":"Client.incr","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Client.incr(str key)","doc":"Adds 1 to the number at key (0 when there is none): the new value.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":336},{"name":"Client.incrBy","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Client.incrBy(str key, i64 n)","doc":"Adds n to the number at key (0 when there is none): the new value.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":339},{"name":"Client.expire","kind":"method","sig":"pub !bool Client.expire(str key, Duration d)","doc":"Makes key go away after d: false when there is no such key.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":345},{"name":"Client.ttl","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Duration Client.ttl(str key)","doc":"How long until key goes away: -1s when it doesn't, -2s when there is no\nsuch key.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":352},{"name":"Client.hset","kind":"method","sig":"pub !void Client.hset(str key, str field, str value)","doc":"Sets field of the hash at key.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":358},{"name":"Client.hget","kind":"method","sig":"pub !(String, bool) Client.hget(str key, str field)","doc":"A field of the hash at key, and whether it is there.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":363},{"name":"Client.hgetAll","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Map[str, String] Client.hgetAll(str key)","doc":"Every field of a hash, with its value. The map keeps its own copies of\nthe names; the values are the caller's to free.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":376},{"name":"Client.lpush","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Client.lpush(str key, str value)","doc":"Adds value at the start of the list at key: the list's new length.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":391},{"name":"Client.rpush","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Client.rpush(str key, str value)","doc":"Adds value at the end of the list at key: the list's new length.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":394},{"name":"Client.lrange","kind":"method","sig":"pub !List[String] Client.lrange(str key, i64 start = 0, i64 stop = -1)","doc":"The items of a list from start to stop (-1 is the last).","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":397},{"name":"Client.sadd","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Client.sadd(str key, str member)","doc":"Adds member to the set at key: 1 when it is new, 0 when it was there.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":405},{"name":"Client.smembers","kind":"method","sig":"pub !List[String] Client.smembers(str key)","doc":"The members of the set at key, in no particular order.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":408},{"name":"Client.publish","kind":"method","sig":"pub !i64 Client.publish(str channel, str message)","doc":"Sends message to the subscribers of channel: how many got it.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":414},{"name":"Client.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Client.close()","doc":"Closes the connections. Calls that are running keep theirs until they end.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":428},{"name":"Client.pipeline","kind":"method","sig":"pub Pipeline Client.pipeline()","doc":"A Pipeline: commands added to it are sent together by run.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":443},{"name":"connect","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Client connect(str address, i64 max = 10)","doc":"A client for the server at address, with at most max connections open.\nIt connects once, to check the address and the password.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":259},{"name":"Pipeline","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Pipeline { }","doc":"Commands sent together and answered together: one round trip.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":436},{"name":"Pipeline.add","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Pipeline.add(str[] args)","doc":"Adds a command; nothing is sent until run.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":446},{"name":"Pipeline.run","kind":"method","sig":"pub !List[Reply] Pipeline.run()","doc":"Sends the commands and gives their replies in the same order. An error\nreply is a Reply of kind .error, so the others still come back.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":453},{"name":"Pipeline.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Pipeline.free()","doc":"Frees the commands not sent.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":478},{"name":"Message","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Message {\n    pub String channel\n    pub String data\n}","doc":"A message from a subscribed channel.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":485},{"name":"Message.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Message.free()","doc":"Frees the channel and the data.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":491},{"name":"Subscription","kind":"struct","sig":"pub struct Subscription { }","doc":"A connection of its own, subscribed to channels.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":497},{"name":"Subscription.next","kind":"method","sig":"pub !Message Subscription.next(Duration timeout = 0s)","doc":"Waits for the next message, for at most timeout when it isn't 0.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":534},{"name":"Subscription.close","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Subscription.close()","doc":"Unsubscribes, by closing the subscription's connection.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":557},{"name":"Subscription.free","kind":"method","sig":"pub void Subscription.free()","doc":"close, for scoped.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":562},{"name":"subscribe","kind":"func","sig":"pub !Subscription subscribe(str address, str[] channels, bool pattern = false)","doc":"Subscribes to channels; patterns like \"news.*\" are subscribed with\nPSUBSCRIBE when pattern is true.","file":"redis/redis.iris","line":503}]}]};
